      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>•• • • • II • •</head>
        <p>•• • • • II • •</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDA DECEMBER 18, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>emi,7l".-TM74S SHOPPING</head>
        <p>emi,7l".-TM74S SHOPPING</p>
        <p>SANTA CLAUS, JUNIOR, TOURS THE</p>
        <p>SHOPS.</p>
        <p>Finds Places Where All His Requirements Can Be Met.</p>
        <p>A GREAT SEASONABLE SHOW.</p>
        <p>"Make a tour of the shops," said the Editor to me, and forthwith I.set out on my mission. The festive season is nigh, and with its approach our thoughts are turned to the necessities necessary for keeping up the spirit of this, the happiest and jolliest holiday in the year. We must be merry in spite of the present horrors of war, and to those of us who have given of their own kith and kin on behalf of the common cause, the message has been given—" Make you merry on this festal day, for we are happy and joyous in the doing of our duty. Just a thought of us and we are content." So we ape going to celebrate Christ- mas in the good old English style, and hide our sorrows at least from the young folk, for, after all, this is their great and glorious time, isn't it? On looking around the shops of our town one is particularly struck by the splendid and artistic display of goods arranged for the pur- chaser, and one cannot help thinking that at this time of the year the wares.set before the visitor seem much more pleasing and tempting than those of former years. Christmas is a time for giving, and, consequently, present- buying is a part of the unwritten ritual that precedes the celebration proper. We are now given the opportunity of making our choice of gifts to friends and relatives, and, in consequence, I am at your disposal to help with suggestions which may prove service- able to you. Come, therefore, with me on the tour round the seats of merchandise—the places where you buy, and, buying, receive satisfaction.</p>
        <p>sex in going to these particular shops, so I waited outside. In the establishments of Messrs. Dutton's, Hodgkinson, W. T. Williams, Madame Lillie, Jessie Flewitt, Miss Law, and the Warrington Co-operative Society she felt at home, and I had to curtail her expenditure, or else there would have been nothing left in the exchequer! A new set of furs, a charming and chic hat, a leather handbag, gloves, hand- kerchiefs and scarves; truly did I think that my partner had relatives and friends in every town and village in the British Isles. One likes to look one's best at Christmas time. Another source of special attraction to my partner hereabouts I found in ray list, sa, armed with the purse and a resolute determina- tion, the good lady directed her search for suit- able gifts towards the establishment of Messrs. Pattison and Perkins and J. Hammond, whose addresses are 17, Sankey-street, and Market Gate respectively.</p>
        <p>"-I've been measured for a new costume," she •replied later, "and I purchased a showerproof which will be handy for both the winter and spring months, and oh! the prices were reason- able.</p>
        <p>"I purchased also several dainty blouses and a set of furs and—well just one or two other articles. You see, considering what I have purchased, it hasn't been such 'an expensive journey !"</p>
        <p>I remembered that a new suit and overcoat would not be out of ke4ing with the season's purchases, and I am sure many of our male readers will think likewise. If so, I cannot do better than recommend you to pay a visit to any of the shops advertised in this week's issue, viz., the Grand Clothing Hall, Kellett's, Craik's, Suits!" Ltd., or the Manchester Clothing Depot. A fine range of cloths, with perfect fit and satisfaction, is assured you, and there is only just time to be measured. You do want to look your best before the ladies, don't you?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Jewellery For Peace And War.</head>
        <p>Jewellery For Peace And War.</p>
        <p>Believe me, when upon inspecting the fine display at Messrs. Eustance's, Miller &amp; Co., and J. Finlayson's, even I in my staid reflec- tions was sorely aroused into purchasing, for there I saw before me such a medley of Yule- tide gifts, appropriate and useful, that I could not possibly resist the temptation. "Tommy" in the trenches asks for a watch, just the one suitable, not too expensive, yet withal reliable. What a splendid gift to any- one in either of the Services! Yes; it would be a wise choice, and it does not hit one's pocket too much, for basiness has been brisk in spite of the war, and is it not helping our industries? Yes, Mr. Shopman, it must be British; no more of German make. What's that? You do not now sell goods of foreign manufacture? Good for you, Britisher, that's the spirit! Anything else sir ?—a ring, pendant, cigarette ease, ornaments, &amp;c.? Here I hesitated, and he who hesitates is lost. Per- fectly satisfied, I left the respective shops; but my pocket was fuller than when I first went</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>Turkeys And Fish.</head>
        <p>Turkeys And Fish.</p>
        <p>"Meat and mirth never hindered any Man," and with the thought of these words I reMem- bered I had to pay a visit to the purveyors of the Chlistinas dinner.</p>
        <p>A plump turkey; I knew I could get one at F. and J. Riley's, in Sankey-street; or at Mr. T. Baldwin's, of Pa,dgate-lane (Who, . by-the- way, has also an establishment in , Penketh), and I was not ,mistaken. Dressed 4or undressed, just as „you please, that is what I was in- foimed. "Shall I sena-it down for you, sir?" " Thank you," I replied, courtesy and an obliging and business-like manner made me purchase something further. At Mr. Riley's shop can be seen the best of fish and game, and at Mr. T. Bald win's I saw also rounds of beef, wholesome and appetising, quite in keep- ing with the high-class reputations of these worthy establishments. For The Plum Puddings.</p>
        <p>Turkeys alone or pheasants do not constitute the Christmas dinner, so to the grocers and provision dealers I hied.</p>
        <p>_ This took some time, for we have many in this particular branch of business, as the fol- lowing list will show: Messrs. Milling's, A. Clowes, Peter Leigh (here I tarried some time, for I saw bottles filled with beverages for after dinner), the Warrington Co-operative Society, Price's Stores, and the new shop of Messrs. Burgons. Sultanas, raisins, muscatels, California plums, figs, oranges, hams, cheese, and other multitudinous things necessary fo- providinr, wholesome fare. After visitin.r 41 places.efelt the need of a _special</p>
        <p>requisites, but the difficulti_A, . Exceptional value at all thes II ni4 :S-,d. Clothes For BA ?" Drapers and milline 'W ..re I was in a quandary and had tr he services of one of the ladies on ou ,REsr There is always a natural shyness on ~t... -part of the masculine</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>x</p>
        <p>And Boots For Everyone.</p>
        <p>- - The new premises of Messrs. R. Roberts and Son, and the Public Benefit Co. were within easy reach, and there in the windows one was struck by the artistic display of ladies' and gentlemen's boots and shoes. These commo- dities at this particular time of the year are essential, for the weather at present is not of the best. All the best makes and types can be obtained here, quite in accordance with these respected firms' reputation. Many of us will be making merry at parties, whist drives and balls. Don't spoil the ship for a ha'porth of tar, for someone is sure to notice if you are wearing a decent pair of slippers, fancy or otherwise. You can purchase them at either of these two shops.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Bakers And Confectioners.</head>
        <p>Bakers And Confectioners.</p>
        <p>At this point of the tour I remembered that I had not paid a visit to the local 43akers and confectioners, -which would have created trouble at home if I had forgotten. Who does not like sweets an the dainty things of the dinner and tea table? Where can I get these delights? I know! Messrs. Carter and Sons, Ltd., and J. Toft and Son. Here dainty maids greeW one, and -on the shelves and in the shop winf.lows an assortment of cakes in many shapes and sizes 'met my view.</p>
        <p>A Christmas cake? Yes, I wanted more than one, for he out in France must be thought of.</p>
        <p>"Greetings froni home," that was the one of my choice, which, ready packed and ad- dressed for posting, saved me unnecessary trouble. Biscuits and chocolates, white and brown bread, all ordered, all of the best quality, and another load was off my mind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Lady Shopper.</head>
        <p>The Lady Shopper.</p>
        <p>_ Now it was the turn of my lady partner again, so I left her eventually for a little while, to see how things were standing finan- cially. She had, with persuasive powers, al- ready relieved me of a considerable amount, but• there was no loophole of escape. She cer- tainly had to visit several ladies' and chil- dren's outfitters' establishments. _</p>
        <p>"Is this all?" she saidTo—ine, as I handed ler handful of coins, "and I have to shop tcnot po. F. and W. Isherwood, Mrs. Walsh's, la:•"wBrown, Misses Woods and Corbett's ) enaps you had better go yourself." The thought was disturbing, so my hand again went into my capacious pocket. Later on, she came back smiling and contented, and with the look of gladness I knew that there would be ,no change. Oh, you ladies! "Did you see any- thing you particularly wanted?" I asked; and</p>
        <p>Continued on Page 3.</p>
        <p>_ 1E S'ri3iIBLISIETRID }1.11143F 231. CIENTUFtir. X</p>
        <p>Street,</p>
        <p>"eibk;</p>
        <p>ELEPHONE No. 386.</p>
        <p>-- ,„. .I •A'.4 • , ;4 "p:' / ' '''4l.lo - ' ; . •.#"' '' ' -4' '-'• ''' 'o:* ' • '. ' , ,e' .t, f _ ‘ ~ •14" •-• 4,, ~.. - .c. •• , . '';', \ , , ~ GOLDSMITHS , • 1 . . ... . SILVERSMITHS -..- ~,,,,: ikl, "` :447 ,43 6-- DIAIVIC)N D . .• ..- r 1 t __ _ "i'f, -+, ••:-; ••• 'lv ''' "'---.4. '•). •• - ~' ' ..b-- ' ' t's A '''''i, 6) 12: 0 '' - ' AFAY:721217, • , ..t.l.-s /7,,1, ,i! 1 , t • 4':l- N,-, '. :,.:"',,,,, , kiit7lo: '1',..",41, ..1: !,,4•4t, ' M E RC HAN TS .?.... ~-041:e44,-,.'•7::,,,r4A. • : :-.-_,—..W.. -r' . .. 'll .44 . 444 .&gt;.-.0.1 simarummismaniap..-- i</p>
        <p>!t:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>with the question was thrust into my hand the list of purchases. A white silk blouse, several pairs of hose, a blanket coat, articles of neckwear, trimmings, and several umbrellas was what I read; but I did not mind, for I knew that value and quality, combined with reliability, were prior factors witn these worthy houses. She had certainly • obtained worth for her money, so I. was content. One thing pleased me, and that was that she had not been too selfish with the gifts for the elder folk, for included in the list were children's wearing apparel of all descriptions. Many tiny tots will be pleased this Yuletide. The hours were passing rapidly,- yet what of them? —for isn't shopping different at Christmas time than any other. One supposes it is because of the forthcoming pleasure of gift giving which, gladdens one's heart. Gentlemen's Outfitters.</p>
        <p>Oftimes had I visited the premises of such well-known caterers for masculine necessities as C. Mounfield's, J. E. White's and J. Banks's, but, verily, I had never seen a better or more bright display in their windows and show- rooms than on the present occasion. It was whilst visiting these places that I gave my partner the' slip, which possibly was for the best, for had she seen the unlimitable supply of goods purchased I know there would have been "some" scene. A new cellular shirt, clean-looking, for summer or winter wear; an umbrella, smart of appearance, without a fancy price; a dozen or so of Irish linen collars (yes, the right size, Cecil, I had it in the diary), handkerchiefs, snowy white, of classic design and workmanship; several ties, Wolsey and Reger underwear, socks and mufflers, all of the best, yet distinctly reasonable and within the reach of all. Collar boxes and walking- sticks of unique design and pattern—truly would I have liked to purchase their whole stock. Hie you to these shops, dear reader; they are well worth a visit. Christmas Cards.</p>
        <p>." A merry Christmas to you, sir !" a wee, small voice cried at my side, and as I looked round I saw it was the boy from the stationer's, and his cheery festive greeting (which earned him monetary reward) reminded me that I had not called at the shop of Mr. Arthur Davidson's, of Bridge-street. On reaching my destination the first thing to catch my eye in a window splendidly and tastefully set out were fountain pens by many noted makers. What more suitable as a gift for either- lady or gentleman? An. appropriate and pleasing gift for " Tommy " or ." Jack." They will wel- come a present such as this; furthermore, one, maybe, would be the means of him writing home oftener. Possibly many of your friends have this useful gift and would rather prefer something else Ah.! there is a nice card ease, handy and compact, just the thing for a business man; a dressing-case, either lady's or gentleman's, of British workmanship. Too expensive? Not at all; that is if you buy at Davidson's, for he has a splendid variety to suit all pockets. "Yes; send them to that address, please," and away I went, well satis- fied with illy purchases.</p>
        <p>"Any cigarette cards, Mister?" a newsboy asked me. "Good heavens !" I had forgotten all about the Christmas cards. "Business as usual" I thought, and more revenue for the Post Office.</p>
        <p>I turned back and made ray selection, for it would be Accounted a crime if I did not pur- chase the usual messages of goodwill. That newsboy did me a good turn, so I fumbled in my pockets—now considerably lessened—for stray coppers, and thrust them into his hand. "Cigarette cards, sir, I said," retorted the boy. "It's Christmas, ray lad," I said; and he smiled and wished me the compliments of the season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>For Use In The Home.</head>
        <p>For Use In The Home.</p>
        <p>I thought I had finished my shopping ex- pedition, but, no, for I had not as yet visited such shops and showrooms as are to he found at J. S. Garland's„, Pendlebnry &amp; Co.'s, E. Coates', and Tanner. Bros.' I was beginning to be weary and fatigued, so I hired a taxi. (Did you notice it on the petty cash voucher, dear Editor?) It was whilst looking at the wares at the above establishments that I thought of home and of those friends perhaps to be married this festive season. A carpet sweeper, just the thing for mother; a tea ser- vice, yes; that's a splendid gift to send to Tor- quay as a wedding present; a brass curb, artistic and of neat design, a roll of linoleum, red and easy looking; all these household requisites I saw on_nay visit to these shops.</p>
        <p>Coal scuttles, carpets, easy chairs, sewing machines. and everything for the home was to be found there, and my advice to you is: "Get you to any of these noted house furnishers and ironmongers, and you are certain of obtaining best value for your money." -</p>
        <p>Coming out a one of these shops I espied an old journalistic friend, at present on a leading daily newspaper. "Hello! old man," he cried. "decent almanac your people have produCed this year. Say, it's a fine reproduction of the Old Fox."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Photographs.</head>
        <p>Photographs.</p>
        <p>Photographs. I had forgotten for the moment that I had to call on our old friends, Messrs. J. Birtles, Walter Burrell and Fred Arthur. A clean collar, wash up and tidy, and</p>
      </div>
