      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 18, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 18, 1915.</p>
        <p>Nips SHOPPING •</p>
        <p>strictly in accordance with the label</p>
        <p>Specially for the Children.</p>
        <p>Now I am to write a few lines for the chil- dren. "Hang up the Christmas stocking, be sure you don't forget," and with the remem- brance of these words I think of the days when I was a tiny tot, who once used to pretend I was asleep in the small hours of Christmas morn.</p>
        <p>Boys and girls, keep up the usual custom and hang up your stockings. What do you want? A railway engine with lines, a Meccano outfit,' an airgun, a clockwork steamboat? Well, we will see what we can do for you. No, I haven't' forgotten you, little girl! You want a, dressed dolly which goes to sleep, .a picture book, n perambulator, a fancy box of -fancy handker- chiefs. Perhaps you are not - quite decided. Here's a friendly hint for you children. Ask mother to take you to " Fairyland " at the Co- operative Society, Sankey-street, or just tarry with mother at Messrs. W. Hodgkinson's, Bridge-street, for there you will be able to see such an assortment of toys you never dreamt of: If you take mother or father they cannot re, sist filling your stockings from the goods set on view. "You live in the suburbs, do you, little one?" Then if it is too far to town ask your parents to take you to Mr. Knight's shop, London-road, Stockton Heath. Ile has a fine display of toys, &amp;c., and I know you can get what you-want there.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>SANTA CLAUS, JUNIOR, TOURS THE SHOPS.</head>
        <p>SANTA CLAUS, JUNIOR, TOURS THE SHOPS.</p>
        <p>CONTINUED FROM PAGE 2.</p>
        <p>I went to be snapped. in different positions and poses. Full length, or just head and shoulders, it makes no difference, you can have your own choice at any of these expert photographers. Large photos or small, miniatures or as Christ- mas' cards, your wishes are respected, and per- fect workmanship guaranteed. Why not send your photograph to those at the Front or have an enlargement of your own dear lad now serv-. ing his King and country. He deserves premier place on your walls this Christmastide, and really ought to be adorned with •mistletoe and holly. • Come father and mother, sister and brother, sweetheart or friend; I'm right, am I not? Then away with you to our worthy friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>And Perfumery.</head>
        <p>And Perfumery.</p>
        <p>At this stage several young ladies from the perfumery department of a local works passed me on their way home, and as I recognised one or two I remembered that I had as yet to visit the shop of Harold Smith, chemist, Bridge- street. So thereto I.was driven. At this time of the year, when parties are in full swing, the completing of a lady's toilet is essential. Per- fumes addi sense of freshness to a crowded room and. reminds one of summer days. Gentle- men, look you to the ladies! The gift of a bottle or casket of perfume is always acceptable to a lady friend, besides it shows a touch of refine- ment. You will not find the cheap or vulgar class of perfume at the above establishment, for high-class quality is the maxim of our worthy chemist, be either your purchase toilet soaps, drugs, electric torches or cameras. Ah ! talking of cameras. Here is a; gift for young and old alike, for winter or summer days, it makes no difference. The recipient cannot be otherwise than delighted with a gat of this description.. A camera helps to bring back memories of holidays in the country or by sea and mountain. Some people say it is an expen- sive hobby. Not at all; it depends upon your choice. Varied prices, according to circum- stances, it really is worth your consideration. Ask Mr. Smith, he will tell you how to go about it without entailing unnecessary trouble and expense.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>Inviting Cafes.</head>
        <p>The Inviting Cafes.</p>
        <p>Only a few more calls to make to complete my Christmas shopping. Well, it is interesting work and one mist not grumble, for at least he who gives freely receives abundantly. Being young, I mean to have a right royal time this Christmas. Already many invitations to parties, &amp;c., have been showered upon me. Dining in cafes I adore. Cafes, cosy and clean, such as those of Messrs. Atkinson's, Ltd., or the War- rington Cafes, Ltd. Oftimes have I whiled away many pleasant hours in these select and up-to-date establishments, where the fare pro- vided is ,excellent and of the best. You dear reader who maybe intends to have a Christmas party of your own and your abode is not large enough, just give a call at either of these two cafes. The catering is to be relied upon, for they have a staff first-rate in the culinary art. Why not, if you anticikate having a decent time, approach them, they are bound to give satisfaction. If your friends are not numerous, then try their noted confectionery. Their stan- dard of excellence is well known to all. He who likes alcoholic drinks cannot do better than patronise those bottled by the noted firm of B. Cunningham. Established a number of years, you are assured that the contents are strictly in accordance with the label.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Music's Charms.</head>
        <p>Music's Charms.</p>
        <p>Without music Christmas would be a sorry time. The old folk like to sing the carols of bygone days; it brings back memories of the happy days of the past, so 'ender in their. thoughts. Others like the lilting melodies associated with musical comedies, or selections from such famous masters as Handel, Mendelssohn, Tschaikowsky and Sullivan, and for them to be played properly the piano or organ must be in trim. Hadn't you better run over the keys be- fore your visitors arrive this Yuletide, and see that everything is in order? You may find some little defect, and if so, well, just give a call at Messrs. Dawson's, Sankey-street, whose staff are fully competent to put things right. You may want some fresh music, there always are new songs at this time of the year. You can obtain the latest and best at their music stores. Gladden the hearts of young and old alike by a visit to our friends' establishment.</p>
        <p>Ile who likes alcoholic drinks cannot do better that patronise those bottled by the noted firm of B. Cunningham. Established a number of years, you are assured that the contents are</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Spectacles Are Useful Presents.</head>
        <p>Spectacles Are Useful Presents.</p>
        <p>;3, permission of the proprietors of</p>
        <p>Punch.</p>
        <p>VISITOR: "How delighted you must have been when you heard your son had won the V.C. SCOTCH WIFE: "0 ay! I was pleased enough but I wasna surprised. He stood up to me once!"</p>
        <p>I have nearly finished my article, which is fortunate, for the Editor is waiting for copy. Some of you are gifted with the blessing of good sight, that is as the good GOd wishes. One must take care of one's sight. You, my friend, perhaps advanced in years, have come to that stage in life when other means have to be employed if one wants to mad the news of the day., We, all of us, are not endowed with good sight. In many eases it is through our own negligence, not so if we took the necessary pre- cautions at the proper time. If your sight is failing it behoves you to take steps to remedy the defect. Fortunately in our town we have many opticians, clever and accomplished.,</p>
        <p>Messrs. Johnston Watson, F. Neubert a.nd R Jackson Moss, all of whom give careful atten- tion and advice to sufferers. Those throbbing pains in the head and achings behind the eyes all tend towards failing eyesight. Go to these gentlemen for treatment, it is really worth it, for after all, if , you have to wear spectacles, it is better than partial blindness.</p>
      </div>
