        <p>• r</p>
        <p>4,</p>
        <p>t - ' N- .,</p>
        <p>BIRTHS, MARRIAGES, DEATHS.</p>
        <p>Marriage.</p>
        <p>PALMER-LLAKE.—On tlie 16th inst.. at St. James' Church, by special licence, by the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, Private H. J. Palmer, M.T.A.S.C., on of Mr. T. J. Palmer, of Shoreham-by-Sea, to Lillie Holt Lake, daughter of Mrs. P. H. Lake and the late Mr. P. H. Lake, of Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Deaths.</head>
        <p>Deaths.</p>
        <p>ANDREWS.—On December 10th, at 31, Alexandra- atreet, Warrington, John Andrews, aged 77 years. GRAHAM.—On December 17th, at 32, Priestley- street, David Graham, aged 74 years. LAWTON.—On December 9th, at 18, Orford Avenue, Sarah Lawton, aged 70 years. PRICE.—On December 13th, at 51, Allen-street, Mary Price, aged 73 years. QUINN.—On December 10th, at 24, Watkin-street, John Quinn, aged 32 years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Acknowledgment.</head>
        <p>Acknowledgment.</p>
        <p>IVIR. and MRS. A. TILLING and FAMILY desire to THANK all friends (too numerous to allow of individual acknowledgment) for their kind tokens and messages of sympathy, and for the floral tributes received, on the occasion of their sad bereavement.</p>
        <p>•-- WITNERALS BY J. MOUNFIELD &amp; SONS. -12 The Best in all Grades. Office: 15, Church- Argot Tel VI</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, DECEMBER 18, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>WHAT OF THE SHIRKERS?</head>
        <p>WHAT OF THE SHIRKERS?</p>
        <p>it is with pride that we record, in general terms, the result of the appeal that was made to the manhood of Warrington by means of the Derby scheme of recruiting. True, there are some reasons for disappointment—but there are spots On the sun.. Pending the full revelation by Mr. Asquith next week, we have been permitted to learn* the result, locally, in percentages—which is quite sufficient for our present purposes. Of all the men, married or single, in the borough who were eligible, eighty per cent. presented themselves for en- listment. For medical and other reasons, twenty per cent. of these men were rejected, leaving, a full 'sixty per cent. of potential effectives. Only twenty per cent. of our eligibles failed to offer themselves, and even of this twenty per cent, three-parts of them gave satisfactory reasons to the canvassers for their abstention. So that there remains</p>
        <p>a residue of only five per cent. of the whole who remain unaccounted. It is stated that most of these said they were waiting until they were fetched. Many a true werd is spoken in jest, and they may yet be fetched, especially if they happen to be unmarried men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RECRUITING GOES ON.</head>
        <p>RECRUITING GOES ON.</p>
        <p>With the completion of the task Lord Derby set himself and his civilian army of workers, the Military recruiting officers take up their work in earnest , once again. Anyone who wishes to enlist now, apart altogether from the group system, may do so. Recruiting is open for cavalry, horse artillery, and field artillery, and, as we are all intent on win- ning the war in the shortest possible time, it is desirable that those who wish to do their bit should begin at once. There arc two points that should be borne in mind by those who are thinking of enlisting during the com- ing week. The first is that they may do so in the full assurance that they will be allowed to spend the holidays with their friends; the second is that they should not present them- selves at the last moment on Thursday even- ing, because, after the arduous time they have had, the recruiting staff are anxious to get away to spend their holidays among their friends. The recruiting office at the Police Station will be closed from Thursday evening, December 23rd, 'until 9 o'clock on the follow- ing Tuesday, December 28th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>AFTER-THE-WAR PROBLEMS.</head>
        <p>THE AFTER-THE-WAR PROBLEMS.</p>
        <p>An extremely interesting and important debate took place in the House of .Lords on Tuesday upon the problems Of demobilisation and of employment after the war. The debate was initiated by Lord Parker, and Lord Crewe expressed a hope that something would/ be done soon to carry out his suggestion for the appointment of a body devoted to the exam- ination of the question of Imperial reorganisa- tion as a whole, working largely through sub- committees, with the ultimate view of the conduct of many of the necessary 'operations through local bodies all over the country. Lord Haldane said that he would like to see a small:committee, the best that could be got together, set to work to consider the pro- blems that would be on us when peace came, and to produce something that would be of advantage to the departments of State, muni- cipalities, and employers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 18, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 18, 1915.</p>
        <p>BELGIANS IN BELGIUM.</p>
        <p>Reasons Why We Should Be Liberal Towards Them.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln connection with the Envelope Collection which Warrington and the sur- rounding districts are invited by the Mayor to make on Christmas Day on behalf of those who are destitute in Belgium, there are a few facts which require restatement. Events have crowded so rapidly upon the world's thought since the .commencement of the war that we are. in danger of forgetting what occurred at the beginning. These facts may be giouped under the following heads:— First—Belgium had no quarrel with Ger- many, and if she had allowed free passage to the Germans she would probably have saved much of the horrors which she has since endured. Instead of taking the easy and safe .path, she threw herself into the gap, and by</p>
        <p>holding back the Germans for ten days saved Paris, and probably Prance. Second.—Belgium of all the countries has suffered most in the destruction of her indus- tries, homes and belongings. Third.—Proportionately Belgium has suffered in the loss of life and the disablement of her son's far more• than England. Fourth.—Belgium has in the trenches, risk- ing their lives, a much larger proportion of her free men than has England. Fifth.—A special obligation rests upon Eng- land to succour Belgium because of her treaty obligations. We view with contempt the vio- lation by Germany of her solemn promise. Let us not neglect those who trusted, our word and merit a like shame.</p>
        <p>Sixth.—Apart from treaty rights the human need cries out to us. Those who may starve if we withhold our help, those who are desolate and bereaved, appeal to our sympathy ,upon the day of the birth of Him who multiplied the bread for the hungry, fed the world with the Bread of Life, and brightened it with the Light of Love. Seventh.—The relief work is admirably and economically organised, and on. the authority of the Relief Commission the Germans have</p>
        <p>honourably respected the supplies and facili- tated the distribution to ,those for whom it was intended. - I have never entertained the idea that the Belgian people were angels. They have their proportion of awkward and selfish people. They are more ready to quarrel than our own placid race. I have been brought into as close con- tact with the Belgian life as perhaps anyone in Warrington, and formed a high opinion of these people, and, while not blind to their _</p>
        <p>faults, have admired many of their national traits. But even if the Belgians who have come amongst us had turned °tit badly, which, with a very few exception S they have not, it should not make us withhold our sympathy for those in Belgium, who are still under the iron heel of their callous ctnquerors. CHARLES E.-PARKER. December 15th, 1915. ,</p>
        <p>We are informed by Mr. A. Tilling (joint secretary along with Mr. Parker of the Christmas Day collection) that over 50 Christian organisations in the town and dis- trict have consented to co-operate in the work. This augurs well for the success of the effort in this neighbourhood.—The Editor]. DONATIONS ALREADY TO HAND.</p>
        <p>His Worship the Mayor has received a cheque of £25 from Mr. C. E. Parker, and one of £1 7s. 4d. towards the effort that is being made in aid of the relief of the Belgians in Belgium.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>OUR NOBLE DEAD</head>
        <p>OUR NOBLE DEAD</p>
        <p>Memorial Service At The Parish Church.</p>
        <p>COMFORT FOR THOSE WHO</p>
        <p>MOURN.</p>
        <p>"Give eternal rest unto them, 0 Lord !" the English interpretation of the initial words of the introit which commences "Requiem reternam dona eis, Domine!" was the keynote of the opening of the memorial service at the Parish Church last night, but " Alleluia!" was its closing eharaoteristc. The service was on behalf of the 300 local men who have been killed in action and drowned as a result of the war. Warrington has given freely of its man- hood, and though hearts are sorrowful, there :s deep down in each of those who remain behind a joy brought about by the knowledge that they who have made the supreme sacrifice have earned and gained their reward. Th 4 service opened with that fine hymn which commences:—</p>
        <p>"Now the labourer's task is o'er; Now the battle day is past, ' Now upon the farther shore Lands the voyager at last," Father, in Thy gracious keeping Leave we now Thy servants sleeping." which was sympathetically sung by the large congregation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>De Profundis.</head>
        <p>De Profundis.</p>
        <p>The first Psalm chanted, is one of the most impressive .in the Book of Common Prayer, "Out of the deep have I called upon- Thee, 0 Lord; Lord hear my voice." The antiphons were "I am the resurrection and the life"; "I know that My --Redeemer liveth "; "We brought nothing into this world; and it is certain we can 'carry nothing out."</p>
        <p>The other Psalm was "The Lord is my Shepherd." The lesson contained an especially comforting word to sufferers. It was taken from,St.-John',. Gospel, the 14th chapter, the first verse of which is "Let not your heart be troubled; ye believe in God, believe also in Me."</p>
        <p>Previous to the reading of the names of men who had fallen, the choir chanted the Con- takion of the Departed: "Give rest, 0 Christ, to Thy servants with Thy saints; where sorrow and nain are no more; neither sighing, but life everlasting." _ _</p>
        <p>The long list was read in awful silence At its conclusion several prayers from the 'Burial Office were read. Then followed the Lord's Prayer and the briefest of prayers.." Lord, all pitying Jesus blest, grant them Thine eternal rest." During the, service Mr. F. M. Darby (the organists played the Dead March in "Saul."</p>
        <p>The chOir 'rendered the anthem "Blest are the Departed." Then came the triumphant and final note:—</p>
        <p>"Jesus lives! no longer now qui thy terrors, death appal us; Jesus lives! by this we kpow. Thou, 0 grave, eangt not enthral us.</p>
        <p>Alleluia Y'</p>
        <p>The prayers were said by the Rey. T. Vickers, and the lesson read by the Rev. S. G. Stanton. The Rector of Warrington (the Rev. Canon Willis) pronounced the blessing. In the congregation were the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Peter. Peacock), and a detachment of the Boys' Brigade, under Major L. Brookes.</p>
      </div>
