      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>frIIE NEW BANK.</head>
        <p>frIIE NEW BANK.</p>
        <p>On' Monday the Warrington' Branch of the London City and Midland .Bank, , Limited, which was opened in Sankey4street in Febru- ary, 1910, will be transferred to a handsome new building which has been specially built for the Bank on the east side of Bridge-street, near the Market Gate end. Here it replaces some old property, and, besides giving to its owners a commodious, convenient and up-to- date place of business, will add considerably to • the dignity and appearance of the centre of the business part of the town. The branch is under the management of Mr. Thomas Brookes Handley, who opened it in 1910. Mr. Handley, who belongs to the Hand- leys, of Runcorn, a "family well-known and highly respected in this neighbourhood, came from the old head office of the North and South Wales Bank, which had been amalga- mated with the London City and Midland Bank shortly before the Warrington Branch was opened. - The Bank, which has agents in practically every •town in the kingdom, has for its chair- man and Managing director. Sir Edward H. Hoiden, Bart., who was on the Commission for negotiating the Anglo-French loan in America. Some of the leading figures of the Bank are :—Authorised capital, £28,200,000; subscribed capital, £22,947,804; paid.up capital, £4,7•80,792 10g.;.'reserve fund, £4,000,000.l</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>NEW BUILDING.</head>
        <p>THE NEW BUILDING.</p>
        <p>The front of the new building is of Portland stone of Italian design, with large centre archway, closed with wrought-iron gates. A feature of the front is the carved spandrils over the archway, by Mr. Forsyth, of London. The interior is panelled with American wal- nut of carefully-selected figure, and above are fibrous plaster decorations by Messrs. John Tanner and Co., of Liverpool. It is lighted by a square-domed skylight, underneath an upper skylight, and between the two lights there is a coil of hot-water pipes to prevent down draught, and to assist ventilation. The Bank fittings are all of dull-polished American walnut, of beautiful figure, and the floor of the public space is laid with Cararra and Tends marble, the latter being executed by Messrs. John Stubbs and Sons, of Liver- pool. The whole of the framing and fittings were carried out by Mr. James Parkinson, of Liverpool. In the basement there are very large, strong rooms, which are quite bomb-proof, and vari- ous stores and lavatories.</p>
        <p>The general contractor for the building was Mr. Peter Edwards, of Chester. The plumbing and painting has been done by Messrs. S. and F. Acton, Warrington; and the electric fitting work by Mr. Harry Bibby, Warrington. The architect was Mr. T. B. Whinny, of London.</p>
        <p>Stories By Well-known Authors. Next Saturday's "Examiner "—which will be published on Friday—will contain, besides the full news for the week, the following stories by well-known writers:— " His Christmas Sweetheart," by Barry Pain; "A Christmas Wooing," by Helen Wallace; and</p>
        <p>'A Sprig of Holly," by L. G. Moberley. ''rho Examiner" will, therefore, be of distinct value for Christmas Day reading, apart from its ordinary news features.</p>
        <p>(3IIIISTAIAS HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>" Examiner " Will Be Published On Friday Morning.</p>
        <p>Next Saturday will be Christmas Day, and " The ,Examiner " will be .ptallisitti cm .Irriflay. The paper will be on sale on Friday at the ordinary times am! places. Newsagents will be served at the same .hour as _ _</p>
        <p>Will advertisers and correspondents send in their communications as early as possible next week?</p>
        <p>Special Christmas • Markets. Patrons of our markets will note that in consequence of ChriStmas and New Year's Days occurring on 'Saturdays, special markets are to be held on the following dates; Fray, Decem- ber 24th; Friday, December 31st.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Christmas Day Picture Shows.</head>
        <p>Christmas Day Picture Shows.</p>
        <p>At a recent meeting of the Watch Committee, cinema licences were granted to local picture house proprietors for a performance on Christ- mas Day, between the hours of six and ten in the evening: The programmes must be, sub- mitted to the Chief Constable for his approval.</p>
        <p>Priory School: Music Successes. The following pupils from the above school have been successful in passing the R.A.M. and R.C.M. Associated Board examination recently held in Manchester:—Marie Byerley, lower division; Gwendoline Bennett, elementary; Vera Nicholls, elementary; Kathleen .Byerley, primary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Chemistry Student's Success.</head>
        <p>Chemistry Student's Success.</p>
        <p>Thomas Malkin, a student of the Warrington Technical Institute, obtained the highest num- ber of marks in the lower examination of the Board of Education in theoretical inorganic chemistry, held in May last. For this distinc- tion he was awarded as a prize a chemical laboratory, the donors being Messrs. James Woolley, Sons and Co., Ltd., of Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Warrington Musical Society.</head>
        <p>Warrington Musical Society.</p>
        <p>The Musical Society, as. will be noted from the advertisement columns, are due to render "The Messiah" on Thursday evening next in the Parish Church, and as it is in aid of War Funds it is to be hoped that a good attendance is forthcoming.</p>
        <p>The seats are free, but a collection is to be taken.</p>
        <p>Warrington music-lovers, make a note of the date!</p>
        <p>Gifts for Secondary School "Old Boys." A collection recently taken amongst the students and staff of the Secondary:- School, and amongst a number of former students, for the purpose of supplying a Christmas gift to each "Old Boy" who is now serving in His Majesty's Forces. About 130 "Old Boys" so serving in practically every sphere of the war received well-selected parcels as the result of the liberal response that was made. Many ex- pressions of thanks for the ,seasonable gifts have been received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Two Motor Accidents.</head>
        <p>Two Motor Accidents.</p>
        <p>On Thursday night the motor w ail for Lymm was delayed owing to an accident in Knutsford-road. The driver, Mr. Patrick Maginn, in attempting to avoid a woman named Nellie Welsby, 159, Knutsford-road,. caused the car to swerve on the footpath, damaging the wheels. The woman was knocked down, but her injuries were not of a serious" nature. Another accident occurred near the Central Station, where a boy- named Frank Peacock, of, 4, Collin-street, was knocked down by a motor, car. When conveyed home in the car it was found by Dr. Martin the boy was suffering from slight concussion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Death of Mr. John Andrews.</head>
        <p>Death of Mr. John Andrews.</p>
        <p>We regret to record the death of Mr. John Andrews, who passed away at 31, Alexandra- street, on December 10th. Mr. Andrews had enjoyed the esteem of a wide circle of friends, and his loss will bring regret to many. He had completed his 76th year. The funeral took place on Monday at the Hill Cliffe burial ground. the Rev. G. Curtis conduct- ing the service. Floral tributes were received from " Jennie and iMary," "John and Maggie," "Ernest and Daisy," "Sarah and Alfred 'S (nephew), Dr. and Mrs. Bennett, Mr. and Mrs. Daintith, The employes of J. Geddes and ,Son. "Will and Emmie," "John and Dell," "Katie."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Wounded Soldiers Entertain Poor Children.</head>
        <p>Wounded Soldiers Entertain Poor Children.</p>
        <p>On Thursday twelve of the patients and five of the nursing staff from the Whitecross Hos- pital, gave a capital entertainment to the chil- dren of the Padgate Cottage Homes, at the request of Miss Broadbent, chairman of the committee. Corporal Guy, of New Zealand, arranged the programme, which was greatly enjoyed by the children and officers. Mrs. Lawrence, the matron, proposed a vote of thanks to the performers. Mr. and Mrs. Loxham, master and matron of the Workhouse, were present, and the whole party were conveyed in a motor char-a-bane, by the kindness 9f Messrs. Peter Walker. Corporal Molyneux, at present a patient in Raddon Court Red Cross Hospital, and formerly a master at the Padgate Schools, played all the accompaniments. , The enter- tainment was a great success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Carol Services.</head>
        <p>Carol Services.</p>
        <p>To-Morrow a carol service will be held at ,Golborne-street Baptist Church, the preacher for the day being the Pastor, the Rev. IL Ogden. In the evening selections from "The Messiah" will be rendered by Miss Olive Dale, Miss Maud Jones and Mr. A. Pearce. Old scholars -will be particularly welcome. At the Primitive Methodist Church, Legh- streets also, is to be given a special musical carol service, when the soloists due to appear are Mrs. C. Worthington, Mrs. A. X. Ilbyle and Mr. J. Dakin. A violin solo is to be ,ren- dered by Mr. H. Woodland, and an anthem entitled "Sweetly through the night." The preacher is the Rev. T. A. Young, of Widnes.</p>
        <p>- A. R. Gaul's cantata "'The Prince of Peace' is to be rendered at the Wycliffe Congregational Church, Bewsey-street, in another. Christmas choral service, the collection being in aid of the Choir Fund. The Rev. C. P. Pitt is to deliver the sermon. ' •</p>
        <p>• Selections of "The. Messiah" will also be.ren- dered at the evening services at the Bewsey- road. andAßold-street Wesleyan' Churches. The: soloists at the latter place of worship will be Mrs. A. Booth, Miss E. Taylor, Miss Brongh, Miss Blackburne, Miss. Whitmore, Miss Ker- shaw, Mr. J. Clayton, Mr. G. Wilde, Mr. B. H. Pearce, Mr. W. Warburton. Organist, Mr. Frank Hickman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>TOT %I, ABSTINENCE BIRTHPLACE.</head>
        <p>TOT %I, ABSTINENCE BIRTHPLACE.</p>
        <p>Narrow Escape Of An Historic Building</p>
        <p>At -Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>The Independent Methodist Sunday School at Stockton Heath, Warrington, better known as the old Providence Chapel, had a narrow escape from destruction by fire on Sunday afternoon. The outbreak was discovered after the afternoon session, and was extinguished by a few of the members who were recalled to the building. The school was the birthplace of ,the temper- ance movement, the first Total Abstinence Society having been formed within its walls in April, 1830. A memorial tablet recording this fact was unfortunately destroyed by the fire. The building contains the old pulpit from which Robert Moffatt and. many other notable preachers have.spnken.</p>
        <p>The Infirmary. The Matron of the Infirmary begs. to acknow- ledge with grateful thanks the receipt of seven- teen articles of clothing from the Warrington Needlework Guild.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>"Sacrifice and Service."</head>
        <p>"Sacrifice and Service."</p>
        <p>Mr. David P. Boyd, of Manchester, will speak on " Sa.crifice and Service," at the Friends' Meeting House to-morrow (Sunday) evening at 6.30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>First-Class Marine Engineer.</head>
        <p>First-Class Marine Engineer.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Harding, eldest so-ii of Mr. and Mrs. Harding, 21, St. Mary-street, Latchford, has been successful in passing the Board of Trade examination for marine engineers, and has been awarded his first-class certificate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Workhouse Master Attested.</head>
        <p>Workhouse Master Attested.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Board of Guardians on. Wednesday, it was reported that Mr. W. Lox- ham, the Workhouse master, had been given per- mission to attest under Lord Derby's scheme, and that he had been informed that the matron's position would be fully secured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Gasworkers' Union.</head>
        <p>Gasworkers' Union.</p>
        <p>It was announced at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council that Mr. Charles Dukes, the secretary, had been appointed organiser for the Lancashire division' of the Gas Workers and General Labouyers Union. The Council passed a resolution of con- gratulation to the secretary on his new appointment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Birthrate Decreasing.</head>
        <p>Birthrate Decreasing.</p>
        <p>The report on the health of Warrington for the week ended December 11th, shows that the number of births in the borough was 26, as com- pared with 31 for the same period last year, whereas the deaths was 29, which is the same figure for the corresponding period of last year. Of the 29 deaths, six were children under one year of age, and three between the ages of one and five.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Compulsory Thrift.</head>
        <p>Compulsory Thrift.</p>
        <p>At the meeting Of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council, held on Tuesday # the Executive Committee were instructed to lodge a protest against the acticn of Trade Union leaders and officials in attending the recent contrence held in London on the question of compilsary thrift without the officials first o!.taining an cx- pression of opinion on the subject from the general council or branches of thb various Trade Unions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Shorthand Successes.</head>
        <p>Shorthand Successes.</p>
        <p>• The Principal of Wood's College- has just re- ceived a notification that four pupils have been awarded special prizes for meritorious work in connection with the public examination held in shorthand a few weeks back :—First Prize (best- wdrked paper at 100 words per minute) Miss A. Chantler. Second Prize (80 w.p.m.): D. Dendy. , First Prize (best-worked Theory Paper) : Ff. Shirtaiffe. Second Prize (Element- ary Theory): N. Gorton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Local Musical Successes.</head>
        <p>Local Musical Successes.</p>
        <p>Miss Ellen Miller, Bronze Medallist, 12, Nora- street, has very 'successfully obtained the Diploma of Associate for Piano, having gained 96 marks out of a Possible 100, which entitles her to append the letters A.V.C.M. after her name, also to wear the College cap, gown and hood. Miss Miller has been coached throughout her tuition by Miss M. Jennings, of the Cause- way. Certificate of Bronze Medallist, Miss Alice IF urnival, 53, Bewsey-street, the maximum number of marks (100). Primary Grade (piano), Miss Doris Bennett, 25, Stafford-road, Stockton Heath, :4: marks, Ist Class with Honours, also pupils of Miss Jennings.</p>
        <p>, Motor Kitchens for Troops. , The National British Women's Temperance Association has provided three fully equipped motor kitchens for the use of the troops. These are 'working at Boulogne, Salisbury Plain and Whitchurch (Salop). The Association is also arranging to supply light coffee vans, .and to furnish recreation institutes in billets in France .and Flanders' under the auspices of the Wesleyan Army and Navy Board. Of the Warrington Branch of the • British Women's. Temperance .Association, Mrs. Lloyd- Jones is the president, Mrs. R. Henshall, vice- president, Miss Olive Bacon secretary, and Mrs. Thomas Albinson treasurer.</p>
        <p>The Making of Photographic Paper.</p>
        <p>Mr. Digweed,-of Liverpool,-WhO represents the fainous firm of Baryta Ltd., gave an interesting lecture to the Warrington Photographic Society at the close of the 1915 session. He demon- strated the care with which P.0.P., Bromide and Gaslight papers are made, and showed many specimens of defects and their evil influences on our prints. How the rags ,mukt be dusted free of every speck of metal, how the fibres must be 'felted together, how the paper must be coated with Baryta preparation (the four sable brushes in one machine cost £BO apiece), how all this manufacture has been entirely in the hands of the Germans up to the commencement of the war, and lastly the coating with emulsion —all these were - describeth with much detail. The coated paper must be dried at an even temperature or. the speed of printing is thrown away, and so a drying shed is needed .1,000 feet long, all in absolute darkness, and with dust- proof double doors, through which the men enter as into an air lock. With all this wonderful care it is a matter of congratulation that photo- graphic papers can be sold at such cheap rates. Mr. Price and Mr. Wilcockson expressed the thanks of the Society to 'Mr. Digweed..</p>
        <p>Gasworks Wages. It was alleged at the monthly Meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council that the Corporation employes at the Longford Gasiyorts and the. Electricity Works wAe receiving less than the recognised standard rate of wages. The Secretary (Mr. C. Dukes) re• ported that the negotiations with the Gas Com.; mittee relating to the 'rates of wages and over- time at the Longford Gasworks had not yet come to a, satisfactory conclusion. The correspondence extended. over a very considerable period, and from information it could be truthfully sail that the members of the Gas Committee could not be unconscious of the fact that there were in their employ men who were receiving less than the standard rate of wages. They did not seem to be .getting much " forrarder " in the matter.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. E. Plinston) said they ought /to press forward in the matter. They must not allow the Town Council to get the idea that the Trades Council reprgented no- body. They represented a bigger constituency than any of the members of the Town Council. If they could not get proper consideration they must take other steps. The Executive Committee were authorised to press forward their claims.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Death of Mrs. Dakin, of Orford.</head>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Dakin, of Orford.</p>
        <p>We regret to announce the death of Mrs. John Dakin, Orford Farm, who passed awaY\on December 7th at her residence. Mrs. Dakin belonged to a Cheshire family and spent her early years in the neighbourhood of Lymm. She was married to Mr. Dakin over 50 years ago, and during the past twenty years they have resided in Orford, where they have enjoyed the esteem and affection of their neighbours in a, marked degree. Alm. Dakin had 'entered her 71st year. The funeral took place on 'Saturday at the burial ground of the Higher-lane Baptist Chapel, Lymm, with which she was connected in her early years. A service was conducted in the home by the Rev. A. J. Morton. The service at Lymm was conducted by the Rev. A. J. Morton and the Rev. Joseph Howard. Among the mourners were: Mr. John Dakin (husband), Messrs. Joseph, John M., and William Dakin (sons), Masters Roland and Charles Dakin (grandsons), Mr. J. M. Davenport (brother-in-law), the Rev. Joseph Howard (cousin), Messrs. Alfred and Henry Robinson (nephews), Messrs. William Massey, William Smiith, Hugh Eden I and Arthur Tucker. Wreaths and floral tributes were received from "Her Husband," "Annie and Mary," "Joseph and Hefty," "John," "Wil- liam and Floss," "Robert and Alfred," "Charlie, Roy and. Mollie," "Her Loving Grandchildren" (Roland, Norman, Joseph and Kathleen), "Sister Lizzie and family," "All at The Walk, Partington," Mrs. Taylor and Mrs. Dunn, Mr. and Mrs. Alfred Robinson and family, "All at Sandy-lane," Messrs.. Hugh and George Eden, Messrs. Charles . and John Smith, Mrs. Dean and Son, me. ,and Mrs. Swinton and family, "Hilda," Miss WOlmer,, and Mr. and Mrs. Tomlinson. Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons carried out ,tb.o *funeral arrangement&amp;</p>
        <p>40.- \Oak Vigek `NOW VIM. VOW Villik Vilik WI</p>
        <p>" THE EXAMINER"</p>
        <p>ALMANAC for 1916 is given with to-day's issue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>fir Vela, Null" Naeski, Nasal% No" ',NA )allibe, ‘01b....06</head>
        <p>fir Vela, Null" Naeski, Nasal% No" ',NA )allibe, ‘01b....06</p>
        <p>Ambulance Men For Duty. Nine •more men of the local division of the St. ,john Ambulance Brigade went into LOrd Derby War Hospital at Winwick on Monday lot military home hospital service.</p>
        <p>Market Prices. Warrington, Wednesday,</p>
        <p>Wheat remains firm, a slight advance on good samples; 78. 10,1 d. to Bs. per bushel of 701 b. Oats remain firm, prices ruling 4s. to 4s. 3d. per 451 b. Straw firm at late prices, £3 ss. to £3 75., 6d. per ton. Wheat straw, 25., ed. per ton less. Clover hay in good demand, but supplies short. XX flour 435. per 2801 b. Eggs, 4 and 5 for is..</p>
        <p>Less Gas!</p>
        <p>Many people are complaining about the quality of their gas, but it appears that soon they will have to find fault with the quantity, for the Warrington Gas .Manager is adver- tising the fact that “Alwing to the difficulty in obtaining supplies of coal, it may be necessary to :educe the supply oS gas!' lie also states that coke will be supplied to regilar customers only.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Health Hint.</head>
        <p>A Health Hint.</p>
        <p>With the growing shortage of both men and horses, the Corporation are finding it more and more difficult to collect all the house refuse, and they are earnestly asking householders to assist them in their difficulty by burning such materials as fish heads, vegetable refuse, bad fruit, waste paper, &amp;c. By helping the cleans- ing department. of the town, householders will inciden,tly be lessening the chances of an out- break of some serious epidemic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Queen Alexandra's Birthday.</head>
        <p>Queen Alexandra's Birthday.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Thorp, 160, Manchester-roaa, War- rington, who has made a practice since 1902, of writing to Queen Alexandra on her birthday, has received the following reply from the Honourable Charlotte Knollys, Woman of the Bedchamber, on behalf of Her Majesty, to whom he sent his good wishes on the Ist inst.: "Marlborough HouSe, December 13th, 1913,— Miss Knollys is desired by Queen Alexandra to thank Mr. Thorp very much for his kind good wishes on Her Majesty's birthday."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Generous Teachers.</head>
        <p>Generous Teachers.</p>
        <p>The National Union of Teachers have set themselves the work of raising a fund of £lOO,OOO, which may be increased to 000,000 by the growing needs of the war, for which grants are to be made to teacher-warriors who suffer, or to their widows and families. It is gratify- ing to know that this worthy object is receiving the enthusiastic assistance of the local branch, whose members have already subscribed £396 10s. Of this amount £336 10s. has been sent to the headquarters, £6O having been retained for possible local necessities. As many teachers are already away and the subscribing strength thus reduced, it follows that the remaining members, and especially the ladies, have responded hand- somely to this call for help.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Death of Mr. John Bowers.</head>
        <p>Death of Mr. John Bowers.</p>
        <p>The death occurred on Thursday, December 10th, of Mr. John Bowers, 23, Longdin-street. Mr. Bowers, who had entered his 66th year, had been in uncertain health for some little time. Mr. Bowers was formerlyl in business in Priest- ley-street, and retired about three years ago. The funeral took place on Monday at the War- rington Cemetery. Among the mourners were Mr. W. Bowers, Mr. R. Bowers, Mr. A. Bowers (sons), Mrs. Sinclair (daughter), Miss J. Bowers, Mrs. Pritchard, Mr. A. Sinclair (son-in-law), Mrs. W. Bowers, Miss Taylor, Mrs. J. Acton. Mr. W. Pritchard, Mr. J. Acton, Miss N. Acton, Mr. W. Acton, Mrs. Bodle, Mr. Pa Mr. Warburton, Miss Ida Bowers, Mrs. Ander- son, Mr. Rickers, Mr. and Mrs. J. James. Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons carried out the funeral arrangements. Fat Stock Sale.</p>
        <p>At the annual Christmas fat stock sale, con- ducted by Messrs. J. Mairawaring and Co., this week, a hundred beasts sold at the average .of £27 ss. 10d. a head. Sheep and lambs made correspondingly satisfactory prices. The prize- winners included:— -</p>
        <p>Auctioneers' Silver Cups.—Bullock, Mr. Waiter Norcott; cow, Mr. Thomas Norcott. Class Prize (cow).—J.J. Newton's Exors. (The Bent). Class Prize (bulloc ).-11r. James Ditchfield (Penketh) ; purchased by Mr. T.. Baldwin at £35. Class Prize (heifer with more than four broad teeth).—Mr. William Norcott (Penketh). Class Prize (heifer with four broad teeth).— Mr. Robert Stone.</p>
        <p>Class Prize (heifer with not more than two broad teeth).—Mr. William Norcott (Penketh). Messrs. Gartons, Ltd., Prize (cow).—Mr. Thomas Norcott.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Carter and Son, Ltd.—Best beast (not a cow), Wafer Norcott. Mr. F. C. Dutton's Prize (second best pen of lambs).—Mr. Robert Stone. Messrs. -Boydell Bros.' Prize (runner-up of silver cup cow).—John R. Newton's Exors. Sons of Temperance Confer.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the Earlestown Grand Division of the Order of the Sons of Temperance was held on Saturday in the Brook- street Congregational Church Institute, St. Helens. ,The Warrington sub-division was represented by, Bros. R. Jones (Worthy Patri- arch) and H. Wardman (Worthy Scribe). The gathering was presided over by the Grand Worthy Patriarch (Bro. W. Burrows, of St. Helens), who was supported by the Most Worthy Scribe (Bro. Davies. of Manchester). After the ordinary business of the Order (which was of a purely routine character) was at an end, a special session of the delegates for the con- sideration of ~amendments to several of the present °rule s was presided over by Bro. Davies. Included among the various alterations was a uew- scale of contributions, which, after careful deliberation, was referred back to the Management Committee for further consideration. At the invitation of the St. Helens hrethren, the representatives, at an in- terval during business, partook of tea, after which a vote of thanks was accorded to the hosts and hostesses.</p>
        <p>Funeral of Mrs. Baker, of Lower Walton. The funeral of the late Mrs. Clara Baker, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. ' Alfred Tilling, Lower Walton, and widow of , the late S. J. Baker, took place at the Warrington Cemetery last Saturday.</p>
        <p>The deceased, who was 40 years of age, and leaves one daughter, contracted pneumonia just week -before her death. Further complica- tions developed rapidllr, and she passed away on Thursday morning, the 9th inst. Mrs. Baker is mourned by a large circle of relatives and friends. She was connected with the Working Men's Mission Chapel and School all her life, a member of the Women's Liberal Association, and auditor for the Women's Branch‘ of the Ancient Order of Foresters. The burial service was, taken by Mr. George Dale, and Mr. George Maybury also read the very impressive Foresters' burial service. The bearers were Messrs. H. Edwards, S. Milling- ton, E. Pownall and W. Worrall. Floral tributes were received from Father and Mother, "Ethel," "George and Lizzie and children," "Dick and Mary," "Will and Lizzie and family," "Alf and Polly," "Joe and Emily," "Harry and Eva," "Eddie and Eric," "Tom and Olive," "Sailor Brother Jack," "Soldier Nephew Ben," "JOO and Lucy," John an,i Clarrie Monks, Mr. and Mrs. Baker (Wolver- hampton), Mr. and Mrs. Baker (Shotton), "Albert and Alice," Mr. Hunter, 1!olly Gleave. Mr. and Mrs. Gleave and family, "Cousins at Bradford," Employes Tilling, Gray and Crown-street; Employes Tilling Gray and Co., Bewsey-street; C. Hassall and H. Gray, Working Men's Mission, Apcient Order of Foresters (Women's Branch). I. B. Tanner, Mr. and Mrs. Allanson and family, Mr. Banks end sons, Mrs. Bowers, "Belgian friends" (Gol- borne-street), Nurse Cliffe, Mr. and Mrs. Cooper, Mr. and Mrs. Hollowell, Mr. and Mrs. J. H. Jones, Mr. and Mrs. Pemberton, Mr. and Mrs. Millington, Mr. .inci Mrs. R. Monks, Mr. and Mrs. E. Pownall. Mr. and 'Mrs. A. Pownall, Mr. and Mrs. Sumner, Mr. and Mrs. West, Mr. and Mrs. Yates. The funeral arrangements were carried out by. Messrs. I. lifounfield -and Sow.'</p>
        <p>L</p>
        <p>4</p>
        <p>JaiL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>MAYOR AS HOST.</head>
        <p>MAYOR AS HOST.</p>
        <p>Successful Whist Drive And Dance At The Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>OVER 500 PRESENT.</p>
        <p>The annual whist drive and dance arranged by the Mayor, and Mayoress of Warrington (Alderman and Mrs. Peter Peacock), was held at the Parr Hall on Thursday evening, and proved to be a wonderful success The object of the gathering was to supplement the Tobacco Fund organised for the benefit of the troops at the Front, and to give a donation to the Working Men's Infirmary Committee. Whist was played from about eight Until eleven, and dancing followed until 2 a.m. The number of whist players exceeded 500, but even this large number was increased when the dancing com- menced. •</p>
        <p>WILL BE THE HAPPIEST MAN IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>-Addressing the players previous, to the com- mencement of the whist, The Mayor said that on behalf of the Mayoress. and himself, he wished to thank them for assembling in such large numbers. He thought that they would have to make the event an annual function. (Hear, hear.) Whilst they had had two during ,the last twelve months— and they had been supported well—he trusted that the next Would be held when peace had been made and the war At an end. By attend- ing they were supporting an institution that had been severely hit by the war. The Infirm- ary was suffering more than any other institu- tion in Warrington as a result of the war. They were also enabling him to send more tobacco to the boys who were fighting their battles at the Front. He knew perfectly well how charitable the people of Warrington were inclined. His thanks as Mayor were due to them in More than one sense. -He would like to thank the stewards who had come forward that evening to 'help in the whist drive. Perhaps the ladies and gentlemen present would suffer some incon- venience owing to the large numbers present, but bearing in mind the object of the event, he 'did not think they would grumble. Mr, and Mrs. Mitchell had had practically the whole of the arrangements in their hands, and Mrs. Mitchell 'had worked very hard with regard to the cater- ing. Messrs.. Peter Walker and Son had given him nearly all the decorations, and had sent their men to decorate the hall. His thanks were particularly due to them. He hoped again that the next time a similar function was held, it would be when peace had been made, and then he would be the happiest Man in Warrington. (Laughter, and applause.) • THE WHIST WINNERS.</p>
        <p>• It was originally intended to play 24 games of whist, but this programme, for various reasons, was curtailed, and only 19 were played. Four solid silver prizes were given for the ladies' 'prizes, and four for the gentlemen's, the win- ners being : , Ladies': 1, Mrs. Fred Short; 2, Mrs. Hassall. 'Hidden: 1, Miss Stringer; 2, Miss Marion Smethurst. Gent's: 1, Mr. Harvey Cook ;2, Lance-Corporal Langhara. Hidden: 1, Mr. Douglas Peacock; 2, Mr. George Marshall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>MAYOR'S WAR-TIME SERVICES.</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S WAR-TIME SERVICES.</p>
        <p>Later in the evening, the prizes were dis- tributed by the Mayoress, amidst loud applause, at the conclusion of which, , Mr. J. H. Smethurst said that they would be very much lacking in theiy duty if they did not propose a hearty vote of thanks to the Mayor ,and Mayoress for the kindness they had shown ,in arranging such a successful function. They all recognised the enormous amount of work ,that -they, had done. He regretted the absence ,of Alderman Button owing to illness, but they .had had an able substitute in Mr. George Walker. He moved that a vote of thanks -Should be. accorded the Mayor and Mayoress. ~(Applause.)</p>
        <p>_ _ , Seconding, Mr. G. Walker said ;that they all .knen, the aetivity that the Mayor had shown ,during a very trying time in the borough and they all knew how hard he worked for philanthropic movement:, (Applause.) , -</p>
        <p>The motion was carried with cheers for the _Mayor anitMayoresS: • - • Responding, the Mayor said that they very much appreciated the vote of thanks. Their -(the Mayor and Mayoress') time was always at the disposal of the town so long as they held their positions. Whilst the war was On, they •could not devote their services in a better man- ner than they had done that evening. Mrs. Fred Short, who won the ladies' first prize, hag decided to organise a raffle for her prize in aid '.of the Serbian 'Relief Fund. (Applause.) Whilst generally he had not accepted donations ,or goods, he had been compelled to accept a .cheque for £lO from Mr. Ernest Houghton, of Messrs. W. B. Houghton and Co., Ltd. (Ap. lause.) • The dancing was most enjoyable, an excellent selection having been made by the The chiefi M.C. for the evening was the Mayor, the whist M.C. was Mr. George Walker, -and the , M,C. for dancing was the 'Mayor, .-asSisted by Mr. S. H. Walker. The stewards were Messrs. George Bebbington, A. C. Binks, W. E. Clegg, R. Cook, J. T. Cooper, W. 'Kilduff, Harry Lawrence, F. Short, H. Smith, A. • Starkey, T. C. Wadsworth, S. H. Walker, A. H. Webb and J. A. Whittle. At the request of the Mayor, Mr. J. B. Starkey supplied the orchestra for dancing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>WAR FUNDS AUCTION.</head>
        <p>WAR FUNDS AUCTION.</p>
        <p>Result And Allocation Of The Proceeds. •</p>
        <p>'On Tuesday afternoon the final meeting of the General: Committee responsible. for the recent sale by auction on behalf of local War Funds was held in the Town Hall, under the presidency of the Mayor (Alderman ' Peter Peacock). The balance sheet was _presented by the Hon. -Treasurer (Mr. J. Fairhurst), and showed the gross receipts of the sale to be £l7B 44d. The expenses, printing, advertising, postages, &amp;c., were .227 2s. 5d., leaving a net balance to be divided of ~tl5l 15s. Hid.</p>
        <p>It was unanimously resolved that the proceeds :should be allocated as follows :—Lord Derby's Hospital, £10; Whitecross Hospital, £10; Bar- ' racks Hospital, £5; 'lnfirmary Special War Fund, £2O; Tobacco Fund; ..t.5; Oaklands, £l5; 'Thelwall, £l5; Raddon Court, £4O; Prisoners ,of War (balance), 15s. On the motion of Alderman Smethurst, sec- ,onded by Mr. C. E. Parker, all ~the officers were heartily. thanked for their services and congratu- lated on the result of the sale. Particular men- tion was made of the services of Mr.. Herbert .Johnson, who acted as hon. auctioneer. The cataloguing and selling of the goods.' was an .arduous task, and it was felt by all present that :special thanks were due to him, for on his :,shoulders had fallen the bulk of the work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Sale Of Work Brings In Over</p>
        <p>£lOO.</p>
        <p>The Wycliffe Congregational Church sale of work was held in the Wycliffe Memorial Hall on Thursday afternoon. There was no formal opening ceremony. Music, was supplied during the afternoon, whilst in the evening an enter- tainment was given. Amongst those who gave selections were :—Mrs. Haviour (violin solo), Mr. Eric Jeffery (recitations), whilst the choir :rendered several songs. The following were the stallholders : Mrs. Armitage's Stall.—Mrs. Armitage, Mrs. E. Garland and Miss Lee. - Ladies' Working party StAll.—Mrs. Walker, Mrs. Slack, Mrs. Winskill, Mrs. J. W. Jeffery, 'Mrs. S. W. Jeffery, Mrs. Lowe and Miss Ellison. Young Ladies, Stall.—Mrs. James, Mrs. Joseph He.wthorn, Mrs. Harold Hawthorn, • MiSs Lowe, Miss Warbreck, Miss A. Wallington. Miss F. Wallington, Miss. Womersley and Miss .0. Bateman. Crockery Stall.—Mrs. Pickton, Mrs. Knowles and Mrs. Alfred Podmore. Refreshment Stahl.—Mrs. Atkinson, 'Mrs. Edel- *ten, Mrs. Wats. Mrs. Monks, Mrs. Payton, Miss Atkinson, Miss Rathbone• and Miss Harrison. The proceeds of the sale, which are to be .devoted to the Maintenance Fund, amounted to the no sum of OW,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>80 PER CENT.</head>
        <p>80 PER CENT.</p>
        <p>Success Of Lord Derby's Recruiting Scheme.</p>
        <p>WHAT OF THE SHIRKERS?</p>
        <p>Armlets Arrive For Immediate</p>
        <p>Distribution.</p>
        <p>Lord Derby's recruiting scheme has been a huge success in Warrington, but in view of Mr. Asquith's postponed statement, we are not in a position to give details of the number of men who have attested, Or who have been rejected. The statement so far as Warrington is concerned was in the hands of the Central Authorities in London on Ttesday evening, and everything has been declared satisfactory. Fully 80 per cent. of the eligible unstarred men of Warrington have' presented themselves for either attestation under Lord Derby's scheme or direct enlistment, but of these 20 _per cent. have been rejected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>"WAITING TO BE FETCHED."</head>
        <p>"WAITING TO BE FETCHED."</p>
        <p>Of the men who have not presented them- selves, the majority have given what may be termed adequate reasons for not doing so, but about 25 per cent. of them have refused point blank to enlist, giving as their reason "that they are waiting to be fetched." Returns have been prepared of men of every age in the scheme, whether single or married, who have declined to attest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>ADVISORY COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>THE ADVISORY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>As we stated in a recent issue, a lqcal Tribunal has been formed to hear appeals fiom men who have been attested and wish, for domestic or business reasons, to .be exempt from military service. Before appeals go to the Tribunal they will first be investigated by an Advisory Committee, consisting of Messrs. C. E.</p>
        <p>Parker, Edwin Bradshaw, Councillor Locker, Mr. Ernest Sanders, Mr. IlerbOrt Hayes, and a representative of the War Office. It is hoped that two Labour representatives will be appointed. We understand that Captain Mathams haq been appointed representative of the War Office to act in conjunction with the Advisory Com- mittee, and appear before the local Tribunal on behalf of the War Office for this district. An appeal is made to men attested /in the later groups not to' appeal immediately, but to wait until the earlier classes have been called up.. Claims must be sent in not later than ten days after the issue of the proclam.eclon calling up the groups.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>DISTRIBUTION OF ARMLETS.</head>
        <p>DISTRIBUTION OF ARMLETS.</p>
        <p>The distribution of armlets to men who have been attested was commenced on Friday afteil- noon at the Drill Hall. The groups dealt with were Nos. 1 and 2. This (Saturday) afternoon the armlets will be distributed to the men of groups Nos. 3, 4 and 5. Further issues will be announced later. Each man is asked to take his white card with him when he goes for his arm- let. To quote the words of the poster ' " No White Card; No Armlet."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>LAST WEEK'S RUSH.</head>
        <p>LAST WEEK'S RUSH.</p>
        <p>The rush of recruits in the last few days of the week ended December 11th was not main- tained over the week-end, and so far as War- rington is concerned, the 24 hours' extension was hardly necessary.' As a matter of fact only about 30 men gave in their names on Sunday. The last man to attest presented himself at the offices of the Civilian Recruiting Committee in Horsemarket-street at midnight on Sunday. He was attested, and at the conclusion or gentlemen- present, including Alderman Ben- nett, Messrs. E. A. Sanders and H. Hayes (the hon. secretaries) and Mr. George H. Smith, sang the National Anthem, a very fitting close to a strenuous—and we hope successful—cam- paign to save the voluntary system and the honour of the manhood of England.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>LORD DERBY'S THANKS.</head>
        <p>LORD DERBY'S THANKS.</p>
        <p>Lord Derby has expressed, on behalf of him- self, the Parliamentary Recruiting 'Committee and the Joint Labour Committee, his apprecia- tion and thanks of the services that the Press have rendered to make his scheme a success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>THANKS FROM THE MAYOR.</head>
        <p>THANKS FROM THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>LORD DERBY'S RECRUITING CAMPAIGN</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—As Chairman of the Parliamentary Recruiting Committee for Warrington, may I crave the hospitality of your columns in order to thank all those who by their very willing voluntary work either as canvassers or in the clerical department have done so much to ensure that Warrington Should do its best? I am sorry that I am not able personally to thank each individual worker, but I am sure all will be glad to ,know. that their efforts have been very much appreciated. In conclusion May I also thank you, 'Sir, for the assistance you have so readily given in the columns of your paper? Yours faithfully, * PETER PEACOCK,</p>
        <p>Mayor, Mayor's Parlour, Town Hall, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>STILL NEUTRAL.</head>
        <p>STILL NEUTRAL.</p>
        <p>Trades Council And Appeal Tribunal. The question of whether the Warrington Trades. Council ought to apply for representa- tives on the newly,appointed Appeal Tribunal under Lord Derby's recruiting sheme, was dis- cussed at their, monthly meeting on Tuesday. , In reply • to a. letter of the. executive asking for 'information relative to the proceedings governing the • Tribunals, .tlve Town Clerk of Warrington , wrote Stating that the regulations had not yet been issued as to the procedure of the Tribunal, but no doubt that would be done shortly. The decisions of the Tribunal would be subject to an appeal by either the recruiting officer, the man concerned, or the employer if he should bring an appeal. The Secretary (Mr. C. Dukes) pointed out that the Appeal Tribunal had been formed of the following gentlemen: The Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), Deputy Mayer (Dr: Joseph).. Alderman Tinnion, Messrs. F. A. FrOst and J. J. Whitley. Mr.. W. Blackwood Proposed that the Council' should take .steps to obtain • representation on the Tribunal. Mr. F. Berry seconded, and said such a, course was necessary in the interests of many trade unionists, who ought to be safeguarded as far ,as possible. Mr. W. Harrison opposed the resolution, and said it was not worth while writing. in view of the fact that the Tribunal of five had already been appointed. The Chairman (Mr. E. Plinston) said he quite. agreed with the opinion expressed by Mr. Harrison. He .was practically certain that the Tra.des .Council would not get representation. They did .not take part in getting men to • enlist, and he ,did not think they were likely to take part, in getting exemptions • for those who had attested., Mr. T.'-lloward said they ought still to remain neutral as to' who 'went to France and who Stayed in England. On a vote being taken ten voted for the pro- posal and. ten against. The Chairman gave his casting vote against applying for representa- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>SCALDING FATALITY.</head>
        <p>SCALDING FATALITY.</p>
        <p>Child Falls Into Saucepan Of Boiling Pigs' Food.</p>
        <p>A verdict of "Accidental • death " was re- turned at an inquest held at the Police Station on Wednesday by Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, concerning the death of John Deakin, the two- year-and-ten-month-old son bf Isaiah and Esther Deakin, 331, Liverpool-road., who died in the Infirmary on Friday from severe scalds, sus- tained through falling into a large saucepan of boiling potato skins, Prepared for pigs' 'food. The mother left the child in the kitchen whilst she went into the Scullery for a bucket. The child must have knocked oft the lid of the pan -and fallen inSide it, .as ho was found in a sitting Lpoittion to the saucepan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 18, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 18, 1915.</p>
        <p>_I</p>
        <p>PRIVATE PERCIVAL.</p>
        <p>• •, • , . . . . • • . • • • :•Ki:•.•••••:•• • • ' • • ••• • • • ••::*?',;•••.1',1i::? • • • , .;.. • . . ' - •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>" TOMMY " AND XMAS.</head>
        <p>" TOMMY " AND XMAS.</p>
        <p>Curious Sentiments And Jampot Wisdom.</p>
        <p>AFTER LIGHTS-OUT.</p>
        <p>In the dormitory the bugle-call for "Lights out" had been complied with; at the risk of a reprimand from the Corporal, talking con- tinued. It ,struck me that readers of "The Examiner" -would be interested to hear " Tommy's " Views on the approaching Yule festival, so I determined to introduce the topic. It was Monday. During the last two nights most of us had been away pleasuring, only early that morning making the return to Barracks. "Well boys," said I, "I see all the shops in the City are getting ready for Christmas." " Jampot " is a talkative, pleasant little leather-stitcher with a wooden leg and a serious countenance. He it was who replied—from his " bed " across the dark room,: "Is that War- rington who's been to London?" "Aye."</p>
        <p>"Oh! I've been there as well." Suddenly he sat up. "I'll tell you what it is; people. I've been amongst some real Cockneys this week-end, and they're the finest chaps that is—real gentlemen. You know how people insult them? Well, it's not true; they're real gentlemen." George from Devon: "Oh, they're all right till they take a hatred, and they're not worth speakin' of. Shure they're not. Jampot: "Well, I'll tell you this: The wiDrst people for friends is the Welsh." Rochdale (doubtfully): "Oh. I don't know." Llanelly (in loud indignation): "Well then they're not." (Disgustedly.) "And look here, Jampot you; if you can't speak without insult- ing the Welsh for Heaven's sake shut up." (Turns over and goes to sleep.) Rochdale (innocently;): "What nationality are you, Jampot?"</p>
        <p>Jampot:" Well ". . . (Hesitates) "Well " (with determination) "I live in London." A Voice: "Come On, Jampot; own tip. You're a Cockney!" - Jampot (loudly): "All right then, I am a Cockney."</p>
        <p>The Voice: "Yes. And a bonny [decorated] specimen you are, too." Bolton (in tones of conciliation—he wants to get to sleep): "Still, I don't blame anyone stick- ing up for his own clan." George from Devon: "Shure !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>George From Devon.</head>
        <p>George From Devon.</p>
        <p>George from Devon is a popular man of medium build. His face is furrowed; his eyes bright; his breath whiskified. This last in- genious remark he follows up with a piece of intelligence:— "Shure boys, I'm going across the water next week."</p>
        <p>Tick from New Cross: "Get away! You're drunk."</p>
        <p>George: "Shure I'm not drunk either. I tell you I'm going with -- Company to the Med— Mederranean." (With an effort.) Rochtlale: "Don't you want to?" George: "Shure no. I want to go to Jeroosalem." Chorus of Voices: "Jerusalem! ! ? ! ! " George: "Shure yes. Jeroosalem the olden city." ,</p>
        <p>Smiles and silence. George (continuing) : "Where the Master ;rod. And He was a corpenter." "I'm a corperiter," announced George.</p>
        <p>" Wunce I was in the choir too at our church. And I tell you this: There isn't a finer choir in England—well, I tell you, the best picnic I ever been to we wus singing all the way home on the'waggonette: And the driver he said 'Ol all he heard. . .." Silenced by a pillow. ' Lighting the Fire.</p>
        <p>Tich from NeW Cross (springing out of bed noisily at the far end of the room): "Well I'm going to light this blessed fire for a change. There's a chilly air about this end of the [decorated] atmosphere. (Proceeds to fill bucket with coal, rattling the shovel a great deal and bringing upon himself from all parts of the room a chorus of hearty maledic- tions quite in excess of the blessing to be earned by his lighting the fire. Then there is quiet- ness.</p>
        <p>Twenty minutes later:— Leicester (banging a boot heavily on the floor, in great excitement): "I say boys, I've got another mouse!" But one is drowsy—asleep. Anyway, that is what " Tommy " thought of Christmas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>POST EARLY!</head>
        <p>POST EARLY!</p>
        <p>Instructions To Note To Save</p>
        <p>Disappointment.</p>
        <p>Already the work at the Post Office has in- creased enormously, and in order to cope with the rush of Christmas week, the authorities have engaged, in addition to the present staff, a large number of women as auxiliaries. Some of the women will be employed as sorters and stampers. As in former years, the Parade Room at the Police Station .has been taken over for the dispatch and receipt of parcels. We would call our readers' attention to the following postal arrangements :</p>
        <p>WAR RI NGTON.</p>
        <p>On the 23rd and 24th of December the 12.10</p>
        <p>delivery will not be made. The 3.20 delivery will be made as usual.</p>
        <p>On the 23rd and 24th of December the final clearance for the night mail at the head office will he 9 p.m., and the late fee suspended. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>On the 23rd and 24th of December' the town letter boxes will be cleared finally, at 6 p.m. for the night, mail and at 7 p.m. for the local delivery. The above times also apply to all town sub-offices for both letters and parcels. FRODSHAM, HELSBY AND LYMM.</p>
        <p>On the `.-Ith December the last collection for the night mail at Frodsham, Helsby and Lymm will be made at 7 p.m.</p>
        <p>LYMM ONLY.</p>
        <p>The usual supplementary clearance from the sub-office letter box and the railway station wall box at 9 p.m. will be suspended on Christy mas Eve.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>PARCELS BY lit UN.</head>
        <p>PARCELS BY lit UN.</p>
        <p>Precaution It Is Necessary For All</p>
        <p>To Take.</p>
        <p>Senders of parcels by railway are advised that as an extra precaution, a duplicate address label should be enclosed in each parcel so that the name of the consignee may be ascertained in the event of the outside address beooming detached or defaced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>MILITARY WEDDING.</head>
        <p>MILITARY WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Pretty Ceremony .At St James</p>
        <p>Church.</p>
        <p>A very pretty military wedding took place by. special licence at St. James' Church on-Thurs- day. The contracting parties were Miss Lillie Holt Lake, daughter of Mrs. P. H. Lake and the late Mr. P. IL Lake, 275, Knutsford-road, and Private Henry John Palmer, of the Mechanical Transport of the A.S.C., and son of Mr. and ,Mrs. T. J. Palmer, of Forthaven,Thoreham-by- Sea. The officiating clergyman was the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, while Mr. Frank Booth pre- sided at the organ. The bride, who was given away by her uncle, Mr. Thomas Domville, was charmingly attired in a dress of brown satin, and carried a shower bouquet of pink carna- tions. She also wore a sold wristlet watch. The bridesmaid., Miss Ivy C. Palmer, were a blue dress and carried a shower bouquet of pink carnations. She wore a gold regimental brooch. The best man was Private Wilkinson, of the\ Mechanical Tfansport of the A.S.C. The reception was held at the Winmarleigh Cafe, Sankey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Support for the Red Cross Society.</head>
        <p>Support for the Red Cross Society.</p>
        <p>The lion. Treasurer of the local division of the British Red Cross Society acknowledges with grateful thanks the receipt of the first two weekly subscriptions to the funds of the above by the employes of Messrs. Plumpton, Ltd., Albion Works; week ending December 4th, tl 3s. 7d.; week ending December lltb, Zl Is.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</head>
        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</p>
        <p>LIEUTENANT S. SPRUCE.</p>
        <p>Burtonwood Man Wounded In Attack</p>
        <p>On German Patrol.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Samuel Spruce, of Burtonwood, has lost his left arm as the result of a gallant and successful attack upon a German patrol, "The Burtonwood Parish Magazine" says "Those who understand the heroic spirit of Lieutenant Spruce and those who are like him in that particular will easily enter into the bitterness of his disappointment, and sympa- thises with him and his family in the unusually hard experience through which he ie passing at the .early age of 24. _ _</p>
        <p>"Burionv.-ood N proud of its brave young soldier; proud, too, of all those who have gone forth from us to endure hard experiences at the Eastern and Western Fronts, who are not afraid (as so many are to face wounds, wounds which may prove fatal there and then, or later on, .agonies of shock and torture, which the sufferers themselves are powerless to describe."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>'MATE WILLIAM FEARNLEY.</head>
        <p>'MATE WILLIAM FEARNLEY.</p>
        <p>One Of Three Soldier-Brothers Wounded In The Shoulder.</p>
        <p>Private Williaji Fearnley, who, previous to the outbreak of war lived with his sister at 2, Fountain-street, Latchford, has been wounded while serving in. France with the Ist King's (Liverpool) Regiment. The news first reached Warrington on Tuesday morning, when Private Feqnley's sister received a post-card dated Dedember 11th from an Army Chaplain, in the following words; '".l am sorry to say that your brother)Private Fearnley, has been wounded in the shoulder. He is now much easier and is making progress. He asks me to send his love to all at home.."</p>
        <p>Private Fearnley, -who is 32 years of age and married, is a Reservist, having served for three years in the Army. He was called up imme- diately on the outbreak of war, having been employed by the London and North-Western Railway Company as a sheeter. He has two other soldier-brothers, one of whom is a Reservist Both are serving at the Front.</p>
        <p>Telephone 179. Telegrams : "Cunningham. Warrington."</p>
        <p>B. CUNNINGHAM,</p>
        <p>BREWER. WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>CROWN ALE, OATMEAL STOUT. EXTRA STOUT.</p>
        <p>iTOR</p>
        <p>Best House Coal</p>
        <p>NUTS &amp; SLACK, WASHED NUTS, BEANS, &amp; PEAS, :ROMPTO &amp; SHAWCROSS, LTD.</p>
        <p>COLLIERY PROPRIETORS. Dallarn Lane, Warrington,</p>
        <p>HERE WE ARE AGAIN!</p>
        <p>Just one week off Christmas Day. We wish you a Happy Christmas, and hope by this time next year the War will be won—BY L'S—and the Boys safely home again.</p>
        <p>KEEP</p>
        <p>ON SMILING!!</p>
