      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>IN MEMORIAM.</head>
        <p>IN MEMORIAM.</p>
        <p>Pastor's Tribute To The Late Sergeant-Major Davies.</p>
        <p>'HE DIED VINDICATING</p>
        <p>THE GOOD."</p>
        <p>An impressive service was held at the Gol- borne-street Baptist Church, Warrington, on Sunday evening, in memory of the late Sergt.- Major Davies, who for many years was one of the stalwarts of that place of worship. Sergt.- M'ajor Davies, as our readers are aware, was killed recently whilst on duty in Prance. Mrs. Dairies, his widow, attended the morning service, but ~was unable to attend at night, though a number of kelatives were present. The church was Crowded with many friends of the late Sergeant-Major, who wished to pay him a last tribute. As he 'held an important position on the staff of the Warrington. Corporation,. it</p>
        <p>was only fitting that the May-or\ (Alde-rman Peter Peacock) should be present. Ile was accom- panied by the Mayoress (Mrs. Peacock), th.-1 Deputy-Mayor (Dr. Joseph), Alderman John Evans (chairman of the Water Committee). Councillor Lindley (deputy-chairman of thu Water Committee), Councillors T. Locker, E. Cunnington, S. Gough, and Mr. James Gray (water engineer). Also in the church were four companies of the Warrington Free Church Boys- Brigade—of which Sergeant-Major Davies used to be an officer—under the command of Colonel A. Grounds, and six wounded soldiers of the 4th Smith Lancs. Regiment.</p>
        <p>The service was conducted by the Rev. If. Ogden, the pastor of the church. It opened with the hymn "Our blest Redeemer, ere He breathed," and the other hymns of the service were "Nearer, my God, to Thee," "Beneath the Cross of Jesus," and "Abide with me." Madame Sara Silcock rendered the solo "Sun of my soul," and at the conclusion of the Service Mr. S. Rickers (the organist) played the Dead March in "Saul." .</p>
        <p>The lesson was read from 11. Samuel, i, which contains the lamentation • of David over the death of Saul and Jonathan. The text for the sermon was-taken from the 25th verse of this chapter, "How are the ,mighty fallen in the midst of the battle! 0 'Jonathan, thou west slain in thine high places." Vindicated the Good.</p>
        <p>Opening his discourse, the Rev. H. Ogden re- ferred to the beauty of Jonathan's life. Be said that he (the preacher) had not had the privilege of knowing the late Sergeant-Major Davies from the days of his boyhood, but there were some present who had had that privilege, and he had no doubt that if they sought a phrase in which to express their poignant grief and desolation, it would be found in the phrase of David's, "I am distressed for thee, my brother Jonathan." It was something qf their sorrow that their brother died—if die he must--;</p>
        <p>when his face was not turntd towards the enemy. "When he died, he died vindicating the good," so said his Lieutenant-Colonel. Their brother died whilst he was vindicating the authority of the Army, and urging the responsibility to the Divine, lie passed to God on the highest plane, with the noblest sense of duty; he passed vindicating the love of God and Christ, whom he loved. The source of his authority was the 'Cross. His favourite hymn was "Master, speak Thy servant heareih,</p>
        <p>Waiting for Thy gracious Word, Longing for Thy voice that cheereth, Master, let it now be heard. 2 I 'am listening, Lora for Thee, What hast Thou to say to me?" The Basis of His Faith. Sergeant-Major Davies' religion was not one that desired the affection of the drawing-room or the seclusion of the Chapter House. It was a religion that he lived every day. His retigion was not .a faith that Would do anywhere: any time or anyhow. He had very Alefinite and dis- tinct beliefs. Their brother did not talk about brotherhood; he acted it. "I believe in God the Father" was the basis of his faith, as it was the basis of many of their faiths. When he acted in his capacity as leader, he gained an immense power because he knew how to serve. ,No man , could command if he could not obey.) He never 'took his men from the rest trenches to the billets without 'first leading them in prayer. Wherever Sergeant-Major Davies *inched human life, there they found praise for him from his fellow men. His loss would be felt in his own particular sphere of life. He commenced life with many disadvantages; there was no silver spoon to his lips, no cushion for his repose. What he had obtained was obtained by hard toil. He filled his sphere and they as a church might be pardoned if they were proud of 'him. They were proud of the position he filled in the Army, but they were also proud of him in the church, for he was fostered there. He went there as a lad. . He (the preacher) was not going to suggest that the church made 'his virtues, but the church nurtured them into a beantiful groove. They rejoiced that Sergeant- Major Davies left the world better than he found it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>CASUALTIES.</head>
        <p>CASUALTIES.</p>
        <p>SOUTH LANCASHIRE REGIMENT. 2nd Battalion.</p>
        <p>Expeditionary Force, reported 1 rom tll t, Base, December Bth Killed.--Private M. Myers (2,167); Private W. Taylor (19,210). Wounded.—Private A. Darby (18,768); Pri- vate J. Fallon (3,377).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>1. INCE-CORPOR 1L C. I).(\ :LL.</p>
        <p>NN PH-10101‘11 Local Athlete 'Wounded In The Leg.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Dagnall, who esides at 21, -HaMelds- road, Orford, on Wednesday morning received a field post-card from her husband, Lance- Cor-Poral Charles Dagnall, of the Lancashire Fusiliers, stating that he had been wounded. A letter from Lance-Corporal Dagnall, posted on a hospital ship, and received 'yesterday, stated that the wound was in the leg, and that he was progressing favourably. Lance-Corporal Dagnall, who is 36 years of age, served for ten years in the Territorials, at the end of which time he held the rank of sergeant. lie enlisted in the Fusiliers soon after the outbreak of war. He was formerly employed by Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., as a packer in the milling room. He was at one time a member of the Warrington Athletic Club, and has won many prizes for his prowess on the cinder track. He also acted as secretary for the above-named club for considerable time. He was educated at Heath- side School. and attended the Cairo-street Unitarian Church. He is well known and esteemed by a large circle of friends in War- rington. • lie has three children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>AN EPIC OF EMPIRE.</head>
        <p>AN EPIC OF EMPIRE.</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur Crosfield, Bart.,</p>
        <p>As Poet.</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur Crosfield has written "An epic of Empire," which comprises same hundred and fourteen stanzas.. It opens with an appeal to arms against the barbarities of the Buns, and is sounded first in the United Kingdom, then in</p>
        <p>the Colonies, leading to a certain amount of description of Colonial scenery, beginning with Niagara and the Canadian Rockies, and in- cluding some features of Indian and Himalayan scenery. The tour finishes in Egypt, and a survey of the empires which have come and gone, and brings the writer finally to the position of our own great Empire to-day. The following are the last three stanzas</p>
        <p>Many there are we ne'er shall see again. Our sailors and our soldiers of the line,. They find their resting-place beneath the main, Or foreign soil; the lads whom we enshrine In treasured memories, whose names shall</p>
        <p>shine In every hamlet, province, country, town ; We'll reverence the deeds which shall entwine Their story with our honour, and go down To all posterity, a tale of proud renown.</p>
        <p>A tale of sacrifice, 'tis true, and loss— The more 'twill be our glory through the</p>
        <p>years: When bloated Crcesus and his vulgar dross Are long forgotten, still the hopes and fears, The gay And cheerful humour which endears Sailor and soldier to us: and their grim And dauntless resolution shall with tears And smiles and pride be sung in honour's</p>
        <p>hymn, A triumph for all time which never can grow dim.</p>
        <p>Shoulder to shoulder through the shades of night, Together they have fought, but earliest dawn Now glimmers in the East with fairy light And heralds golden glory of the morn When peace on earth shall yet again be born. Then shall our warriors in their hosts return With glory of achievement to adorn Their triumph, from all kith and kin to earn The gratitude and pride with which all hearts shall burn. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
