      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>AFFLICTED SERBIA.</head>
        <p>AFFLICTED SERBIA.</p>
        <p>:4</p>
        <p>i)p loved by all who came to work with him and know him.</p>
        <p>sir. Gill, in replying, said he would preach he Gospel as it came to him, and be felt sure in time he would grow to love 811 who came into</p>
        <p>- _ touch with him. He thanked the ministers who had spoken such high praises of him, and the ladies who had helped in the tea meeting. The following ladies presided at the tables: Miss West, Mrs. A. Leather, Mrs. Miller, Mrs. E. Walker, Miss Walker, Mrs. J. D. B. Smith, Mrs. E. Guest, Miss A. ,Egerton, Miss E. Hes- keth, Mrs. C. Leather.</p>
        <p>The meetings were in every way a success, and we feel confident that there is a good time for the church ahead, with Mr. and Mrs. Gill amongst them. Special hymns were sung.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>RED CROSS WORK.</head>
        <p>RED CROSS WORK.</p>
        <p>Workirgmen's Committee Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The first ..t1 general meeting of the Working s Committee of ' the British Red Cross s' ty, was held in the Conservative Club on "iday, the 6hair being occupied by Mr. K. Tweedale. This gentleman, in a brief speech, spoke of the work carried on by the committee, and he felt sure that the subscribers ,would be satisfied with the result of the year's work, and of the substantial amount which had been handed to the Red Cross Hospital. When forming the Working Men's Committee, he said. no one thought that the war could continue so long, yet they had to fight on, and in conse- quence he urged the subscribers to give hearty support to the best of their ability.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>REPORT AND BALANCE SHEET. Over £5130 Collected.</head>
        <p>REPORT AND BALANCE SHEET. Over £5130 Collected.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hudson read the balance sheet and also his report, as follows :</p>
        <p>"Some twelve months ago a public meeting was called to form a Working Men's Committee to collect money towards the upkeep of the local British Red Cross Hospital; and at an adjourned meeting a committee, along with officials were elected, who soon got to work. "The village was divided into seven districts, with a total of twenty-five collectors. On November 17th, 1914, the first collection was paid in, since then the committee have met week by week, when the money collected has been handed in, there is generally discussions, and when. necessary, resolutions sent to the General Committee. "</p>
        <p>"During the twelve months something like 13,500 receipts have been given, which works out at about 91d. each receipt; to collect £53.3 15s. Bd. with the small expense of £6 Is. 3d., and let me here say that we have sufficient receipt books for another twelve months, which in reality brings the expenses down to £3 ss. 3d. I considered it a splendid amount to raise, when one takes into consideration that other organi- sations are tapping the residents, and hero I wish on behalf of the Working Men's Com- mittee to tender their best thanks to the resi- dents who have so nobly helped a most worthy cause, and our thanks are due to the employes of the Charles Moore an CO.'S Lymm .Self Works for their monthly gifts, to the Lymm Wednesday Football Club, to the Western Tennis Club, to the Savoy Concert Party, and to all others for their financial help. "In addition, the Working Men's Committee have organised three concerts, two for our own funds and one for 'Our Day.' funds; and in order to keep down the expenditure we did not give all the items of expenditure in connection with same. No doubt it would be interesting to the gathering to hear how the expenses of £ll, odd and £l3 odd are accounted for, but before reading out the separate balance sheets I would like to say that the Crosfield's Band, Warring- ton Glee Party and other artistes gave their services, which we appreciate. We had to pay their railway fares or conveyances. "With regard to concert on November 24th, I am still awaiting ticket money, but as near as I can say the net amount will be about £lB. "Before finishing. I would like to say a word for the sterling work of the collectors, who, week by week, give up their time to collect for their wounded brothers. We sincerely trust that there will not be a long necessity to do this kind of work, and that a speedy and victorious end to our arms will soon be forthcomina. but we ask that our friends will rally around the cause, which is essential until the enemy is beaten and our wounded fully healed. We require more collectors. Who will help?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Criticism Answeree.</head>
        <p>Criticism Answeree.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rogerson asked how the money had been paid over to the general fund, and was informed by' the treasurer various dates which proved satisfactory. • _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Barnes (subscriber) criticised the expenses or the concerts, drawing comparisons with the small amount incurred in the collections. The Treasurer, in reply, pointed out that it was not a fair comparison, as in collections, these were done by the committee. and the con- certs were efforts which could not be produced without charges being incurred. Every artiste</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Splendid Response to Appeal for help.</head>
        <p>Splendid Response to Appeal for help.</p>
        <p>During the last few months the eyes of the world have been centred on that small State in the Balkans whose army has made such a glorious_ stand against enormous odds, and is now left by the train of ghastly war in untold agony. Across the Continent of Europe, borne on the breeze the wails of children and home- less women have pierced our hearts, yet the spirit of this hardy race is still unconquered. "Help us in our misery" has been the cry, which has not been in vain, thanks to brilliant organisation of such ladies and gentlemen as the Rev. and Mrs. G. B. Thurston, of Lymm. On Wednesday, at the Lymm Grammar School, in consequence of an appeal for relief was held a sale. 'tea and entertainment on be- half of afflicted Serbia.</p>
        <p>Opened by Mrs. Stewart-Smith, of Lymm Ilan, it greatly exceeded even the expectations of the organisers who, within the last week, have worked so valiantly. A pleasing feature was the capital attendance of the inhabitants of the village and the surrounding districts. Mrs. Stewart-Smith, in a brief speech, touched on the untold sufferings of this Eastern people, and expressed the hope that no matter how slender the audience's purses, yet every little helped. The words were not uttered in vain, for as a result of the call the handsome sum of £250 was realised.</p>
        <p>The Lymm Grammar School is admirably adapted for work of this kind, and the stalls were both tastefully and artistically arranged. Such a conglomeration of useful articles we have never before seen, and it was no wonder that the patronage was unlimited. A feature of the day were the excellent entertainments which were attended in great numbers, the showing of _cinema films proving a great draw.</p>
        <p>A string band, arranged by Mrs. Cook. pla appropriate music during the day, which called for appreciative applause. Well done, Lymm ! The little Cheshi,•,, village can compare for patriotic response with any other hamlet or town in the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>CONGREGATIONAL. CHURCH, Recognition Services for the Rev. S. Gill.</head>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL. CHURCH, Recognition Services for the Rev. S. Gill.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday a good gathering of members and friends of the above church attended the recognition service of the Rev. S. Gill, the new minister. The chair was taken by the Rev. Dr. Powicke, of Romily. Prayer was offered by Mr. J. D. B. Smith, and the Scripture reading was by the Rev. T. W. Bond, of St. Helens. Addresses were given by the Rev. Dr. Powicke (secretary of the Cheshire County Congrega- tional Union), the Rev. C. Richards (Bowdon Downs Church), the Rev. W. D. Thomas (Broad- heath), the Rev. W. H. Turner .(chairman of the Congregational Union), the Rev. E. E. Lovell (Baptist Church, Lymm), Mr. Bladen; and the Rev. Dr. Scurrah. A presentation was made to the Rev- S. Gill, which consisted of an .autograph album and £25 cheque from Stab- bing Congregational Church, Essex, Mr. Gill's late church. A letter was read from the same church, saying how Mr. and Mrs. Gill would be sorely missed; they had won the esteem of both rich and poor. The other ministers all spoke of Mr. Gill's good work, and felt sure he would</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>3103/M IIMIEW1:1104COlgilir</head>
        <p>3103/M IIMIEW1:1104COlgilir</p>
        <p>4.-'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>EItAIVIIMart,SATURDAY DECEMBER 18, 1915.</head>
        <p>EItAIVIIMart,SATURDAY DECEMBER 18, 1915.</p>
        <p>who had so kindly assisted, had doiv't '-!"_,o with- out any .fee whatever, only receg railway fare or other small travelling,fripenses. Re- garding the cost of refreshments, the sum of tils. was spent on 70 to 80 people, which he .1-taped Mr. Barnes would agree was not a very .lavish amount. The treasurer invited this gen- tleman to join the committee and assist in the work.</p>
        <p>was employed as a maker of bricks at Statham. Fond of sports, he is well known in the village. The following are extracts from his letter : "I am at present in the best of health, and regret very much that I shall not be able to be home for Christmas. I was really expecting to have a week off at the festive season; but suppose it cannot be helped. A few weeks ago we were able to have a rest for a day or two, but we soon had to go back, as the Division which relieved us are going to . I am at present guard- ing a lot of bombs and shells, about two miles from the firing line. It is no use explaining how terrible this war is, for it would not make it any better, but it is a pleasure to know we are doing it for those at home, and our King and Country. French Mother's Heart-breaking Sobs.</p>
        <p>I have just been having a talk with an old man and his wife, who have had their home wrecked. I have seen some wrecked homes, but none like theirs. .1 can now understand a little French, and, they told me about losing their son, and the old lady was sobbing as if her heart would break. It was a sight, my sitting round a stick fire trying to make two miserable souls happy. I cannot write as I should like to, but will express it better when I come 'home. We are having rain every day. and in many places the water is overflowing the road, as the coun- try around here is very flat and monotonous. I am glad I have not to go in the trenches yet, .as They are up to the waist in water."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Splendid Balance Sheet.</head>
        <p>A Splendid Balance Sheet.</p>
        <p>Coum•illor Holt said that he had not collie to criticise, but to congratulate the committee on the splendid balance sheet which had been pro- duced. To hand over the sum of A:500 to the general fund was sufficient proof to show how earnestly and willing the committee had worked for this worthy object. Mr. Rogerson asked if the subscriptions to the general committee were being continued. The Acting Secretary (Mr. H. E. Odber), re- plying, said that Mr. Giles Hunt some time .ago made an appeal for further funds, and the re- sponse had proved very satisfactory. It was proposed by Mr. Brazen&amp;le and sec- onded by Mr. Rogerson that 'a vote of thanks be given to the Conservative Club for the use of the room, which was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>CORPORAL BEN ELLIOTT..</head>
        <p>CORPORAL BEN ELLIOTT..</p>
        <p>Killed Six Germans And Wants To Go</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Elliott, of emit-ex:bury 'Cottage, whose son is at preserit in hospital t Bitton, near Bristol, have this. week .:received the fol- lowing communication : , . • " I am sorry I cannot get home for Christmas, as I am not "quite strong enough yet, for I -am having twelve 'teeth drawn .and ,eight stopped. More pain; but I (don't mind so 'long as it cures the pains in my head, 'which are awful some- times. I shall not 'be sorry when I am well again, so that I may go back to France. I shall certainly-vOluriteer for active service again when I get back to London, as I am not satis- fied yet, and I don't 'think 1 should be doing my duty if I Stayed in 'England 'whilst there is work to be done. canna make out 'what is 'keeping all the chaps from 'joining. Fancy;being afraid of av German'! I killed six With ten !bonibs 'the morning 1 .was .wounded, rind 1 tool.' a helmet -off one of their heads after he lay nuitioriless. 1 was bringing it 'away when 'it was knocked 'out of my hand rind trampled upon, SD 11 'had to leave it:"</p>
        <p>Corpordl Ben 'Elliott's ' regiment, it Will be remembered, was the •2nd 'Scots 'Chiapas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>SAPPER :PERCY CLARE.</head>
        <p>SAPPER :PERCY CLARE.</p>
        <p>Rdidtes The Life Of Himself And Comrades In The Trenches.</p>
        <p>Sapper Percy Clare, who, upon the outbreak of war, was attached to the Lymm Territorials, and latterly to the Royal Engineers, .gives graphic details of life at the ,Front in a letter dated December 5, 1915, written to Air. D. Daniels, of Booths Hill. It is as follows : • Fun in the Mud.</p>
        <p>"In the first place let me assure you that I am in the best of health and spirits, for which I thank God. I don't think we are having it quite so cold as you are at home, but at any rate it has been very wet during the last week, which has made the trenches very dirty. For- tunately, most of us have good top boots,, which reach' well ,up the legs. They are fine, but an incident occurred only yestel;day which makes we laugh as I write. We were coming down from the trenches last' night, and it was very dark. When we put one leg forward it s,tuck in the mud, and we had to pull the other out with our hands. You can tell how long it takes us to go a little distance. In the afternoon of the same' day we had one man with us, only a little chap, with 'no top boots, and when we came to a pool of Water one of us had tO get him across on his back.</p>
        <p>"It's very funny, we might be going to a football match instead of into, the trenches, there is so much jollity amongst us. The other night it was raining fearfully and as dark as pitch. All at, once I heard a laugh and a splash. The man in •front had come to a pool of water upon which a log of wood was paling. He put his foot on it to get across, when over he toppled. I should have done likewise, only at that moment the Germans sent one of their lights up, so we, in consequence, could see our way across. You see, they do us a good turn sometimes. Howeyer, we all take it in good part, for it's no use grumbling, for we Might as well be happy as not.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Trench Concert.</head>
        <p>A Trench Concert.</p>
        <p>"We often have a concert, and this is the pro- gramme of the " Tommies" in the trenches. Performance day and night until further notice:—</p>
        <p>Overture, "Dawn's Expectations," gas shells and bayonets.</p>
        <p>Song: "Little Drops of Water," by Ration carriers.</p>
        <p>Sketch, "Breakfast," Tommy Atkins and Co. Exhibition of Aeroplane Flights, Allies and Germany. Duet, "Musketry Practice," Tommy and Fritz. A Glimpse of Hell, German Artillerymen. Juggling with bully beef tins and biscuits. Same as Item 5. By special request. Lightning cartoonist drawing rations, Ration carriers.</p>
        <p>Song, "The Toilers," Royal Engineers. Song, "Whisper and I shall hear," Listenihg Patrol.</p>
        <p>Grand Firework Display. -German star shells.</p>
        <p>Song, "Long here upon guard am I," by night sentries.</p>
        <p>Grand chorus, "A hunting we will go" (searching our shirt), by Tommy Atkins and Co. Subject to alterations at a moment's notice. Music supplied by Maxim machine guns. Re- freshments by Johnnie Bull and Co. Admission free to young men who will do their bit for King and country. Respirators must be worn. Standing room only. "Well, I must close now, as the candle burneth low."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>PRIVATE JOSEPH DANIELS.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JOSEPH DANIELS.</p>
        <p>Popular Lymm Soldier Home On Leave.</p>
        <p>Private Joseph Daniels, attached to the 6th South Lancashire Regiment, has this week been enjoying a well-earned rest from his labours at the Front. Before joining he lived at the Rookery, Booths Hill, Lymm. but immediately upon the declaration of war he offered himself for service to his King and country. -In June last he was drafted abroad, and was wounded in the right hand by a shell two months later. September last saw him safely in England at the Lord Derby Hospital, Winwick. A fortnight later be was sent convalescent to Leeswood 'Tall. on the North Wales coast.</p>
        <p>In civil life Private Daniels was employed by the Grappenhall Tanning Co. as a tanner, is married and has three children, lie worshipped at the Lymm Parish Church. In his younger days Private Daniels was a noted athlete, being captain of the local football club for many seasons. Whilst in • hospital he succeeded in winning a bowling handicap, playing with the left hand, the prize being a box of "Tommy's" favourites (Woodbines).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>TRUMPETER THOMAS LEIGH.</head>
        <p>TRUMPETER THOMAS LEIGH.</p>
        <p>At Present Guarding Bombs And Shells Near Firing-Line.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Thomas Leigh, Warrington-road, Statham, has this week received a letter from her husband, Trumpeter Thomas Leigh, at present "somewhere in France," serving in the Cycle Corps. He joined the Army on Septem- ber 4, 1914, and was drafted to the Front seven months ago. He was educated at• the Lymm School, and he attended worship at Lymm Church.</p>
        <p>Twenty-three years of age, Trumpeter Leigh</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>LYNIM PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>LYNIM PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. A. Watkin (presiding),. T. J. Ridgway, J. Cowan, S. W. Gould and R. H.</p>
        <p>Gotobed</p>
        <p>Too Busy to Attend.—Frederick Taylor, Par- tington, was summoned for being drunk on the licensed premises of Ellen Clarke, the Grey- hound Inn, Partington, on December .4th, and also for using 'obscene language in his home within hearing of persons on the public .high- way.—Taylor, who is employed on munition work at the Partington Steel and Iron Co., did not appear, but a letter was read by. the Chair- man from the firm stating that ,Taylor could not be spared to attend.—The case was ad- journed until the next !Sessions. No Name.—Joseph Matthews, marine store dealer, 2 Pump Yard, Warrington, was charged with allowing a cart to he used without having the owner's name and address uainted thereon. —Defendant, who pleaded guilty, stated that the cart had been hired out to two women rag- gatherers, who had arranged to -bring it back each week to be examined, but he had not seen his name on it for the last three years.—P.C. Bolsover proving the case, the defendant was tined ss.</p>
        <p>Willing to Return.—William Fleetwood, Broomedge, was summoned by William H. Bar- low, threshing machine owner, High L4ll, in respect to a claim for 19i. in lieu of notice and Ms. damage for loss of work through the engine being stopped. Fleetwood', it was stated, had left the complainant to take up another posi- tion, and had now written to say that he was willing to return to his former duties, and had asked Mr. Barlow to withdraw the case.—The niagistrates adjourned the case for a month to enable the defendant to fulfil his promise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Adult School ,Whist Drive.—There was a large assembly at a whist drive held in the Adult School on Saturday evening. The-following won prizes at whist:—Ladies': 1, Mrs. T. Norbury; 2, Miss Owen. Gent's: 1, Fred Langley; 2, Tom Thompson.</p>
        <p>Soldiers' Wives and Children.—A tea and en- tertainment for the soldiers' wives and children of, Stockton Heath will be given in the school on Boxing Day by the workers of St. Thomas' Band of Hope, tea being F.-,erved at 4 p.m. Last year the numfier of cbil4ren 'entertained was ,but this year it is .expected to reach 150. The workers provide the tea, but Mr, Hallows, Grappenhall-road, would be pleased to receive any extras Such as oranges, mince pies, or bon- bons, &amp;c., .or they could be left at the Post Office. Donors would be welcome to assist at tea.</p>
        <p>Free Will Offering Scheme.—The annual general meeting of the subscribers to the St. Thomas' free will offering scheme was held in the -Parish Room on Tuesday evening, the Rev. James Collins (vicar) being in the chair. The secretary, Mr. T. R. Westbrook, presented the accounts, which showed that the amount raised during the past year was £147 lie. 5d., a small decrease on the previous year's amount. After a short discussion the accounts were passed, and a vote of thanks was accorded the officials.—A vote of sympathy with Mr. J. T. Lee (the Vicar's churchwarden), who was unable to be present on account of illness, was also passed.</p>
        <p>Wives and Mothers at Tea.—The wives and mothers of the men who are on Stockton Heath Roll of Honour held a tea and entertainment in the Church Room on Monday. Nearly 100 ladies were present at tea, which was well served. Mrs. Henry Roberts presided over the entertainment, to which Mrs. Short and Miss Blackburne contributed songs in an accom- plished manner. Two dialogues entitled "The Postbag" and "England Wants Them" were presented by members of Mrs. WorralPs class, and were well received by the large audience. During the evening a short address was given by the Rev. the Hon. C. P. Cross, who spoke on character and the need of prayer and thrift. The arrangements for the tea and entertainment, which were ably carried out. were in the hands of Mrs. C. W. Hallows, Mrs. 'Woods, Mrs. Worrall and Miss Sewards.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Church.—A Wesley Guild social gathering and sale of articles was held in the schoolroom on Thursday evening. The sale was opened by Mrs. A. J. Ardern. Refreshments were provided by the members of the Social Committee. Games were .much enjoyed. Songs were sung by Miss May Isherwood, and recita- tions given by Miss Harrington and Mr. G. Whitfield. The proceeds of the sale, which were very Satisfactory, will be devoted to defraying the cost of soldiers' comforts to :be sent to the young men of the church and con- gregation who have responded to the call of King and country. Messrs. H. Ditchfield and IC. Hall. acted as secretaries, and their efforts were crowned with success. Miss Turner con- tributed a pianoforte solo, and acted as accom- panist during the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT Stockton Heath Detachment.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 20th December: Monday. Drill at Headquarters, 7.45. Wednesday. Christmas Shooting Handicap at the Drill Hall, commencing at 6 o'clock. Con- ditions: 10 rounds each deliberate and rapid at 50 yards' target, 25 yards' range, open sights, no slings. About 30 prizes. Entrance 3d. Thursday. Drill at Headquarters 7.45 if fine. If wet. fall in at the Institute for distribution of _ prizes.</p>
        <p>The O.C. desires to remind members of the benefits to be derived from joining the Appleton Rifle Club. Full particulars may be had from Messrs. R. M. Swinton or A. Shepherd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>NOTE.</head>
        <p>NOTE.</p>
        <p>There will be no drills or parades during th".., weeks following Christmas Day and New Year'.4 Day.</p>
        <p>By Order, F. WARBURTON, Officer Commanding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Sisterhood.—A free and easy social in connection with the Wesleyan Sisterhood was held on Thursday evening. Tea was provided in the schoolroom, after which .a concert was held. The artistes were Miss Jones, who con- tributed several solos, and also took part in duets along with Miss V. Jones. Miss Collins and Mr. G. Whitfield gave several recitations, the latter creating roars of laughter by his rendering of " Boggart o'th Stump." A vote of thanks to the artistes was proposed by the Rev. A. J. Morton. Mrs. W. J. Bate was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>Junior Guild.—The third of a series of lectures by. Mr. W. J. Bate on a junior missionary tour in India was given at the Junior Guild meeting Dry Wednesday evening. Starting with Bombay, he described the railway journey from this town to Delhi, giving details of the different towns en route. Descriptions of the famous bazaars of Delhi and -a visit to the Jumma Musjid, where 10,000 Mohammedans meet every week to offer prayers, were also given, and a short reference was made to the King's Palace, one of the halls of which was used at the Coronation Durbar of King Edward VII., on January Ist, 1903.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild: True Patriotism.—The usual weekly meeting of the Wesley Guild was held on Monday, presided over by Mr. W. E. Plinston. Owing to the inclement weather the attendance was not so good as usual, but an excellent paper on "What is True Patriotism?" was given by Mr. J. Hamblett, of Warrington. He said that if we are to be true patriots we must have the spirit of Christ in our hearts. In our diction- aries we are told that patriotism means love of one's country, but it means more than this. The true patriotism must emanate from the Church, and when politics are swept away and we all come to believe in the brotherhood of man, then, and only then, shall we get the spirit of true patriotism. Several of the Guild mem- bers, including the Chairman, took part in the discussion, and a very enjoyable evenin_g was spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>Private Percival Well.—Private J. Fred Percival, whose home is at Canal Side, Grappen- hall, writing to his wife and children :under date November 26th, says that he is quite well.. It will be remembered that he was wounded and afterwards drafted to Egypt. He is now with the transports, and in the letter says he is sailing for some unknown destination. "I am having a splendid time," he writes, "so can't grumble. Our Sam has had some cigarettes sent him from Houghton's and Higher-lane. He gave me some, and was very good to me when I got back to the regiment, as I had no money for two months through being in hospital when I was wounded." He adds a P.S. that he is not far from his wife's brother, Private John Statham; and that he is getting tired of travel- ling about, first on the railway and then on the sea. "But -I think we shall be settled soon," he concludes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT Grappenhall Detachment.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 19th December: Drill. Tuesday, 21st December. • Fall in at 'Head- quarters at 7.45 p.m. sharp. Musketry. Monday, 20th December. Friday, 24th December. Fall in at the Range, Bank Quay Bridge, at 7.30 p.m. sharp. A list of men re- quired to attend will be posted at Head- quarters as usual. N.B.—A further list of Drills, if necessary during the week, will be posted at Headquarters on Tuesday, 21st inst.</p>
        <p>Orderlies. Section-Commander C. F. Read. Private B. Cawley. Arrangements have been made for the train- ing of men attested under the Lord Derby Group Scheme, and names of those willing to avail themselves of the opportunity should be sent to the 0.C., c/o Mr. C. F. Read, " Glenholme," Victoria-avenue, Grappenhall, as early as pos- sible.' • Those men who have offered themselves for at- testation, and have been rejected, may be enrolled in the Volunteer Force forthwith, and it is hoped that every one in this district who has been rejected will at once join the local l)etachment. By Order, F. A. FROST, 0.C., (Platoon Commander). Headquarters. 18th December, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>VOLUNTEERS.</head>
        <p>THE VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>Members Of The Local Corps Entertained To Supper.</p>
        <p>STRONG MUSTER AT THE CHURCH</p>
        <p>ROOM.</p>
        <p>The members of the Grappenhall platoon of the Cheshire Regiment of the Volunteer Train- ing Corps were entertained to supper and to a musical evening on Tuesday in the Church Room by their Platoon-Commander, Mr. F. A. Frost. Mr. Frost presided at the supper table, and also present were Mr. Frank Warburton (platoon commander of the Stockton Heath platoon), the Rector (the Rev. 11. V. Pigot), who wore the uniform of a private of the V.T.C., Section-Commanders C. G. Hutchings, C. F. Read (who is also secretary and acting quarter- master) and, F. Short, and Sub-Section Com- manders W. Wanton and F. Tipping. After an excellent supper had been served and the loyal toasts honoured, a short address was given by Mr. Frost.</p>
        <p>MR. F. AYLMER FROST AND THE CRITICS. The Chairman said, that as all the members knew, it was the first time they had met as a detachment, and he did not think it was a bad muster, considering the short time they had been at work. The first drill took place on July 21st, when they were about ten or a dozen strong, but there had been a change since then. When the volunteer .movement was first in- augurated there was a certain amount of critic- ism throughout the country as to what use they were likely to be. Of course, any new institu- tion met with the armchair critic, who stayed at home and only talked—he was best at, home. (Laughter.) The men of maturei vision evi- dently disagreed with the armchair critics, for at the present time there were 'something like 300,000 volunteers in the country, and in Che- shire they had about 9,500. Getting closer home and looking at the Knutsford Battalion, they found it was just under 1,000 strong. That was quite good, but they were going to do better.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Limb of Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>A Limb of Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>The Knutsford Battalion was divided into certain , areas for matters of convenience, and these areas were sub-divided. Stockton Heath was the most thickly populated district within reasonable distance, and here a company. had been formed. The Grappenhall detachment was a limb of The Stockton Heath company. If the Grappenhall men wished to be separated from Stockton Heath—which he did not suppose they did—it would be possible if they, could raise a company at Grappenhall. That would mean securing 260 men, and it was absolutely impos- sible. He thought it was very pleasant to join their neighbours and help to, swell their num- bers and make up a company. So far, at Stock- ton Heath they had about a 'platoon and a half, and at Grappenhall they had half a platoon,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
