        <p>L--Backache Kidney</p>
        <p>which made up two platoons, or about half a company. They had a deal to do to bring their numbers up to strength, but Grappenhall would do its best to accomplish its share. The .actual number of drills that had been held at Grappenhall was 44, and the average attendance had been 26. He did not think that this was at all unsatisfactory, but it might be better. To some the drills might have become a little monotonous, but they were very import- ant. The members were not perfect yet, but they were getting nearer to that state. It was in the earlier stages of drill where the elements of discipline.were 'instilled into the troops, and the ,whole corps ought to move exactly like clockwork. They had in their detachment four " proficients." To be a "proficient" a man had to put in a. minimum of 40 drills and, to a cer- tain extent, shoot accurately. Though they only had four, there were many other men who were approaching the point of efficiency. Then he was pleased to say they had one marksman. He hoped that they would all follow this example and be marksmen. It eould be done; it was a Matter of practice. They could not do compaßY drill and extended formation with the numbers they had, but they would be able to do so when they:drilled with Stockton Reath. There was a great deal yet to be. done, and the More they did the more interesting would become the work. • Rivalry Wanted.</p>
        <p>Next summer, no doubt, they would have many occasions to drill with the Stockton Heath detachment, and he hoped, although Stockton Heath had had much start over them, that ultimately they would not only be able to bold their own, but would give Stockton Heath the lead in proficiency and drill. He wanted proper rivalry between the two detachments. At Easter, Whitsuntide, and the August Bank Holi- day they would have an opportunity of manceuvring with larger numbers.of men. That would be the time when they would show whether they had done good work or bad work. As they all knew, the war was continuing, and they had to get more and more recruits into the Army. Many of the recruits under Lord Derby's scheme had enrolled in the V.T.C., and it was in that corps that they could learn the ele- mentary stages of the profession they had adopted. These men would be shown the right spirit of patriotism, as well as the right way to drill.</p>
        <p>"".•••+..,</p>
        <p>was at the piano, and he also contributed two musical monologues.</p>
        <p>SHOOTING RANGE PROVIDED.</p>
        <p>Through the good offices of Air. J. B. Park- house (L. and N.-W. Railway Co.'s district man- ager) the corps has been afforded the sole use of the L. and N.-W. range at Bank Quay Insti- tute.</p>
        <p>Pints</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Corps Will Be Wanted.</head>
        <p>Corps Will Be Wanted.</p>
        <p>• Concluding, Mr. Frost thanked the Rector for putting the room .at the disposal of the detach- ment, and also expressed his appreciation of the arduous work performed by the instructor, Mr. F. Taylor, and of the assistance of the volun- tary helpers at that evening's entertainment. (Applause.) The health of Mr. Frost was drunk with musical honours, on the proposition of Section-. Ceosmmnadle Mr. Frost briefly po Mr. Frost proposed the toast of the comrades of the Stockton Heath company, coupled with responded. C. G. Hutchings. Commander the name of Mr. Frank Warburton, the platoon commander.</p>
        <p>Responding, Mr. Warburton expressed the hope that attested men were taking the oppor- tunity of training with the V.T.C. He believed that the corps would be wanted in some capacity before the war was over. He had great pleasure in being present. (Applause.) During the evening a number of humorous songs were sung by Mr. Walter Moores. Mr. R. W. Wild also rendered a song. and Mr. W. B. Forshaw gave a recitation. Mr. Albert Trow</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Our Recruits.—The final appeal for recruits under the group system has resulted in the following being attested Acomb, G. -Acomb, W. Ainsworth, E. Ainsworth, E. Ashton, J. Atkinson, D. Atherton, J. Bowyer, A. Bowyer, J. Bowden, sen., Bowden, jun., Black- burn, R. S. Crosby, R. Cookson, W. Casii, J. Carrington, A. Corradine, Chamberlain, F. Davies, Herbert Davies, W. J. Davies, . D. Dobson, W. Dow, D. Fowles, 0. Gibbons, T. Goulden, A. Gouldeu, W. Gibson, H. Glover, W. Hunter, A. Hampson, N. Hatton, G. Hicks, A.. Hicks, C. .114 gins S. H. Hall, J. J. Ireland, t.Jackson, G. Kirkham, A. Lightfoot, E. Light- foot, A. Loxley, A. Leigh, I. Lloyd, F. Massey, D. Massey, J. Moss, V. Owen, S. Plumpton, W. Phoenix, J. Pownall, G. Peever, John Rother- ham, James Rotherham, John Rigby, W. Ray, M. Ray, E. 'tickers. W. Scott, I'. -Sylvester, W. Share, A.: Taylor, .1. Wilkinson, C.:D. Wilkin. son, J. Whitfield, .1. Worslev.and G. Walsh: The Penketh Tanning C*olllllZlll.‘'' Ma (10 suit- able arrangements by which employes could be attested on the premises, of wixich many accepted the opportunity.</p>
        <p>A Sapper's Experiences.—Sapper Tom R. Rigby, a one time employe of the Penketh Tan- ning Co., and well-known to .everyone in tlll3 district, has just returned to headquarters after sick leave following an attack of pneumonia. contracted in the trenches. Joining the service in August of last year, he first went to Chatham, then on to County Cork, where he stayed six months. Two months on munition work in the North of England followed, this, after which he found some exciting times during his three and a half months in the trenches. Attached to the Prince of Wales' Guards, he had some very near calls when placing wire entanglements for the Huns' special benefit. On one occasion, when following up a charge which did not reach maturity, he with five others and an officer lay as flat as they could make themselves on the ground. while the bullets N histled all around them. Another time a shell -dropped in such close proximity to him. and his companions that had it burst (which it failed to do) not one would have been left to tell the tale-. As it was they were simply smothered with mud from head to feet. To make up for such vexatious treat- ment, hoWever, two nights' special bomb bom- bardment resulted in the taking of forty yards of German trenches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>WHITLEY.</head>
        <p>WHITLEY.</p>
        <p>Death of an Old Inhabitant. —Mr. Samuel Maddocks, of Greenhills, one of the oldest in- habitants in the district, died on Monday morn- ing. Mr. Maddocks, who had not been well for some time past, had formerly held the positior. of second gamekeeper to the late Lord Delainere. He was well known in the district, and highly respected. He died in his 86th year.</p>
      </div>
