      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>BEWARE OF WINTER CHILLS.</head>
        <p>BEWARE OF WINTER CHILLS.</p>
        <p>Peps Guard Throat And Lungs.</p>
        <p>The suddenly changing temperatures of early winter at once find the "weak spot" in the throat and chest, unless the weak spot is pro- tected day by clay by the strengthening germ- destroying fumes of Peps tablets. , Peps are not ordinary cough lozenges. They are in tablet shape, but the active medicinal elements in them are released in air-like form as the tablet .dissolves in the mouth, and are thus breathed into the bronchial tubes and lungs in just the same way that the cold, germ infected air is breathed in.,. But being germicidal, the Peps medicine destroys and successfully wards off the cold- germ before it can entrench to irritate the breathing passages mid sow disease in the lungs. It is foolish even for the most robust man to flatter himself upon his apparent healthiness. A cold is the forerunner of pneumonia, and pneumonia flourishes on apparently healthy folk. Far better to be cautious and suck a Pep or two every day. Peps stop colds in old and young, and bring ease and comfort to chronic bronchial sufferers also. Peps protect the lungs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>WAR-TIME RENTS.</head>
        <p>WAR-TIME RENTS.</p>
        <p>Trades Council Opposed To Population Limitation.</p>
        <p>Rents i n war time was one of the subjects which came up for discussion at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council, held at the Socialist Hall on Tuesday evening, under the presidency of Mr. Edwin Plinston.</p>
        <p>The Executive Committee at a recent meeting passed a resolution, which was forwarded to the Workers' War Emergency .Committee, asking the Labour Party in the House of Commons to oppose any population limit.. in the proposed Bill, as the burden of increased rents was felt just as keenly by the workers in small industrial centres as in the larger towns. Congestion was not .confined to the larger„Centres; 'neither bad landlords been - any more considerate in the smaller towns. The hardship Was uniform in it; operation, and the meagre wage increase, includ- ing war bonus, included workers in large and . small towns. No consideration was made by employers for variation in localities and no preference should be shown in any legislation. The Workers' War Emergency Committee re- plied that a similar amendment was being put forward, and that they were of the opinion that the Bill should be general in its application without restriction to population. • Mr. Charles Dukes (secretary) said he did .not think they could effectively protest. It was considered by the Labour party in the House • of Commons that there would be certain 'modifica- tions in the Bill at a late period. So far as Warrington was concerned he did not see • the Bill was going to be of any assistance whatever. It simply permitted the landlord to, go as far in the matter of increased rents as the Town Coun- cil' had seen fit to go in increased rates. To his mind it was just another means of enabling the landlords to pass on the taxation to the rent- payers. The Council' confirmed the action of the Executive Committee, and authorised them to collect further information concerning rents and and to take any action they considered neces- sary.</p>
        <p>Some. men worry for fear they will not get; justice, and some others worry for fear they will.</p>
