      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>GUARDIANS AND OVERSEERS.</head>
        <p>GUARDIANS AND OVERSEERS.</p>
        <p>Parish Authorities Threatened</p>
        <p>With Legal Proceedings.</p>
        <p>REFUSE TO PAY "CALLS."</p>
        <p>On the agenda at the Warrington Board of Guardians' meeting on Wednesday was the item that in pursuance of notice Mr. J. Billinge would move :—" That the 'proceedings author- ised to be taken against the overseers of the township of, Newton-in-Makerfield be suspended until the 12th day of January next, and that a deputation be appointed cons.:sting of the Chair- man and- Vice-Chairman of the Board, along with the Clerk, to interview the said overseers with • the purpose .of an, amicable arrangement being arrived at for payment of their calls now due,"</p>
        <p>At the previous meeting of the Board, the Clerk (Mr. A. Bottomley) reported .that the " calls " due from the township of Newton-in. Makerfield on October 23rd and November 20th last had not yet been paid to the Union's treasurer, and as he (the Clerk) had been in- formed that the 'overseers had decided not to pay the calls owing to the decision of the Guardians regarding the parochial Hats, he now asked the Board to 'authorise him to take legal proceedings against the overseers to enforce payment of the calls, if he /deemed such action to be necesSnry. A 'resolution was passed authorising the Clerk to take what proceedings were necessary to enforce the payment. At Wednesday's meeting Mr. A. J. Jolley (the chairman of the Board) ruled that Mr. Billinge's motion was out of order until the previous resolutionlad been rescinded. , The Rev. A. M. Mitchell suggested that in future. the Clerk should not place resolutions on the agenda when they were out of order.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: It is not for the Clerk to settle as to whether a resolution is out of order or not.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell maintained that 'it was 4. waste of time and energy. Mr. G. Mather: Mr. IliHinge ought to have had more wisdom.</p>
        <p>Mr. Billinge accepted the ruling of .the Chair- man.</p>
        <p>The Matter was again referred to later in thQ meeting. Mr. Billinge said. that it was unfor- tunate that when Mr. • Bottomley asked for powers to proceed against the • Newton-in- Makerfield overseers be did not ask for similar ,powers with regard to other Overseers who were in• the same position. • - •</p>
        <p>The- Clerk replied that he never threatened 'overseers when they got behind with their calls. With regard to Newton-in-Makerfield he asked for legal proceedings against them because they had said that they would not pay. He had first written them _a friendly letter, but when they said that they would not pay, be was bound, On the .Board's behalf, to . take legal 'proceed-</p>
        <p>ings. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: Is .the Board sure of its legal position? The Chairman replied that they could not open that question again. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>WARRINGTON GUARDIANS, Military Authority Inspects A Workhouse Block.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON GUARDIANS, Military Authority Inspects A Workhouse Block.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Board- of Guardians wEiS held at the Workhouse on "Wed- nesday, Mr. A. J. Jolley being in'the chair. •The Chairman said that Dr. Jagger (the resi- (lent medical officer) had been invited to apply for a commission in the R.A.M.C., and as he had responded to this invitation it was possible that he would have to go away at any moment. It was decided to leave the, matter of tempor- ary filling Dr. Jagger's post, in the event of his being called away, to the Chairman of the House Committee and the Clerk. The House Master reported that Colonel Coates, of the Western. Command, .visited .the institution on the 13th, and had inspected the " aged and infirm": block. He was particu- larly pleased with it, and said that it would snit the military authorities in every way. .11-e had also visited the Cottage Homes, and said there would have ,to be .a very bad case of emergency before .he recommended that they should be used for wounded soldiers.</p>
        <p>Ready and Anxious. . Here the lecturer threw upon the screen the portrait of a man well known in the financial world.</p>
        <p>" This," he said, " is one of the great captains of finance. .1 do not need to mention his name. His face is familiar to all of you, 'Look at his corrugated brpw, the furrows in his cheeks, the Pouches under his eyes, the deep lines about his mouth. That face, my friends, bears the unmiStakable and ineffaceable stamp of care. Anxiety has marked it indelibly.' It shows the traces of Sleepless nights, weary days, and bitterly fought campaigns, with millions of dollars at .stake. Success brings such a man no happiness. Look at him! How many of you, my friends, would change places .wftli him? How many of you would be Willing to take his Wealth if compelled to assume the terrible burden of responsibility that goes with it?"</p>
        <p>His hearers rose en masse. "I would !" they shouted in unison</p>
        <p>•:.• • • 4.• ••••• *0 *0 • Children' 8 Corner.: •</p>
        <p>+ Conducted by UNCLE JACK. 4, 46 ++++4,46+++++++. ++++++++.</p>
        <p>Children, Join This Corner! Enter The Competitions! Write Your Best! Always Be Glad! Uncle Jack, " Examiner," Warrington,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>CHRISTMAS.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS.</p>
        <p>MY PEAR CiIILDREN,-It seems only a few moments since' I wrote telling :von that Christ- mas would be here in a fortnight and that you would be .counting off the days as they aped by. To-day we are only seven- days away from the great day, and even before then your holidays will have begun. What a joyful time it wOuld have been had net the trouble and Sorrow of the war been haoging heavily upon us. What might have been But for the war many mil- lions of children would have had fathers and brothers—and mothers and sisters, too, more slrime ii pun the Germans—who are to-day dead. We shall endeavour to bear our Sorrows man- fully and look upon the season more than ever as one of hope. Peace will come again some day, and there may be goodwill even between us and the Germans; but that lies in ihe future. Our immfdiate concern is to make Christmas as happy as it can be for as many as passible. I suppose you will all be counting the days to the time you " break up"; and then to Christmas Day.. You will find lots ,of pleasure iii doing so, and I.hope you will all wake good use of your time when it does arrive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>PRIZE-WINNER.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE-WINNER.</p>
        <p>Last week I asked you to write me a !letter on "How I can be happ at Christmas." I have had, a good many letters, but only a few children have attempted to state their reasons for, what they say. It is ,a grand thing to know why you believe anything and I hope my nieces and nephews will learn to have opinions on the subjects. Upon which they write.</p>
        <p>But I know you are getting impatient to know ,who has earned the Christmas prize. Well, here is the secret. The prize goes to</p>
        <p>Niece Mary Roche, 6, Bostoek-street, Bank Quay, Warrington.</p>
        <p>In a very nice letter, Mary—all the Marys I know are very good girls orWoinen—speaks,only of giving pleasure to ,otheis. -I do ,not say that we should ,uot eon Sider ourselves at all upon these special occasions, but you will all find as you grow older that the .greatOt of,</p>
        <p>happiness can be had from making other people happy. Mary has sfottnd out that secret •very early in life. Here is her letter "Dear Uncle Jack,-7I should like to spend the coming holidays in sending presents to our soldiers and sailors, to let them know that we at home are thinking of them and to hope that they may have a happy Christmas and plenty of victories in the New Year; "Dear [fide, Jack,--1 should also like to send the little boys And girls ofs._Belgium some presents, but 1 alit only a little girl and I take nearly all my pennies to school for the sol- diers; but I will pray to Jesus that He will give the Belgian boys and girls a happy Christ- mas, and also that He will give then': their country back soon. • "-Dear Uncle Jack,—l should also like to spend the coining holidays visiting the wounded soldiers in hospitals and to take a present each."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>4:14.4,43,44,404:;.0?•:. 4,•:,...1**+0 004+ 4+44.04:00400 4 .44.4..41,0 &lt;i)at. 4:‘,404..v:. 4)o‘ .40 0 40 0 434)...):04, 0 0 4.434•4144.***4441414444 449... 4 440 0 Oit“:. .1 . . ~. .. _</head>
        <p>4:14.4,43,44,404:;.0?•:. 4,•:,...1**+0 004+ 4+44.04:00400 4 .44.4..41,0 &lt;i)at. 4:‘,404..v:. 4)o‘ .40 0 40 0 434)...):04, 0 0 4.434•4144.***4441414444 449... 4 440 0 Oit“:. .1 . . ~. .. _</p>
        <p>of several hunts are working hard as teachers and grooms, or, at least, three Government con- valescent remount depots. Mr. Cecil Alden made the sug.:.estion, and a considerable Lumber of ladies placed their time, serv.(es and ex- perience at the disposal of the War I lqice, thus releasing a number of men fur active service. Parties of young officers, belonging to mounted regiments, are to be seen daily in Hyde Park, learning- to ride under the guidance of one .of these ladies. All the rough work of the depots is done by the lady grooms, who clean the stalls, feed the horses, exercise them, and tend the sick animals. The ladies wear in some cases hunting breeches, and live in farmhouses near the depots, where they are often at work twelve hours daily. Ribbons For Christmas Gifts.</p>
        <p>There is no more adaptable medium for the mabing of acceptable, yet- inexpensive Christ- mas gifts, than the beautiful ribb6ns which even in war time "are cheap, and make a goodly show for sixpence." A little lace, some fine nainsook, or thin silk, and a generous supply of narrow ribbon, will make the, prettiest of camisoles, always an acceptable offering to young girl. Deft fingers too, may evolve in a couple of hours the most delicious of pinafores. or tiny dresses, from a short length of spotted or other- muslin, a little fine .embroidery, and plenty-of ribbon. A 'child's bonnet is easily made from less than two yards of a pretty chine ribbon, to which strings are added, of a plain colour. Little books to hold packets of needles, stickiiig plaster, bodkins and other trifles, afford scope for ingenuity and taste, and although not particularly novel, are still valued for their utility. The chine and brocaded ribbons are the most useful for the manipulation of . these trifles. They also make -the Most delightful bags, and despite the revival of pockets, these useful receptacles are still to the fore, and show no .signs of a waning popularity.</p>
        <p>Simple Evening Frocks. That simplicity will be the keynote of all Christmas party frocks this year,. goes without saying. Fortunately the output of chiffon has not been affected by the war, for this delightful material is in universal demand for afternoon frocks and evening gowns. Silk net and crepe georgette, with tulle, and mousseline de soie, are also all to the fore. But -chiffon appears to combine pdmirably ..with all sorts of unlikely materials, and more especially with certain furs. The possessors of old-faohioned seal or moleskin cloaks or coats, may turn these to</p>
        <p>good account this winter. They can be cut into bands for the trimming of chiffon skirts, the small piece's coming in for the corsages and sleeves, or these may be used on blouses. Soft effects in black and white are in high favour, and chiffon and lace play important parts in these confections, whilst novel and. most at- tractive evening frocks with trains and blouses are shown made almost entirely of ribbon.</p>
        <p>Wait and See Before Buying. It is not always wise to buy winter clothes early in the season, as the modes invariably undergo modifications later On, or they may be changed altogether. A wider choice is to be had at the mid-season. The models too are more exclusive, for it is the earliest winter modes that are copied for the shops that make ready-to-wear garments their speciality. In the beginning of the autumn the needs of the well developed woman were altogether ignored by designers, who introduced styles suited only to the slim and youthful. But now that mid- winter is approaching the modes have been changed, and the styles that proved so trying to many have given place to those more becoming to buxom middle age. The trained velvet gown for evening wear is one of the amendments, moreover the front is made to cover the instep. Some of the corsages for day wear are quite loose, and these suit the matronly figure very well. So does the evening gown that has no waist. at all, but falls in one unbroken line from the bust to the feet. And both of these are modifications of early autumn modes. Mid-winter Modes.</p>
        <p>The same rule applies to millinery. The high postilion hat suited a certain type .of 'youthful face, butte any other it was most unbecoming. Its place will be taken by the Niniche, which has come back during the last fortnight. Its _return will be welcomed, for its drooping side brims give the softening effect which th 9 postilion lacked. This shape had quite a rage a few years ago, and is at its best in black velvet. There are also many toques that seem to have been designed especially for the older woman. The most becoming of these have a high trim- ming of ribbon bows and loops set towards the back. • , Some new furs have also made their appearance, among these are Himalayan cat and natural lynx, both attractive in appearance. One or two novel shapes in muffs may be men- tioned, the leg of mutton and the Zeppelin being the most remarkable. The first resembles the sleeve of the same name, and is bigger at one end than the other, finishing above one elbow with a wide Cuff.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>A Pioneer Women's Society. The Society for the ,Employment of Women has done much to fit. the sex for business, and the work it is now called upon to undertake. The census of 1851 showed that there were only four hundred women employed as clerks in the British Isles. In 1891 they numbered nearly eighteen thousand, and this increase was due mainly to the Society's efforts. Miss Ilarriet, Martineau may be said to have originated the Society, which Was the outcome of an artiele written by her for the "Edinburgh Review.' In this the writer pointed out that the two million women of these isles' dependent upon their own exertions for their living, had: but two occupations open to .them—teaching domestic service, for neither of which was any training considered necessary.. Needlework, it is true, gave employment to women. who. were not governesses or servants, but it was wretchedly underpaid 'and unorganised. The Society established classes for training girls in various occupations, and did pioneer work in assisting them to take up industrial 'and com- mercial employments.</p>
        <p>Warner Land Railways. It is difficult to realise that two thousand women are employed by one railway company alone. But that is the number now working on the Great Central Railway. Not only are -women serving as porters; ticket collectors. window and carriage cleaners, but they have invaded the signal .boxes, where they register the trains. All classes are employed, from the University woman to the charwoman. Some of the positions occupied by the former are very responsible, and demand considerable initiative; but so satisfactory is the report of the Chairman that some of the best paid posts will soon be offered to women. Two hundred women are now working in the machine shops of the Midlani Railway Co. Their efficiency came as a -revela- tion to the directors, who report they are keener than men, and earn more money at piecework.</p>
        <p>Lady Riding Masters and Grooms. Hunting women have not lagged behind in serving their country at this crisis. Members</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
