      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Profitable Recipes And Useful</head>
        <p>Profitable Recipes And Useful</p>
        <p>Greasy Sinks.</p>
        <p>The quickest way of cleaning a greasy sink is it) rub it with a piece of flannel dipped in paraffin.. It will remove all grease and save labour -and time. This is especially applicable to glazed earthenware sinks. Horseradish.'</p>
        <p>To bottle horseradish:—Six tablespoonfuls scraped or grated. horseradish, 1 tablespoonful white sugar, 1 quart vinegar. Scald the vinegar; pour boiling hot over the • horseradish. Steep a week, strain and bottle. Exposure to the air will discolour it. Seal air-tight when putting away. Children's Hair. . .</p>
        <p>Each child should have a brush and comb Of its own, which should be kept in a bag or case apart from any other and frequently washed in warm water, to which a little ammonia or wash- ing soda' has been added. Italian Soup.. •</p>
        <p>Ilb. each of turnips, • carrots, onions, macaroni, .1 small beetroot, loz. parsley, 2oz. butter, 2 'quarts 91 water, 1 pint of.. milk. Slice. the vegetables (except the beetroot), boil them in the water, break the .macaroni small, and boil it in milk. When the vegetables and macaroni are cooked mix them; add the parsley. and also the beetroot, previously boiled and cut up small, adding the butter and seasoning. 'Teat up and serve.</p>
        <p>Palestine Soup. 21b. of jerusalem artichokes, 1 large onion, 2 or 3 stalks (not hea(ls) of celery,' 20z. of butter, 1 quart of milk, 2 bay leaves. Peel and throw into .viliegar and water - the :artichokes, put them with the onion and celery into a stewpan in which the butter has been melted; cover the pan and let the vegetables steam in their own juice until tender, taking care they do not brown when quite soft add the milk, which must slowly get hot but not boil; then take out the onion and celery, and rub. the rest through a hair .sieve with. a wooden spoon; put the puree back in the pan with salt, pepper, and the bay leaves; allow, it to boil for about half an hour. Just before serving, remove .bay leaves and stir in a gill of hot cream or loz. of butter.</p>
        <p>Cabbage Soup. 1 cabbage, loz. of butter, 1 tablespoonful of chopped parsley, 1 small onion, 1 pint of milk, 11 pints of boiling water, 1 tablespoonful of semolina, half teaspoon of salt, quarter tea-</p>
        <p>spoon of pepper. Remove the outer leaves from the. cabbage, cut it in half, and soak in cold water 1 hour, then cut out the stalk, shred the cabbage finely, and put it into the saucepan with the boiling water, the milk, the onion, the parsley, chopped very small, the butter, pepper and salt. Let these come to boiling point, cook for • 1 hour; sprinkle in the semolina, stirring all the time, and then boil 10 minutes longer. Serve very hot with toasted bread cut into squares. This soup is improved by being passed through a hair sieve and then put into a saute- pan to get hot again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>ONLY THING THAT MATTERS.</head>
        <p>THE ONLY THING THAT MATTERS.</p>
        <p>Nephew .John Webb, 27; 'Catherine-street, Warrington, writes:— " I shall not le very happy this Christmas unless my brother .comes home on leave. if he does come home everybody in our family will be overjoyed. That is about the only thing that troubles me just now. "As this is the last eSsay till Christmas, I wish you.d. Merry Christmas and a very Happy New Year."</p>
        <p>I value 'John's good whilieS, more especially as the week before last I failed to announce a subject Upon which my nieces and nephews could write., John 'says lie 'is sorry he did not write last week. "The fact is," he adds, " I could not find the name of the short- story I had to write about. If you .did not, put the heading in it was no't my fault at all." My fault, John! My fault!, But when Uncle Ned returns we shall not have these little blunders. I am glad you wish me a. Merry Christmas, although I disappointed you.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>LOVE TO UNCLE NED.</head>
        <p>LOVE TO UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>Niece Ethel 'Fair, Packet House, Bridge-street, Warrington, after telling me how she expects to be happy .on Christmas .Day with the aid of her mother—mother is the centre upon which all our happiness in this .world turns— writes a "PS." Ladies, they say, •always put the most important part of their letters in a ."P.5.," and this ypung -lady is following the lead of her sex. "Give my best love to Uncle Ned" is her parting word. I have often wondered why my nieces and nephews don't 'more fre- quently send their love to Uncle Ned, who has gone to so much trouble to amuse and instruct them, and who is now \ a soldier taking his part in defending them. Uncle Ned gets his "Examiner" every. week, and would welcome the good wishes of his young friends. He may even send them a short letter sometimes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>CHRISTMAS PARTY.</head>
        <p>A CHRISTMAS PARTY.</p>
        <p>Niece Beryl Malkin, Royal Exchange Inn, Golden Square, Warrington, writes:— " Dear Uncle Jack,—The thought of Christ-</p>
        <p>mas delights .me. Because it is .the _birthday of our saviour it adds to my happiness. "Christmas is a day of enjoyment, but 1 do not think that it will be such an enjoyment to our brave soldiers. I do not think it possible, but I sincerely hope that -peace will be de- clared before the 25th Of this month. •</p>
        <p>I hope to be happy on Christmas Eve.. On this particular night I am sending my Christ- mas, cards.</p>
        <p>" When Christmas morning arrives I ani going to church, which, I think, is the best way to begin a happy day. After dinner I hope to receive my guests, for I always have a party on Christmas Day. Then I hope to have a good and merry -time. We would play games and sing songs. . .</p>
        <p>•• Wishing you a Merry Christmas ,and a Bright New Year." A very good letter, indeed, Beryl; but you must post your -cards before Christmas Eve. If everyone posted his scards on Christmas Eve the postmen would not have a very happy Christmas Day. And the postmen now, you know, are postwomen ! We must not overload them, must we?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>Suggestions.</head>
        <p>Suggestions.</p>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>Misery loves company, but the company isn't apt to call again.</p>
        <p>Some men treat their wives all tight—because the wires 'decline to stand for any other treat- ment.</p>
        <p>- "When a man has a rip in his coat and only three buttons on his vest," writes a western sage, "he should do one of two things, either get married or get divorced."</p>
        <p>Her Choice. Maye: "Which would you rather marry—a rich bachelor or a rich widower?" Joy: "The widower. Be will hare learned to give up without a struggle."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>Wouldn't Tea.</head>
        <p>Wouldn't Tea.</p>
        <p>" The milkman's hire s 1 -girl is ill. I in- quired after her this morning, and he -insulted me."</p>
        <p>'Why,. how F" •"1 said: How's your milkmaid?' And he said: 'That's d trade secret.'"</p>
        <p>Cheering Her Up a Bit. A young lady living in Atlanta visited tha home of her fiance in New Orleans. On her return home an old negro "mammy,". long in the service of the family, and consequently privileged to put the question, asked: "Honey, when is you goin' tO git married?" The engagement not having been announced, the Atlanta girl smilingly replied: "indeed, I can't say, auntie. Perhaps I shall never 'marry." The old woman's jaw fell. "Ain't dat a pity, now !" she ejaculated, and after reflection, she added consolingly, " Dey •do say dat ole maids is de happiest critters dey is, once dey quit strugglin'."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>GIVING THE SECRET AWAY!</head>
        <p>GIVING THE SECRET AWAY!</p>
        <p>Nephew Sam :Brown, 40, Wakefield-street, who attends Heathside. School, tells me in a few words how be will enjoy the holidays. Among other things be is'going to amuse himself with the toys lie receives from Santa Claus, "who," he says, " ray father !" Fancy saying that of Santa Claus! It is sufficient to make him send a very sharp note to the headmaster of Heathside School—although, on .second thoughts, I don't remember Santa Claus ever having sent anything to anschoolmaster up to the present. Sam seems to have a •very good father, however, for • his ' brother, Nephew Harold Brown, says he " can be happy by not only thinking of what-I am going to have, but by giving something to the people that need it." Niece Lily BlackhOuSe, 3, Kendrick-street, Warrington, writes me a neat letter, saying she will be very -happy playing with the toys that 'Santa Claus will bring her, and with her sister. A 'very pretty little Christmas picture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>LITTLE FOLK'S COLUMN.</head>
        <p>A LITTLE FOLK'S COLUMN.</p>
        <p>I am happy to. tell you that the Editor has arranged, as a Christmas treat for my nephews and nieces, to print on this page next week a special. Little Folk's Column. You may like 'to know that the Editor takes a great interest in children and all that they do. In fact, I think he Would make a really first-class uncle to take charge of you. He is always anxious about you, and his desire to provide you with this extra column for Christmas Eve and Christmas Day is really kind. I hope you will read it, and that you will like it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>NEW MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>I have much pleasure in welcoming the fol- lowing new members to the Children's Corner, giving their • numbers :</p>
        <p>537 Clara Warburton, 3, Bostock-street, War-</p>
        <p>Attends Holy</p>
        <p>rington; eleven years of age. Trinity School. 538 Elsie Warburton, 3, Bostock-street, War- ringtbn; twelve years of age. Attends Holy Trinity School. 539 Edith Buck, 11, Walker-street Warring- ton; aged twelve. Edith has soon got to work. Entering the competition in the right spirit, she speaks of Christmas merriment.</p>
        <p>" But," she asks, " what about ' Tommy ' in the trenches, and 'Jack' on the unfathomed sea? What about their Christmas? We hope that they, who are doing so much for us, may have a bright, happy Christmas. .By making them happy we will have happier, brighter and better Christmases than we would otherwise have had."</p>
        <p>I have had other letters, but that, I think, is a very good Christmas Budget. Hoping that you will all have a ,Happy Christmas and that we shall have a happier one next year.</p>
        <p>Your loving</p>
        <p>UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>SANTA CLAUS AIN'T GOIN' TO COME.</p>
        <p>By James J. Montague.</p>
        <p>My mother Ays that Santa ClEtna ain't goin'</p>
        <p>come this year. She says te prob'ly won't have time to find us,</p>
        <p>- - way down *here. And when she tells nie that, I see there's some- thing in her eyes, But it ain't tears, I'm sure of that, my mother . never cries.</p>
        <p>One time we used to live uptown, and every Christmas Day Old Santa Claus would come around, a-drivin' in his sleigh; And leave us lots o' toys an' things—my father was here then— But now I'm pretty awful sure he'll never come again.</p>
        <p>I s'pose I hadn't ought to care, but still I need a drum, And shoes. and lots an' lots o' things, and gee! I wisht he'd come. But mother says there ain't no chanst, he'll never find the way, An' Christmas won't be differunt from any other day.</p>
        <p>I'll just get up an' drink my tea an' eat my hunk of bread, An' watch my mother go to work an' wish that I was dead, An' tend the baby all day long: That kid's in luck because He can't remember back to when we had Santa Claus.</p>
        <p>There is nothing o kingl7 as kindness, an nothing so royal as truth.—Alico Cary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Useful Information In Popular Language.</p>
        <p>Value of Hot and Cold Water. The habit of drinking hot water is sound. Taken at meals instead of .a cold drink it some- times helps digestion; taken an hour or so be- fore a meal it cleanses the stomach; ,it is especially beneficial in the early morning before breakfast. An account is given of a party of travellers who journeyed across the cold parts of North America, and took amongst their scanty baggage a spirit lamp for heating water; when they returned they were loud in their praises of hot water, which, they said, in addition to warmth, gave them great strength and endurance. Japanese soldiers are said to drink a great deal of hot water on the march. Cold water, if not taken in too large a quantity, has also very often a healthy effect on the digestive organs. Just as a cold bath, by the " shock " it produces, calls up a reaction from the heart, producing increased circulation and warmth, so will cold water taken internally administer it wholesome tonic to the ,gastric glands Of the stomach after a meal.</p>
        <p>Chinese and Their Doctors. Many people wonder how it is that the Chinese can live in houses where the supply of air is ,so very limited add- still remain among the most healthy nations of the earth. One explanation is that their food is always Simple, well cooked and clean. ,Potatoes, meats and rice are all boiled together. The -mixtur3 is put in small bowls and eaten with chopsticks. The chopsticks do not hold very much at a time, and, Consequently, they eat slowly. They hardly ever drink water, but consume great quantities of tea, both\ hot and cold.</p>
        <p>Nothing seems to excite the Chinese. They don't know what it is to be nervous. They can work or write, stand or-sit, for a whole day without getting nervous. The Chinese seem to be able to stand any amount of physical exer- tion. ' They can fall asleep anywhere, among the roar of machinery, the crying of children, in any position, either on the ground, bed or chair.</p>
        <p>The Chinese pay, the doctor only when they are well, instead of paying him when they are sick, as we do in this country. For instance, if the Emperor becomes ill, the salary of his physicians ceases until he is well again. The 'result is that the Emperor is so well looked after as to be the healthiest man on the planet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
