      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Cljurth grims.</head>
        <p>Cljurth grims.</p>
        <p>'t‘ MC PEOPLE ATTEND CIIIRCIL</p>
        <p>"Better Than A Concert Hall Or Fashionable Showroom."</p>
        <p>'Why do people attend church?</p>
        <p>Here is a question- to which many answers may be given, says the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, in the December number of the Buttonwood Parish Magazine, and he Proceeds to give a few of the answers.</p>
        <p>Church-going motives, he says, are many; all are not of a high order too many are of the vulgar type. Some frequent God's House from the motive of respectability; some from custom and habit; some for advertisement and business ends; some.to show off their fine clothes and nodding plumes; some to meet their friends; some to gossip and talk slander; some to enjoy the singing; some to worship a favourite preacher; some to enter into .the Presence, to pray and worship in spirit and in truth: More than, we think, perhaps, are actuated by the last-named motive, a- charitable supposition anyhow. Yet it is pretty certain the worldly church-going motives far exceed those spirit- ually prompted. Religion of late years in this country, at least until the bitter, harassing experience of the past year came to us, was mainly worldly-motived ; the church was hypnotised by the Gospel of worldliness, by the life of materialism.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>Good Effect of a Rude Awakening.</head>
        <p>Good Effect of a Rude Awakening.</p>
        <p>We have had a rude awakening, yet not too rude to wholly and completely break the spell under which we lay. To this end a violent: shock was necessary, and we have had it. Men,' women—and children, too—in future will re- pair to God's House from higher, worthier motives than those which, for so long a time, have influenced' and hypnotised the majority. The House of God is now justifying itself as something better than a concert hall or fash- ionable showroom; something superior to a class or lecture room; as the meeting-place be- tween God and man. Already a great change for the better may be noticed. In numbers, church-goers may not have increased, but those who do attend God's Housitwon the Lord's Day apparently bring with them a more reverent and thoughtful spirit, a belief, a firm convic- tion which Cannot be hid that they are about to enter into the more immediate Presence of God, and that Christ, the Lord, is there to be sought and found; that He is there to meet them, spiritually to manifest to all who would worship Him in spirit and in truth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>" AIESSIAII."</head>
        <p>THE " AIESSIAII."</p>
        <p>Services At Leh-street Primitive Methodist Church.</p>
        <p>A very pleasing performance of Handers " Messiah " was given on Sunday at the Legh- street Primitive Methodist Church, Warring- ton, by the 'choir, supported by a number of principals:- Though repeated every year, the "Messiah" seems to lose none of its freshness, and the rendition on Sunday was carried out with vigour. The first part was taken in the afternoon and the second at the evening ser- vice. A number of wounded soldiers from Raddon Court, in charge of Miss M. Broadbent. were present at each, and they enjoyed the music greatly. The principals, who acquitted themselves well, were Miss Maud Jcmes, Miss J. Woodland, Miss H. Spruce, Mrs. Worthing- ton, and Mrs.. A. E. Hoyle; Mr. T. .Goostrey, Mr. E. Jr. Pearce, Mr. J. Phillips and Mr. J. Dakin. Mr. F. H. Banner presided at the. organ and contributed in a large measure- to the success of the services. The choir was under the very able conductorship of Mr. A. Spruce.- It is interesting to note that Miss Jessie Woodland, one of the sopranos, was a former member of the choir, but for the past twelve months she has been engaged as a Red Cross nurse at Whitchurch Military Hospital, near Cardiff.</p>
        <p>The morning service was taken by Mr. Lund), and the afternoon and evening services wer? presided over by Mr. B. T. Parkes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>SAVE THE CHILD.</head>
        <p>SAVE THE CHILD.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Hear Of Dr.</p>
        <p>Barnardo's Work.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. G. Gascoigne, of Manchester, pre- sided over a large meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. on Sunday, when Mr. C. H. Davies, the Manchester representative of Dr. Bsrnardo's Homes, spoke upon the importance of saving child-life-for the nation.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he had no sympathy with the pessimist newspapers. He received cheer and comfort from the Bible, for righteousness wonld prevail. A minister had told him that he had seen at the Front the grave of a brave soldier who had given his life for King and country. Upon the grave Is as a small white cross with the words " I believe" written thereon. That should prove that Germany would not win this terrible war. She could not. Righteousness could and 'would prevail. To do good was to do right. Mr. C. If. Davies thanked the Chairman for all be had done for the Homes in Manchester and the surrounding districts. He had been a medium *hereby a special interest had been taken in the Homes by many of the societies. Warrington had done well for the Homes in the days' gone by, and the Wycliffe P.S.A. had done its 'share, for which he. thanked them. Democracy after the war would ne in evidence. Our first duty after the war would be to see to the child-life. Every child belonged to God and each should have a chance. The children were trained for future usefulness. To save the adult was -good, but to save the child was far better. Dr. Barnardo believed in God, and had said: "It is my duty to save the children." The Government did not support the Homes in any way, and yet 81,000 children had passed through them. During the last year 1,600 had been admitted, and at the present Moment there were in the Homes. 7,000 ehildre,n. ,There were no fewer than 1,600 old boys fighting for King and country. Fathers, whose wives were dead, had, when leaving for the war, left their boys in the Homes, knowing full well that they would be cared for. Many of the lads had done well and had shown an heroic Spirit, both in the Army and Navy. No destitute child was refused admittance, the question of faith not standing in their .way in the 'least. They .had given help before and he was sure they would do it again. The funds of the Homes were lower and the needs greater. After the war they would need More.</p>
        <p>The Honorary Secretary (Mr. B. Farrar) said that Private Yeates and Sapper Pye had sent a message of greeting to the brethren. Sapper Pye had been wounded and was in hospital. • During the service Mr. Arthur Davies .sang very effectively "Honour and Arms," Mr. W. H. Payton presiding at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>FINE WAR RECORD.</head>
        <p>FINE WAR RECORD.</p>
        <p>The local Salvation Army Corps had a great day last Sunday, on the occasion of a visit of Commissioner Iliggins, the Territorial leader of the Salvation Army's work in the United King- dom. By the kind permission of the Chairman and Directors, the Picturedrome was used for the three meetings, and excellent congregations assembled.</p>
        <p>The Commissioner's address ,at the morning service was based on the words "Stretch forth thine hand," spoken by Christ to the man with the withered hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>NEED FOR GOOD PEOPLE'S HELP.</head>
        <p>THE NEED FOR GOOD PEOPLE'S HELP.</p>
        <p>His Worship the Mayor, (Alderman Peter Peacock) presided over the afternoon meeting, when the Picturedrome was well filled. His Worship was supported by the Rev. Canon Willis, Mr. and Mrs. T. Locker, Brigadier Langdon, Brigadier Read, of Liverpool; and Major Aspinall, of London. In the name of Warrington, the Mayor gave the Commissioner a, very hearty welcome. He believed it was the first time Warrington had been honoured by this distinguished visitor, whose wide knowledge of the world and human experience should be of great value. lie thought it was a time when all religious people should be more closely bound together. We were all thinking of the friends far away,- and full of anxious concern for them. It was a time of trial and sorrow; never were the ministrations of good people more urgently needed than now.</p>
        <p>SALVATION ARMY IN PEACE AND WAR.</p>
        <p>The Commissioner, who was very heartily greeted, referring to the atteAting campaign, be-. gan by saying he hoped all who were undecided would get it over and decide to do their duty to their King and country. He then spoke of his topic, "The Salvation Army in Peace and War." The founding of the 'Salvation Army by the late General Win. Booth was brought about by him looking for the greatest need, and then dedicating himself, his wife and chil- dren to the cause of the poor and needy, Um outcast and suffering. Describing the progress of the Army's- work, he said it might be divided into three periods-- first, persecution; second, the period of mis- representation; third, the period of apprecia- tion. He gave striking illustrations wider each head, gathered from his world-wide experience.</p>
        <p>Would Have Blushed For Shame. Referring to fhe war, he said the Salvation Army had hoped for better things. --The Army was international in its character and govern- ment, and had laboured hard to promote tho unity of the nations of the world; consequently when war broke out it .wasn bitter disappoint- ment to the Salvation Army; but; reviewing the crisis of August, 1914, and knowing as he did by experience what the name of Great Britain stood for in countries far removed, he did not think the Government could have done other than they did. He would have blushed for shame if his Motherland had been untrue to her pledges to safeguard the small nations of Europe. Noble Salvation Army Women In Belgium. Commissioner Higgins proceeded to speak of the -value of the Army's international organisa- tion, and told how When the Marines who ,were sent to assist in the defence of Antwerp were interned in Holland the officer of the nearest corps obtained permission to hold -a meeting with them, .armed with song sheets on which he had had printed some of the grand old hymns. He held his meeting, but noticed. many Of the men writing on the sheets. Afterwards these *ere given to him and he found at the close he had the addresses of loved ones at home tc.; whom he had promised to write, about 300 in all. Not a woman officer had left her post in Belgium and France, even in the thick of the warfare. In some corps in this country practically all the band had enlisted. At one Place only the drummer is left. The Army have now 12 motor ambulances in France. Canadian Salvationists have sent four to Russia, and from home they have sent four more to Serbia. Twenty women officers visit the British hospitals in Trance. Over 20,000 of the Salvation Army young men are with the Colours, and in .over 120 camps, huts had been provided for rest, refreshment and recreation. The Rev. Canon Willis proposed a -vote of thanks to the Mayor for presiding, Brigadier Langdon seconded, and it was heartily accorded. The meeting closed by singing "God Bless Our Native Land."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>WAGES OF SIN.</head>
        <p>THE WAGES OF SIN.</p>
        <p>9 For Children Cutting their Teeth. Prevent Convulsions. Are Cooling and Soothing. Contain nothing injurious to a tender babe.</p>
        <p>Sold Everywhere in Stamped Box, is. 3d. and 35., with Fall' Micelion,.</p>
        <p>Safe D -.,• ' Easy . • e ; Teething ! ..,, , . . L ' 9 .'.- 1 ~ Teething! (.410ne hare the Trace Mark: "A BABY IN A Crotnr.r.") rt ' Or .% fl'in, i. , \</p>
        <p>READ FENNINGS' EVERY MOTHER'S BOOK. It contains Valuable Hints on Feeding, e Teething, Weaning, Sleeping, etc. Ask your Chemist or Grocer for a FREE copy, or one will be sent, POST FREE, on application to A.L.FRED FENNINGS, Cowes, hie of Wighz.</p>
        <p>There was a fine congregation at night. Thn Commissioner spoke on the text, "The wages of sin is death, but the gift Of God is eternal life." Major Aspinall, who is a native of War- rington, and for twelve months was in charge of the Salvation ,Army Ambulance, Unit in France, was also present, and sang very effectively.</p>
        <p>THE " Knit IT" PROPOSALS.</p>
        <p>IVarringlon Society Protests Against Suggested New Arrangement.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Branch of the English Church -tJnion was held on Tuesday evening at the Patten Hall, the Rev. W. J. Stanton (president), being in the chair. The Rev. E. A. Glenday (vicar of Holy Trinity Church, Bury) spoke on "Kikuyu." The Chairman said that this question of the Kikuyu proposals greatly concerned the Church of England, and also the whole of the Holy Catholic Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. A. Glenday spoke of the sugges- tion which was made at the Kikuyu conference to bring about a reunion of native Christians and i;ainongst other things, to allow " schis- unities " and " heretics " to receive the sacra- ment in the Church of England and to -preach in its pulpits. He protested that 'these two suggestions were contrary to. the \Rubric in the Book of Common Prayer, which stated that no one should be allowed to -receive Boly Com- munion. unless be or she had been Confirmed, or desired to be confirmed in the Church of Eng- land. The speaker moved a resolution ~which declared "That to allow members of separatist bodies to communicate at our altars without for Mal, reconciliation is ,a grave offence against the discipline of the Church, likely to form a hindrance to the reunion of Christendom and to accentuate existing differences among our- selves."</p>
        <p>The resolution was seconded by the Rev. W. J. Stanton, and carried unanimously. _ _</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was accorded the speaker, on the motion of the Rev. J. H. G. Trolling- worth, seconded by Mr. W. H. Stansfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
