      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Life's Little Worries.</p>
        <p>For the journalist one of These has always been, is now, and I. fear ever shall be—but let me indicate by my latest instance. Some of my friends in the legal profession seem in- clined to make merry over last week's spelling of serjeant-at-law. Ruskin, once wrote in the margin of a besmirched page of a book he had taken Up, ", I did not make this blot." Neither did I spell law-serjeant with a " But I did revise the proof-sheets, and' so, after all my virtUous airs, must share the responsi- bility with the linotype operator who " corrected" my spelling.</p>
        <p>Sergeants and Serjeants. Yes indeed, I do not remember the time when I was unabbi to appreciate the differ- ence between a legal serjeant and a military sergeant. Time was when the serjeants-at- law enjoyed the exclusive right and privilege of practising in the Court of Common Pleas— when they were,the only advocates recognised there. In those days, too, that seine class had a monopoly of the judgeships; a lawyer elevated to the Bench must either already have been a serjeant or be formally made one before proceeding to thd duties of the higher office and dignity. It is just 40 years ago since fhe removal of that obligation. The Judicature Act of 1875 included that among many changes, and serjeants-at-law were no longer appointed. A Dickens' Character.</p>
        <p>That was why I referred to the late Judge Tindal Atkinson as one of the last to wear a serjeant' s distinguishing coif. Another lawyer of the same period and rank was the late Mr. Bompas, Q.C. (he died during the reign of Queen Victoria). I never saw that gentleman, but I used to attend Burnley County Court when His Honour Judge Bop.- pas, who was his son, presided there, and it was generally understood that the former was the original of the famous caricature in "Pickwick "—Serjeant Buzfuz. A Sad Christmas.</p>
        <p>This is being gloomily anticipated by many. Of course, we all know what they mean when they talk thus, though I would not harrow the feelings of any by recalling facts which those now referred to as disposed to pessimism have in mind when they deprecate the observance of the festive season on anything like the old, familiar lines; at the same time I think it is possible to show, unto such a more excellent way than the one they evidently contemplate —nay, it has already been indicated by at least one other contributor to " The Ex- aminer." It is -a timely reminder that our heroic and unconquerable sailors and soldiers are certain to make as much of Christmas Day as circumstances will allow, and are naturally expecting to be remembered to that end by their friends at home. Moreover, the little ones' stockings must be filled—this time Ivith English toys instead of German, and children of larger growth may well go on mak- ing suitable presents to each other (for guid- ance respecting which interested readers should keep an eye on trado notices in this paper and on the establishment windows of the advertising shopkeepers). Finally, no pdor local family, who have suffered keen bereavement and irreparable loss through sonic) 'male-member having made the patriot's supreme sacrifice should be overlooked next week-end especially. Thus there should' re- main no time for pratless brooding over the irrevocable.</p>
        <p>'Kissing Under the Mistletoe. The reference to this by last week's writer reminded me of an article by a learned Yale professor whose profound ethnological studies and investigation into the .social customs of bygone ages disclose the fact that kissing was discovered in India. He has unearthed an epic poem of ancient India which treats of love and which tells of the first kiss. It was, needless to say, the invention of lovely woman. The poet says "she laid her mouth to my mouth and made a noise which gave me pleas- ure." There (comments the "Washington Post," this copy of which I have had in my possession some years) we have it complete ! the Culthination of long ages of yarning for something undefined and unrealised, given life by the genius of a woman inspired by love, and coming into being like Minerva from the head of Jove, full grown from her lips. Other things born into the world have been added to and improved on in the course of time, or have grown stale and have been 'flung aside. The kiss remains as alit and evanescent, as im- palpable and elusive, as blissful .and ineffable, as precious to man as was the first one those long ages ago, when the Indian maiden laid her mouth 'to her lover's, and he responded with a thrill of pleasure to the new-born caress.,</p>
        <p>When Christmas was under Ban. May I amplify the bit of information given under this heading in the last issue? We of the twentieth century can have very little sympathy with the Puritanism which pro- hibited the observance of Christmas Day. The stern suppression of the Christmas spirit as " understanded of the people" at the period indicated seems at the present time strange and even incredible. It is true that, prior to the Cromwellian protectorate, there had been at least one " still Christmas." The Christmas of 1528, when " bluff King Hal"— whose Christmas festivities were generally on lavish scale—was so ill as to cause national anxiety, was a silent one; there were no carols, bells, or merry-makings. ' The Christ- mas preceding the much-married king's union with Anne Boleyn was likewise mirthless; but • it was far otherwise under Edward VI., Elizabeth, James 1., and Charles I. On the outbreak of the Civil War, however, feelings which connected any observance of Christmas . Day with the spirit of Popery (although that such observations fell into desuetude during the reign of Queen . Mary is undeniable) were fostered. One may read in Hervey's "Book of Christmas" how by 1647 this oppo- sition had so far prevailed that parish officers were subjected to penalties for encouraging the decoration of churches and permitting divine service to be performed on Christmas morning; in fact, it was in that year that the • festival itself was formally abolished by the two branches of the Legislature. Proclama- tion was made by town crier "that Christmas . Day and all other superstitious festivals should be put down "—no carols, no yule fires, , no bells, no mince-pies even, although these • latter originally had religious significance, as I could easily show if I had space. "No man of the most rigid virtue," observed the " Tat- ler " in Addison's time, "gives offence in CXCCS6 of plum pudding or plum porridge," and the sentiment will be heartily echoed by many, most of all by our soldiers in the field; hut to the Puritans during the* Common- wealth it was anathema—they swept it away, along with the time-honoured Lord of Mis- rule, with his mumniing, Morris (lancing, and hilarious following, concerning which things Ar. Arthur Mounfield is by far the better authority. Suffice it that reaction followed the Restoration, and, without seeking to .4excuse or palliate the undoubted excesses into which overjoyed Loyalists were betrayed, one need not-affect surprise at Sir Walter Scott's lines beginning "England was merry Eng- land when Old Christmas brought his sports again.", After all, religious bigotry and in- tolerance. was by no means confined to any particular section or party. Nowadays,. hap- pily,_each one is at liberty to.keep aristmas rationally. As someone has said, it is good to be a child again, and that is what Christ- mas amounts to.</p>
        <p>'The blessing of a house is piety. The honour cif a house is hospitality. The ornament of a 'house is cleanliness. The happiness of a house' ts content.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 18, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 18, 1915.</p>
        <p>NATIONALISM.</p>
        <p>Will it he Submerged in a Cosmopolitan</p>
        <p>Humanitarianism?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>GERMAN " SLAVES " TO POWER.</head>
        <p>GERMAN " SLAVES " TO POWER.</p>
        <p>PAr. C. E. Parker's Address To The Literary and Philosophical Society.</p>
        <p>The relationship between nations in the light ot the war was the subject of an interesting paper read by Mr. C. E. Parker, at a meeting of the Warrington Literary and Philosophical Society on Monday evening. The title of Mr. Parker's papeir was " Nationalism, Internationalism, and Cosmopolitanism." There was a good attend- ance of members, the Rev. 11. V. l'igot (presi- dent), being in the chair. Mr. Parker said</p>
        <p>The issues of the hour have given to Nationalism and Internationalisjn a position of great importance. The European conflict has brought to the front vital questions pro- foundly affecting national life and develop- ment.</p>
        <p>The conception of nationality as the im- portant factor in the life of a people is looked upon by some as a passing phase in the progress of civilisation. It is contended that through it humanity is moving forward towardg, cosmo- politanism as its ultimate goal, a goal in which nationalism will be submerged, and almost for- gotten in a larger humanitarianism, in which the things Which now divide nations, such as location, language, race affinity, and religious belief, will only operate in a ,secondary degree. Mazinni tells us that we may have to fight in the interests of the narrower patriotism until we have reached what is more universal. It is claimed that new divisions Etre arising, forming affinities, the result of different concep- tions of life, which have no relation to nationality, that the force which now, and still more in the future, will draw men together will be material, intellectual, sociological and spiritual, rather than geographium I and racial. Hence it is contended that natio:a ality, and its expressions in patriotism, are in -process of dis- integration, and these am o to become so attenu- ated as to be finally lost in a wider nationalism, or rather humanitarianism.</p>
        <p>It must be acknowledged that new bonds of union, and at the same time new lines of cleavage, have of late years beenfkleveloping, at least were doing until the, coming of the war. The Trade Unionist recognised hi affinity with his fellow Unionist in other countries, involving common action in trade disputes and mutual support 'in trade conflicts. The capi- talists were forming intermational ar- rangements, social reformers were meet- ing and conferring with those like- minded in other countries. Thus affinities were being established by different grcupv, which recognised 'no national division's. The German writer of that, remarkable book, " J'Aconse " (all the more remarkable because -It is written by a German), expresses the same thought:— "As geological strata unit veins of iron and mineral are, not dirt-Noted to the surface according to the dividkng lines between properties, but underneath these boundaries pass from one property to another, so the strata of modern human society are not broken up by territorial frontiers, but pass from one county to another- Our horizontal interdependence has taken the place of the vertical line of division. And if there is only: one truly organised idternational, there exist beside it a hundred others unorganised, held together by equally firm internal bonds. Of such are the internationals of trade, of in- dustry, of the technical and moral sciences. and of literature and art, all of which con- stitute the indestructible spiritual bond con- necting the nations."</p>
        <p>T. Batty, an authority on international law, writes :</p>
        <p>"All over the world society is organising itself by strata. W&amp; are confronted, with the coming conditions of affairs in which the force of nationality will he distinctly inferior to the force of class cohesion."</p>
        <p>Norman Angell has given expression to the same idea when he says:— " There is closer community of interest in a vital matter between Lancashire anti Louisiana than between Lancashire and, say, the Orkneys, part of the same State . . . . A Professor at Oxford will have closer com- munity of, feeling with a member of the French Academy than with, say, a White- chapel publican."</p>
        <p>No one will deny the general truth of Mb identical thought expressed by these three writers, but we are disposed to think they all give too much relative emphasis and de not appear to realise the strength of national bonds. I would especially demur to what Norman Angell goes on to say : " In a thousand respects association cut-; across State boundaries, which are purely con- ventional, and render the biological divisions of mankind into independent and warring States a scientific ineptitude. . . State divisions are coming more and more to approximate to mere_ administrative divisions, leaving free scope within them, or across them, for the development of stronger bonils of interest." These conclusions rest upon the assumption that the affinities referred to are more potent, and more enduring than these which spring from national sentiment and racial kinship -</p>
        <p>NATIONALISM PROVES ITS STRENGTH. The European war has proved conclustvely that, whatever be the future of nationalism, fer the present it has; developed .a strength iv hich must have surprised these writers. Patriotism, born of nationalism, instead of. breaking tinder the strain has proved for the time being strong enough to sever, or at any rate relax all other bonds. I do not contend that Norman Angell, and those who think with him, are altogether wrong, but Undoubtedly they exaggerate the effect' of 'these new ties, or rather fail to realise the strength of nationality which only required the appropriate condition:4 to make it active. These' conditions have been supplied by the present war. At its beginning it was believed that, we were fighting only the ruling caste in Germany-. • Mr. Lloyd George, in an early speech saicit---" We are not fighting the German people; the German people are just as much under the heel of this German Prussian caste, and more so thank God, than any other nation in Europe. It will be a day of rejoicing for the German peasant, artisan and trader when the military caste is broken." We now know this idea to be false, and that we are fighting a united German people. Whatever class was responsible for the outbreak of war it has become to the Germans a patriotic war, and this patriotism has practically obliterated all other divisions and made. the causes of the war a secondary con- sideration. If it were possible to convince the German people that Germany was in the wrong it would not now make any material difference in the conduct of the war. Take the German Socialists, whom we knew to be opposed to the arbitrary authority of the military ruling caste of Germany. With the exception of a very small minority who have uttered their protest the great majority are silent, and appear determined that Germany Shall conquer. Mr. Ben Tillett recently said : I have. given years to organise German *orkxnen to raise their standard of living, and</p>
        <p>they promised me that should the Kaiser and the Junkers force war they would make a general strike for us. But when it came to it the Germans, like the slaves they are, took up the rifle against us."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>WHEN LATENT PATRIOTISM FLAMES OUT.</head>
        <p>WHEN LATENT PATRIOTISM FLAMES OUT.</p>
        <p>But is not Ben Tilleiot somewhat unfair io the German workers, and has he not made the same mistake as Norman Angell, that is he under- rates the power of patriotism which. although latent in times of industrial strife, becomes a hot flame, when as they think their Fatherland is endangered In the intellectual class we have Doctor Rudolf Eucken, the great philosopher, who says in writing to a friend:—" Certainly never in its history was Germany so united and great. Our two sons have gone to the war." Now this Doctor Thicken is deservedly honoured as ono of the world's greatest spiritual teachers, has occupied a position of great authority in a sphere which recognises no national boundaries. We would have thought that such an one, whose affinity and interests in high spiritual spheres were world-wide, would not have descended from his Mei estate, and have signed the manifesto of Professors and Theologians in which the facts are all distorted, England unjustly abused, and the situation presented as we might expect it to be from the standpoint of the ignorant German Junker, or the coarse Prussian military officer. What is true of Doctor Eucken is true of almost all the intellectually and spiritually minded of Germany. Thus we see in two representative groups the Socialists and intellectuals (who in their creed ignored national limitations) when their country goes to war their spiritual and economic ideals shrivel up like tow in the hot fire of their patriotism. So we find in Germany, Socialists and capitalists, junkers and their tenants, professors and coal heaverg, all fighting side by side because they are Germans, all de- fending the same trench for what they deem to be the integrity, continued existence and extension of their nation. And so with us, all are in the fight, "Duke's son, cook's son, son of the millionaire." Patriotism has for the time being quenched all other fires.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>NATIONALITY DEFINED.</head>
        <p>NATIONALITY DEFINED.</p>
        <p>But the question may be asked is not Norman Angell in the main right, and only wrong in his dates, i.e., that the stronger bonds, closer community of interests of which he speaks will, in the future, if not now, render, as he puts it, "the biological divisions of mankind into in- dependent and warring states a scientific inepti- tude?" Is not this thought one which, like all progressive ideals, must ever be in advance of what has been reached?‘ To answer this ques- tion it is necessary to define clearly what constitutes nationality. It is a composite pro- duct, and many factors and forces go to its making. We, will only here refer to three of the most important. First: A Common Territory, which we variously designate as the Fatherland, Motherland, Homeland. The Wizard of the North gives apt expression to the • affection for the homeland when he sings:— Breathes there the man, with soul so dead, Who never to himself -bath said. This is my own, my native land! Whose heart hath ne'er within him buried, As home his footsteps he bath turti'd, From wandering on a foreign 'strand! And again:</p>
        <p>0 Caledonia! stern and wild, Meet nurse for a poetic child! Land of brown heath and shaggy wood, Land of the mountain and the flood. Land of my sires! what mortal hand Can e'er untie the filial band, That knits me to the rugged strand!</p>
        <p>The homeland is the hoMe of our childhood; thoughts of its mountain and its flood, its hills and its dales, all twine themselves round our hearts. It is the land hallowed by all f.ha t is most precious and real in our life. It is the place where our homes are; it contains the graveyards where our loved ones sleep, and it forms the material structure out of which our lives are built.</p>
        <p>Second': Language.—The second important ele- ment in nationality is Language. As we call our land our Motherland, so we call our langume our mother-tongue. It is. the language that our fathers spoke, the language of Milton awl Shake- speare, the language of the Bible the " Pil- grim's Progress." It is the chief medium in which we embody our thoughts and the thoughts of our race. Just as we get attached to material things, be it an old armehair, or a father's watch, so the speech which our forebears u3eil becomes 'dear to us and binds together all 1, ho use it. It matters not that our language is the result of the union of many tongues. Jr.Ft as our race is formed by the , fusion .of many peoples, so the individuality of many tongues has been merged in our English language, which has now an individuality and character all its own, and has thus become one of the factors preserving the distinctive features of our race and nationality.</p>
        <p>Third: Race.—The third and most important factor in nationality is Race. Our country is not alone ours, but is the land of our sires, and so doubly ours. A continuity is given, to our national life by its past. Unconsciously in our form, actions, habits, and thoughts we express our past, we are its sum total. The mental differ- ences manifest in the German and tip English people in the present conflict illustrate, this. These differences are in a minor degree the re- sult of recent national developments. The main causes spring from roots which go back into the distant past of the two races. A marked char- acteristic of the Saxon people (of whom our Eng- lish race is composed probably in the proportion of about seven-eighths) was individualism, and hence they were only, loosely held together by what we would now call the State. The family was the primary unit. Each man, with his household, stood in his own right upon his own land. The Saxons were not much concerned about forms of Government as long as their individual and family rights were respected. They were a peaceable people, and because of their individualism were often lacking in cohe- sion when danger threatened the community. Both in the case of the Danish and Norman in- vasions the Saxons lacked the unity necessary to conquer by arms. They paid to the Danes the famous tax of the Dane-geld, because they reasoned that the tax would cost them less than war. They could fight, and fight well, hut only if the cause were worth the trouble, not other- wise, they did noT fight for fighting Sake. From a military point of view at first the• Danes con- quered the Saxons, but in reality the Saxon? conquered the Danes by finally absorbing the bulk of the invaders.</p>
        <p>The Norman conquest was in result similar, although from a military and political point of view it was more complete. For a time the Normans sought to impress their form of civilisation upon the Saxons, and superficially were successful. The Norman barons, in their revolt against the autocracy of their king, bad to adopt the Saxon principle of respect or indi- vidual rights, and depend oxi Saxon support to force Magna Charta from King John. By a long and stubborn effort, of which the great Charta was only an episode, the Saxon people regained their position of individual ,independ- ence, checked the centralising tendency of government, assumed the direction of their own</p>
        <p>destinies, and re-established solf-government practically under the old forms and under the old Saxon laws.</p>
        <p>GERMANY A CONTRAST: THE STATE DEIFIED.</p>
        <p>Now contrast this with the origin and develop- ment of the German people. The Germans sprang in the main from races patriarchal and communistic in their mode of life., This feature naturally developed in aftertime into absolute monarchial government, /which strengthened itself with the idea of the divine right of kings, which archaic fiction survives to-day in the be- lief and utterances of William of Germany. The only tribes which went to the making of the German people, and the German Empire, who were individualistic in their habits, were the Saxons who remained on the Continent at the</p>
        <p>time of the Saxon invasion of England, and the Franks, who were split off from the portion that occupied France. No doubt both the Saxons and the Franks were allied to the Gothic or Germanic .races which, at the time of the great migration, journeyed Westward from the shore of the Black Sea and Northern India. But these two tribes, for reasons we can- not now consider, had developed in a direction other than the peoples to which they were originally allied, that is along the individual- istic line as opposed to what may be termed the communistic or clan line. In the making of the Germanic jieople we have first the patriarch, then the chief, then in later times the King or Emperor, who in turn ruled the family, clan, or nation. Thus tae head of the State became the repository of all power and authority, from which there was no appeal, and of whom it would be blasphemy to speak ill Passing over the long confused history of the moulding of the German people and coming to the time of Frederick, called the Great, ot Prussia, we find embodied in the Contrakt Social a code of laws, in the introdtiction to which are the following significant words:— " The head of the State to which is entrustea the duty of securing public welfare, which is the sole aim in•society, is authorised to direct and control all the acts of individuals towarls this end,"</p>
        <p>Here we have learly set forth the guidirg belief not only of Frederick, but Of the German people of his day, as it is of William and the Germans of to-day. It is the old patriarchal idea of the chief directing, and controlling, all the acts of the individuals forming the group of which he is the head. The teachings of the modern Treitschke, which emphasise and almost deify the State's authority, are not in any way original, they spring out of Germany's past, of which Treitschke is one of the products. Here then we have the fundamental difference between</p>
        <p>the nationality of England and Germany, and incidentally the explanation of the greater mechanical solidarity of Germany. In Ger- many the power of the State being absolute, the Emperor as the State representative can do no wrong, or if lie does wrong, it is no longer wrong. The idolatry of the State has reached its logical issue in the elevation of force to the position of the sovereign principle in national life. This enthronement of force involves the dethronement of right, and from this stand- point the modern Bernha rdi, echoing Treitschke, is logical when he says "Might is ever the supreme right, and the dispute of what is right is decided by the arbitrament of war. War gives a biological just decision since its decisions rest on the very nature of things." Training for war naturally becomes the essence of citizenship to the obscuring of all higher conceptions. On the other hand in Eng- land the power of the State is strictly limited, and the rights of. individual, as in early times, demanded and ,respected. Hence many things which in Germany are ordered and regulated by the State are here left to individual volun- tary initiative. The method of attack in the present war furnishes us vdith a practical illus- tration of the result of this racial difference. The Germans attack in massed formation; with their training and temperament they could scarce successfully do otherwise; the English at- tack in extended formation beeause they can rely on personal initiative, self-direction and courage. The German system, while it con- .</p>
        <p>tributes towards discipline and unity of effort, tends to produce a machine like class of mind in its units. The individual's subservience to the State has given Germany an enormous advantage, and made her task of organising all her ?ones comparatively easy. We on our side have suffered from an absence of discipline and organisation. Our individualism has in all directions made our task much heavier. The natural attitude of a Britisher is not to accept, but to challenge every act of the State. to stand at guard over his individual rights. He wants to know the why of every new departure, and forms and expresses his own opinion as to its wisdom. hut on the other hand we have some advantages which spring out of the initiative arising from this individualism. It is outside our present purpose to consider which of these national characteristics is more valuable in war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>RIGHT TO LIVE AS NATIONS.</head>
        <p>THE RIGHT TO LIVE AS NATIONS.</p>
        <p>Our main object is to show that these diffe.rendes arise from causes deeply embedded in the past, they are racial, fundamental and I have named three of the im- yortant abiding elements which go to /the making of nationality, location, language and race, and illustrated how Eng- land , and Germany are differentiated by their historically determined individuality. I con- tend that the solidarity arising out of these determining characteristics will outlast all affinities formed through other .associations, be- cause they are stronger and go deeper. The right to live as nations is one of ihie most virile of all sentiments, and there is a vitality in- herent in nationality that nothing can crush. Think of- Poland, Italy, Greece, humiliated, submerged and conquered, every effort made by the conquerors to suppress the national life,. habits' and language, but all of no avail. Think of Ireland, where for centuries unimaginative Saxon England has vainly sought to crush national. aspirations. We have tried force and kindness, bribes and coercion; all have failed to induce the Irish to adopt what we consider our more enlightened English culture. She still holds as passionately as ever to her national self- consciousness.</p>
        <p>When we consider the strength and endurance of these ties of nationality we are astonished that .an acute thinker like Norman Angell should say that "State boundaries are purely conventional," and that "they render the biological divisions of mankind into independent States a scientific ineptitude."</p>
        <p>In fairness to Norman Angell it is only right to note that he uses the word State, while our thoughts are about nations. But he goes on to speak disparagingly of biological divisions, which can only apply to the concept of nations, and can have no reference to States apart from nations. It is upon the biological elements we lay special stren in the making of a nation. A. State and a nation are generally, but not always synonymous. A State. may not be a nation, and a nation may not be a State. Austria.</p>
        <p>Hungary is a State because it is under one crowned head, but it can scarcely be called fl nation because it is, to use the above term, biologically divided. made up of many races that have not fused. Where any of the three. named constituent elements in the making of a nation are lacking, weakness is the result.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>NATIONAL DANGERS.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL DANGERS.</p>
        <p>In a nation there will always be divisions among its 'component parts, arising out of character, temperament and circumstances. Divisions as far as they arise out of character and temperament' we cannot alter, and we Would not if we could. But the differences arising • out of circumstances call for serious thought. These are to my mind one of the greatest dangers to the life, strength and unity of a nation. •</p>
        <p>The increase of wealth has broken up old relationships. The rich and'the poor live apart and think apart. Wealth and resultant luxury has of late years been rapidly increasing, and has acted as a great divider. Under the in.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <p>fluence of ihe separate grouping of the homes of the different classes, each group has developed its own tastes and pleasures, has formed habits, manners, even a speech which hds made the several parts almost as distinctive as the peoples of different nations. These classes living apart neither know not. always care to know one another as individuals, there is little conscious identity of interest and co- operation. Without this unity of intetest, and this corporate consciousness there can be little national progress. Canon Barnett, of Toynbee Hall, says: "Wealth and not poverty is the national danger." This assertion needs qualification, which is suggested when he goes on to add "It is capital stored in one part of the ship of State which gives the ship the ugly list which prevents its making way on an even keel."</p>
        <p>Against heavy losses of all kinds which we will have to endure as a result of this war, may we not put a gain suggested by the above? Has not the war done much to break down class and wealth distinctions? To illustrate this I can- not do better than quote from the Rev. Doctor Paget's/little book, "lit the Day of Battle ": "Bad news, it may be, has come to the village. The blinds are drawn at the Hall, standing in its stately reserve behind the park gate; and the blind is down at the cottage too; and the whole place is still and quiet as in the presence of the dead. For two lads, the - one from the cottage, the other from the Hall —the squire's son and the labourer's—have given their lives for the country, and have fallen in the war. 'Your lad and mine,' 'My lad and yours.' Who would think at such a moment of the sort of things which distinguished the one from the other? Each has done his best, each has served his country; they have passed together into the presence of God; and the labourer's wife and the squire's are just two women, weeping for their children., refusing to be comfbrted, be- cause they are not."</p>
        <p>And so this war with all its train of suffering may bring us nearer to one another than ever before, may do much to recreate our national spirit and strengthen our national unity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>INTERNATIONALISM: Co-operation More Profitable Than Conflict.</head>
        <p>INTERNATIONALISM: Co-operation More Profitable Than Conflict.</p>
        <p>Internationalism is not opposed to national- ism, it is its natural outcome. A healthy nationalism makes for a healthy inter- nationalism. Both spring from the same roots— love of kind and mutual interest. The man who has strong affections for his own immediate family, is likely to be affectionate and helpful to relatives of his own blood. So the man who is a good patriot will respect the same quality in those of other nations. There is great need for a better understanding of the real character of the relationships between nations. The rivalry of nations, which is the seed bed of "much of the antagonism which be- gets conflict, is largely due to misconceptions of the effect of relative advancement and pros- peiity. Mr. Balfour once gave expression to the following conviction:—</p>
        <p>"I profoundly distrust the current creed— or the creed which is largely current—that the prosperity of one nation is the adversity of another, and that he best serves the industrial prosperity of his own nation who attempts to defiress the industrial prosperity or to snatch a share of the common work of industry from some other nation. I believe this to be utterly untrue."</p>
        <p>Mr. Balfour had only in his thought the question of industrial prosperity, but the truth expressed has a wider application and suggests that in all human affairs co-operation, using the word in its best sense, is more profitable than conflict. The advance along the road of human progress is best achieved not by holding others back, but by helping them forward. This is as true in the economic sphere as it is in the in- tellectual and moral. For the brotherhood of nations to become a reality, a higher power than shortsighted selfish interest must be evoked. The highest sovereignty is not in dynasties, nor in princes, nor in democracies; it is inherent in the sum Of all ideas of truth and justice, it's throne is in heaven, but it must rule the peoples of the earth. This high sovereign power has to a large extent established its authority within the sphere of the internal life of civilised nations. There is an ever-in- creasing tendency in national affairs to deter- mine all questions by eternal prin.- ciples. All laws •of nations are moulded in conformity with these. 'Unity, self- government, national character and. courage, a spirit of brotherhood and mutual service, has made perceptible progress within the limits of nationalism. True there is wrong and injustice in the most advanced States. The sovereignty of right is .acknowledged. It is the gleam upon which our eyes are fixed, but our eyes are yet dim and we cannot always clearly discern whither this light would guide us. Granted it is still necessary as long as evil exists to have force at the back of national laws, but force is surely, if slowly, receding into the background, and be. coming less necessary. Our police force brought into being for the apprehension of evil doers, are now mainly engaged as social guardians and regulators of traffic.</p>
        <p>When we come to internqional affairs, the relations between nation and nation, we do not find the principles of righteousness and justice as yet fully acknowledged.. Force and intrigue have for long disputed with truth and right the possession of the throne, and the present war is evidence of the fact that this struggle is not yet ended, nor justice firmly enthroned. For example Germany (and when I speak of Ger- many I refer to the State as represented by the ruling powers) does not consider that moral obligations have the authority in international as in internal affairs. Other nations are not free of this evil virus, but in Germany this evil doctrine stands naked and unashamed. Bern- hardi says "The end all and be all of the State is power, that the relations of two States justify the employment of hostile methods, cunning, and deception, and that the acts of the State cannot be judged by the standard of individual morality." Were this an isolated opinion wn could ignore it, but judging from the actions of Germany it appears to be accepted as a national belief. Here, then, we have a State deliberately adopting the law of. jungle, and substituting the right of force for the force of right. This conception, we trust, is not genet.. ally held by the people of Germany. It is an immoral philosophy which ought to be ;hurled back to the pit from ivhich it sprang. The difference between the Allies and Germany seems to arise from a difference in their stages of national . development. Ethical progress appears to have been very uneven in Germany, far advanced in. some things, far behind in others. But is the present state of the national mind in Germany only. a backwardness in national development? Anyone who has a knowledge of the movement of Germany's thought in the higher spheres during the last fifty years is compelled to come to the conclusion that there has been actual retrogression, that the moral effort towards the higher has been turned back on itself, and What Professor Bergson feared has come about: there has been produced "a mechanism of spirit instead of the spiritualisa Lion of matter." The Material.pro- gress has outstripped, negatived, and apparently reversed the moral and spiritual advance. We require to go back at least two hundred years of our own history to find a position analogous to the national outlook of Germany. I mean the national outlook at the beginning of the war, for it seems to be changing. This unfortunate country provides us with the most glaring example of the abrogation of moral principles in international affairs, but it would be a mistake to think that Germany is the only sinner. But this in justice can be said : Other countries have found room in their national thought fel' wider and less selfish ideals, have recognised the right of each nation, however small, to follow the line of its own development, and 'to be safe within its national rights even if it has not the. power to enforce them. THE REAL CURE FOR STRIFE.</p>
        <p>If my contention in regard to the control of internal national concerns by ethical principles</p>
        <p>be admitted surely by an extension and appli- cation of the same principles to knternational affairs will a like result be attained. The real cure for strife as well as the need of force in reserve is the overthrow of selfish ambition and greed, and its replacement by right, justice, and the service of humanity. When this comes about, and not until, is permanent inter- national peace possible. This may appear to be a Self-evident truism which does not carry us far, for if all men and nations were just, true, and self-sacrificing, we should have no war. But we do not require a perfect humanity to ensure international peace. What we need is that truth and right should dominate international tela- lions, just as they do internal national affairs. Unless we advance along this lino we cannot hope to attain the end to which the good and wise in all nations longingly aspire. Unless the community of nations so advance, "Stop the War Campaigns," Hague Conventions, proposed international Tribunals, Democratic Controls, and all the rest of it, will prove but short cuts that land us in a morass. Of course, I recog- nise that some sort of mechanism is required in this practical world to give expression to, and embody moral principles. Amongst the many wrongs which we ascribe to Germany is the denial of the sanctity of international law. One weakness of international lalv, as it at present exists (as for instance the decisions of the Hague Convention), is the fact that it is not supported by what lawyers call sanction. • That is, these laws have behind them no power strong enough to enforce them should any of the con- tracting parties break this bond; and how the imperative of sanction is to be established is the most baffling problem that faces the nations. But as I have contended there is something more important than force in this connection; there must accompany and precede any legal inter- national arrangements a moral and mental advance in the conceptions of what the life of a group of nations should be.</p>
        <p>The true ideal of internationalism—and we unhesitatingly claim that this is the one enter- tained by ourselves and our Allies—is a com- monwealth of 'nations of friendly States differ- ing in size, in strength, each making its con- tribution to the world's thought and work, each contributing its quota to the general good, each free to work out its national development, sub- ject only to the larger international interests. For just as an individual cannot do as he likes even, in his own backyard, so the-freedom of nations must become more and more limited as civilisation increasingly intertwines the nations in common interests. The thought behind the question "Am I my brother's keeper?" whether uttered by an individual or on behalf of a nation is an offence to both God and man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>COSMOPOLITANISM.</head>
        <p>COSMOPOLITANISM.</p>
        <p>Little need be said about cosmopolitanism be- cause this is ruled out if we are right in con- tending that through a healthy internationalism we can best attain human progress., Cosmopolitanism, I claim, is not the goal to which civilisation is marching, but nationalism, involving internationalism, is the natural and permanent line of development. The highest good of mankind can be achieved by the build- ing up of self-respecting free nations, develop- ing along their own lines, and each independent in its own sphere. A cosmopolite is one who claims to be a citizen of the world and not of any one nation, who has no special affection for one country nor homeland, and no patriotism, whose interest ex- tends everywhere, but centres nowhere. The ideas of such are stark and cold when set be- side those which spring from the warm blood and brain of him who has been cradled in the mother thoughts of home and country. The fusion of races, the adoption of a ;universal language, the removal of national divisions, the conception of such a world condition, even if possible is not desired. Such a conception con- flicts with the most fundamental and constant elements in our nature. And so I re-emphasise the virility of nationalism and patriotism, and I join with William Watson in saying:—</p>
        <p>" I'cannot boast myself cosmopolite; I own to insularity,' although 'Tis fall'n from fashion, as full vell I know, For somehow, being a plain and single wight, I am skin-deep a child of the new light, But chiefly am mere Englishman, below Of island-fostering; and can hate a foe, And trust my kin before the Muscovite. Whom shall I trust if not my kin? Ani whom Account so near in natural bonds as these Born of my mother England's mighty womb, Nursed on my Mother England's mighty knees, And lull'd as I was lull'd in glory and gloom, • With cradle-song of her protecting seas?" - And now in conclusion am I not justified in striking the patriotic note in regard to this war? From a material point of view we .have nothing to gain. Our loss will be enormous. With us there is no loveless lust for territory, no desire for world dominion; we are content with our culture, but have no desire to force it upon others. On the fourth of August, 1914, we risked much in staking our all in the -defence of the sanctity of nationality, the right of small nations to live, as represented by Belgium and Serbia. If England had held 'back on this fateful day, then it might truly have been said that her day "Slopes to its twilight, and for honour, there Is needed but a requiem and a grave."</p>
        <p>In England's action, right, justice and mercy were merged with her strong national patriot- ism; and the greatness Of the sacrifice then offered, and being daily offered, stirs every fibre of our being and makes us proud of our country and her people. We stand then by England, the succourer of oppressed nations, the island home of a free and loyal race. • "The England that rejoiced to see Hellas unbound, Italy one and free, The England that had tears for Poland's doom, And in her heart for all the world makes room. The England from whose side we will nbt swerve, The immortal England whom we too would</p>
        <p>serve, Accounting her all living lands above, In justice, and in mercy, and in love."</p>
        <p>NATIONALISM CANNOT BE ABOLISHED. Moving a vote of thanks to the lecturer, Canon Stevenson said that he was practically in agreement with everything that Mr. Parker had said. He could not conceive how national- ism could be abolished _without losing the strength, ,capacity and everything that largely made a nation what it was. When Mr. Parker touched upon internationalism he (the speaker) thought he detected that the lecturer 110(1 been face to face with difficulties which every thoughtful person must feel. The conduct of Germany made one tremble for international- ism. They could only say that it was by the spread of the principles of righteousness, truth. justice and lore that they could hope that</p>
        <p>USEFUL the ONLY Presen this year; and wh; could be more useful. and more "Xma.isy' —than Cosy Slipper. or dainty Evening Shoe; We've all the mot. charming shades. Baby has told us privately he'd love pair of our dear little Nature- form Shoes. These "Benefit" Presents are quite inexpensive. Public Benefit Boot CAi Limited</p>
        <p>Under the C Bridge Str( WARRINGT</p>
        <p>lock, cet, EON.</p>
        <p>the barbarities of Germany could never happen again.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Rymer Young, seconding, said that he was able to describe himself as being in agreement with Mr. Parker.</p>
        <p>FEDERATI ON.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett pleaded for the idea of federation, which, he said, would allow each nation to develop, but would unite them for common purposes. The federation of the British Empire might follow with a federation of the Allies, and if the time came when they could forgive Germany, she might be included. Dr. Manson spoke of the binding influence of the Jewish faith upon that nation. Mr. a. Lofthouse dwelt upon the importance of all people, men and women, doing their best to make their country better and purer. The influence • of racial ties was spoken of by the Rev. IL V. I'igot (the chairman). The vote of thanks was heartily accorded. Replying, Mr. Parker expressed the opinion that religion was a factor that was becoming of less account in nationality. With regard ta federation, he was a little afraid of centralisa- tion. The war was not due to the failure of religion. The more Germany was studied the more it would be realised that in that country there was a denial of the principles upon which Christianity was based. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>„. G n ' i3oenelit ilk._ _........</p>
        <p>.•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>STOP THAT RHEUMATISM.</head>
        <p>STOP THAT RHEUMATISM.</p>
        <p>"There is no need to suffer a day longer with your rheumatism," says Dr. Geo. E. Flood, the great Specialist. "The famous Barkola Pre- scription has cured thousands, and will cure you so that you remain cured, if you will let it." Go to your Chemist and ask for a bottle of Barkola Concentrate (get the name right), and take a few drops four. times a day, in water. The pain and swelling subs:tle from the very first dose. Nothing hut Barkola Concentrate is needed, as the mixture is complete in itself. It costs 3s.</p>
        <p>If your Chemist cannot suPply you, don't waste time, but send postal order for the amount to The Barkola Co.. Ltd.. 1, Dorset-street, Canon , bury, London, and a bottle will be sent by return.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>CONSEQUENCES.</head>
        <p>CONSEQUENCES.</p>
        <p>We Always "Get Back Our Mete As We Measure."</p>
        <p>There is a joy in doing the right thing always. There is sorrow always in doing the wrong thing. The joy or the sorrow may not follow immediately, but they follow surely. There is not a thing that brings affliction to one's mind or body but can be 'found if we go far enough back. All consequences have causes that par- take of their nature.</p>
        <p>All this may be short of Einersonian. So much the better. It is as close to life as the kernel is to the shell. It goes with us when- ever we talk, trade, visit, work, when we write, ride, see shows or run politics. •There is not a thing done but that is ticketed with the appro- priate consequence. The ticket is never lost. It may seem sometime to be blurred or bidden, but at last it appears. Since the world was started it never failed to.</p>
        <p>The man who lies, cheats, steals will some- times find the consequence appear in a form that seems unrelated to the offence. It might not appear in the loss of money or his house burning down, but it may be a steel thrust into the heart of his dearest hope. No fortune no friendship will stand in the way. "We get back our mete as we measure," sang Richard Realf, and it is as true as 12 inches make a foot.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <p>-"ti ~,jr,„,:. - ..../..L:i; ite ' ~,,-,., ~r .: '. ,' ..,...)- ..'"''''''</p>
