      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>POISONOUS PIN SCRATCH.- t</head>
        <p>POISONOUS PIN SCRATCH.- t</p>
        <p>Zam-Buli. Heals A Painful Abscess.</p>
        <p>In the cold weather the tiniest break of the skin may easily result in a bad fester or painful disfiguring sores. To prevent this, keep a box of Zam-Buk handy and use it immediately you cut, scratch, burn or scald yourself. Mrs. E.. F. Ashmore, 22, Carlisle-street, Sheep- bridge, Derbyshire, writes:—" I scratched my chin with a pin and the place became very sore and inflamed. An abscess formed, and my chin got so bad that I was unable to do my work. Reading about*-Zam-Buk I decided to try this healer. Besides allaying the pain, Zam-Buk cleansed and healed tjae place in a most satis- factory way."</p>
        <p>They Have Their Troubles.. • • Samuel Gompers, at the recent convention fro Washington of the Civic Federation, said of children: " Children should be protected front - wage slavery, for, when free as air, they have enough trouble, dear knows. Walking along an East Side st.reet I came on two tiny tots, tho smaller of whom was bawling as if to break his, lungs. A window opened and a little girl shrieked: "'Tommy, who's been a-hittin' of ye?' " 'Nobody's been a-hittite of hint," the 1 grger tot answered. He's swallered a worm.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
