      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>I 1 THE co-unTs.</head>
        <p>I 1 THE co-unTs.</p>
        <p>ATTESTED AND LOST HIS HEAD.</p>
        <p>Defendant Who_Wants To Join The</p>
        <p>Navy.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Monday, Martin Kennedy, a labourer, of 42, Foundry- street, Warrington, was charged with doing wilful damage to the amount of 3s. 9d. by breaking a window at 27, -Hoyle-street. He pleaded guilty. The Chief Constable said that the prisoner broke into the house, but having regard to his 'condition he was only charged with wilful damage. On Stiturday he smashed the window,. got into the house and did some damage. Later he broke into another house and did some damage there. He was engaged on munition work, but on Tuesday last week he attested and got his 2s. 9d. That seemed to have caused him to lose his head. When he came into the Police Station he seemed to think that the wall was softer than his head, but he soon found out that this was not so. Prisoner said he did not remember anything about his alleged offence, and that he wanted to go into the Navy. The case was adjourned to see if this was possible. . Defendant was brought before • the Court on Triday and was fined ss. and ordered to pay the damage, 7s. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>WORKMEN SUMMONED.</head>
        <p>WORKMEN SUMMONED.</p>
        <p>Left Their Work IVithout Notice.</p>
        <p>'rive men were summoned for leaving their Nvork without notice by Mr. John Tennant, of the Phcenix Brickworks, Hawthornestreet, War- rington, at the Borough Police Court on Mon- day. They were • Henry Wainwright. of 221, Winwick-road ; Robert Mason, of 42, Elizabeth- street; Alfred Jones, of 15, Aiken-street; William J'. Keddie, 25, Allcard-street; and Henry Buckley, 219, Winwick-road. Mr. Tennant said that on Monday, November '29th, he saw these men knocking about the shop and he asked why they were not working. They replied that they had an objection against the foreman, and unless he was discharged they would not work. This he (Mr. Tennant) could not agree to, and they left work. The men • declared that other men had left .without notice. Mr. Tennant said that two days' notice was the custom of the trade; and it was decided to adjourn the ease for a week, in order that evidence might be brought proving this. • .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>REAR LAMP.</head>
        <p>REAR LAMP.</p>
        <p>Chief Constable In Danger Through</p>
        <p>Obscured Light.</p>
        <p>For failing to have a visiblp rear light 'on a motor lorry, to display the identification plate, Albert C. Lock, 35,Ross-street, Salford, was fined 10s. at the Warrington Pence Court on Tues- day. P.C. Fish stated that at 6.40 on the night of November 24th defendant was driving along. Liverpool-road a motor lorry, the 'rear light of which was not visible. On examination it was found that a tarpaulin sheet was hanging over and obscuring the lamp, which was lighted.' The Chief Constable stated that he was motoring from Prescot on the night in question and the chauffeur was almost on top of the defendant's lorry owing to the absence of the light. He did not suggest it Was. intentional on the part of the defendant. Mr. Platt,„ solicitor, who appeared for the defendant, said it was purely the result of an accident.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (ConnCalor Roberts) in an- nouncing a nominal fine of lOs.. said it was a proper ease for the police to bring forward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>HARD ON LORD DERBY'S SCHEME.</head>
        <p>HARD ON LORD DERBY'S SCHEME.</p>
        <p>At the 'Warrington Borough Police Court on Tuesday,</p>
        <p>David Sharpe, carter, 193. Thelwall-lane, for being drunk and disorderly on Friars' Green, was fined 13s. or fourteen days.</p>
        <p>A police officer heard him shouting To --- with Lord Derby's scheme. They won't get me." The Chief Constable pointed out it was high time a. firm step was taken by the magistrates to stamp out drunkenness in such troublous times.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>ABSENTEES.</head>
        <p>ABSENTEES.</p>
        <p>Man Who Stayed Away Three Times.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Wednesday, before Mr, J. W. llayivard (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst and W. T. Williams, f Charleg Henry. Thompson, a private in the sth Manchester ..Regiment, stationed at South- port, was remanded to await escort for being an absentee from his regiment. • The Chief _Constable said this was the third occasion upon which accused had been brought before the Court. as an absentee. The Chairman (addressing Thompson): 'We are surprised at you! You don't look like a man who would run away,</p>
        <p>STI{ANGERS iMIRESTED.</p>
        <p>Their Atm men ts In _Warri no ton And</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>Detective Green, having his suspicions aroused by two strangers who arrived at the Central Station, Warrington, " shadowed? them both in Warrington and Storkton Reath, and eventually felt justified in airesting them the same evening. . On. Tuesday the Men appeared in the dock at</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>Have you known A Worse Case of Indigestion</head>
        <p>Have you known A Worse Case of Indigestion</p>
        <p>Than This?</p>
        <p>over 8 months I was laid up with Indigestion, with pains under shoulder blades, and agony in the stomach. In the mornings, after a very sleepless night, I would rise feeling dull, heavy and depressed, with no appetite for breakfast. I was so bilious that the very sight of food made me , sick. A friend brought me a bottle of your Seigel's Syrup. I found that after a few doses, I wag able to eat a little food, with some show of enjoyment. I continued until my appetite was restored. The stomach was so relieved that meals became a time of pleasure. When retnember the doctor's words that he could do no more for and that my .working days were over, I . Consider that your Syrup has worked a real miracle. I have now been .at work for some years a real healthy man, and consider that this is entirely due to Seigel's Syrup." Letter from Mr, John Charming, of 1, Sydney Cottage, Whipton, Exeter. - July 12th, 1915. •</p>
        <p>the Borough Police Court on the charge of loitering with intent to commit a felony. The magistrates on the Bench were Councillor Henry Roberts (in the chair) and Messrs.. C. S. Burgess and J. Rymer Young. Prisoners gave their names as Harry Davies and Henry Drummond. The Chief Constable, in applying for a week's remand, said the men were seen to arrive separately at the station by Detective Green and join together at the end of Bewsey-street. For about an hour and a half the officer kept the accused under observation. Their move- ments in the town and at Stockton Heath were very suspicious. When arrested they declined to give any information. In order that the police might make their inquiries complete, the remand was necessary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>AN UNFIT HORSE.</head>
        <p>AN UNFIT HORSE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court on Thurs- day,</p>
        <p>Louis Rosenthal, 11, Clifton-street, was sum- moned for cruelty to a horse by working it in an unfit state. Sidney Jolley, 33, Dixon-street, his employer, was summoned for permitting the horse to be worked. On December 7th Inspector Dee saw the horse in Bridge-street. - On ex- amination he found a large blood-raw wound, the size of a crown piece, on the chest. There was also another sore on the withers. Defendant Rosenthal, who was driving, said he had not noticed the sores when he harnessed the horse on the morning in question. Jolley stated that he had been away on busi- ness for a few days and had not seen the con- dition of the horse. A fine of 20s. each was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>KINDLY GAOL.</head>
        <p>KINDLY GAOL.</p>
        <p>From Station To Station On A Hand- Cart. At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Thursday, before Mr. J. Rymer Young, Mr. A. J. Jolley and Dr. Naden, Mary Swift, or Leonard, Dakin's-yard, was sentenced to 21 days' imprisonment for being drunk and disorderly, and assaulting Railway Constable Clarke.</p>
        <p>On_ Wednesday night accused, who was in a</p>
        <p>drunken condition, was creating a disturbance on the Central Station. Immediately the officer spoke to her about her conduct she caught hold of him by the throat. A struggle ensued and she threw herself on the ground. In order to expedite her removal to the Police Station a handcart was requisitioned. On the way her flow of language was disgraceful. The Chairman said the magistrates considered</p>
        <p>they were doing her a kindness by sending her to prison for the term mentioned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>"LET IN."</head>
        <p>"LET IN."</p>
        <p>2:18 horse Causes New, Owner To Be</p>
        <p>Summoned. At the Warrington Petty Sessions on Wednes- day, before Mr. J. Rymer Young and Mr. W. Fa irclough John Ball, Bent's Cottage, Winwick, was sum- moned for working a horse in an unfit state on December Ist. Inspector Dee, 'of the N.S.P.C.A., saw the</p>
        <p>horse attached to a cart laden with coal. Tho animal was very lame on'the off fore leg, and on further examination was found to be .suffering from sprained tendons. Defendant told the in- spector that he bought the horse two months ago for £lB. In the opinion of the inspector defendant had been "let in" with regard to the horse. A nominal fine of ss. was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>MILK DELIVERY.</head>
        <p>MILK DELIVERY.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Magistrates</p>
        <p>And Child Labour.</p>
        <p>WAR-TIME CONCESSIONS.</p>
        <p>At the Daresbury Petty Sessions. held at Stockton Heath on Tuesday. before Mr. F. A. Frost (in the chair), Councillor Henry Roberts, Mr. W. Fletcher, Mr. James Hepherd, and Mr. J. A. Ransome,</p>
        <p>Richard Campbell, of 36, Walton-road, Stock- ton Heath, was summoned for having, on Nov- ember sth, employed a child for the sale and delivery of milk at 8.45 in the morning. A similar charge was preferred against Joseph Bradford, of Whitefield-road, Stockton Heath. Mr. IT. J. Westbrook represented the defend- ants and pleaded guilty to a technical offence. Prosecuting, Mr. James Hallard (Inspector of Weights and Measures, of Chester Castle) sard that oh November sth he saw a boy named Robert Wheeler, in the employ of Bradford, delivering milk near the Swing Bridge at 8.57 a.m. In Campbell's case he saw a boy named Percy Richardson with a can of milk at 8.45 a.m. According to the by-laws, the boys were only allowed to deliver milk between 6.30 and 8.30 in the morning, in addition to the evening hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>Conditions Not Normal.</head>
        <p>Conditions Not Normal.</p>
        <p>Mr. Westbrook asked that the case should be dismissed without' costs, saying that in these abnormal times such proceedings ought not to be lodged. This by-law was made in 1911, in normal conditions. It did not appear to be known by some people that the country was at war. It was common knowledge that there was a serious shortage of labour amongst farmers, and it was a fact that milk-dealers could not get their milk until thirty minutes or forty minutes later than they could in normal times. It was absolutely impossible at the present time to deliver the milk in, the time stated by the by-law.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>Milk Once A Day.</head>
        <p>Milk Once A Day.</p>
        <p>The farmers had also refused to make more than one delivery a day, so the milk-sellers had to do a double round every morning. Both his clients had done their best to comply with the by-law. They had employed additional labour, but there was a limit to this, as it would be impossible, if more boys were,employed, to make a profit out of the business. On the morning in question both of the boys must have been at school betweeii 9 and 9.5 a.m., as they re- ceived. their registration mark. He asked the Bench to show. their disapproval of proceedings of this kind, in these exceptional times.</p>
        <p>Dismissed.</p>
        <p>The cases were dismissed without costs.</p>
        <p>A -similar charge was preferred against Wm. Brereton, of Hawthorne-grove, but in view of the magistrates' decision in the last case it was withdrawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>FOUND DROWNED.</head>
        <p>FOUND DROWNED.</p>
        <p>Warrington Crane Driver's Unaccountable Disappearance.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held by Mr. S. Brighouse on Wednesday at the Sankey Chapel House Inn, on henry Gust (38), eranedriver, 140, Longford- street, Warrington, whose body was found in the brook adjoining the Sankey and St. Helens Canal, near Bewsey Arehes,,on Monday. Guest was last seen by his mother, with whom he lived, on Wednesday night, December Bth, when he left home, telling his mother to put up his breakfast, as he was going to work the following morning. He had not enjoyed very good health for a number of years, suffering, from pains in the head and dizziness, but he had never threatenedlko take away his life. It was nothing unusual for him to stay away from home at night, and no importance was attached to it. When he did not return to work the matter was reported to the police. It was pointed out to the Coroner that the meadows in that locality had been in a flooded state for some. tithe past, and recently the nights had been very foggy. A verdict of "Found drowned" was re- turned, there being no evidence to show how the man got into the water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>THE POLITE SCOTCH CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Mention was made of the word " cowhouse " in this column the other day. To a Scotsman the word conveys no meaning. When he wishes to speak of a building in which cows are housed he uses the word "byre." And thereby hangs a tale. In the course of a concert given•recently by a party of Scotch Territorials "somewhere in England," the next item on the programme read: "Violin solo, 'The Muckin' o' Geordie's Byre,' Private A. McNab." The Chairman, himself a Scot, knew this would be utterly un- intelligible to the majority of the people present, who were mostly English. He rose to the occasion, however, and his announcement of the item in question ran thus: "Private Angus McNab will now favour us with the well-known Scottish air, 4. The—all—Cleansing of—ah— George's—ah—Cowhouse.' "—"Daily Chronicle.''</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>CORNISH BUSHELS AND CHESHIRE ACRES.</head>
        <p>CORNISH BUSHELS AND CHESHIRE ACRES.</p>
        <p>This week our Cornish friends step in to throw light on the "wheat at £1 a bushel" mystery, says "Farm and Home." Alas, the " bushel " is not a bushel, but a Cornish bushel," which is a very ..different thing. It is another illustration of our chaotic system of weights and measures.• -</p>
        <p>How full of pitfalls these are to the unsuspect- ing stranger to a particular neighbourhood we have often shown. There are imperial acres, there are Irish, Cornish, which are greater, and the Cheshire, which excels all others by incor- porating within its dimensions no less than 10,240 square yards. There are imperial bushels of 8 gallons, Carlisle bushels of 24 gallons, and Cornish bushels of the same, and the Winchester bushel, which gives us the nice figure, very pleasant for calculation purposes, of 7.75557 gallons—and a lot more. ' Wheat is sold by bolls, that may be anything from 3 to 6 bushels, according to locality, and when used for oats the same measure may be either 6 or 8 bushels. We have wheat quoted also in some markets by the windle, butter by the pound, which may be 16 or 240 z., though ' when made of the latter weight it is generally known as a roll, and we have stones of 81b., 12. I 14, 16, 171, 24, and 281 b. How is anyone to com- pare market quotations in one part of the country with those in another? It is hopeless confusion dating back to old days, when travel- ling facilities were bad, and each district. sufficed to itself, but surely an anachronism at this stage of the world's history. One would have thought that long ere this the advantage of using standard weights and measures would have led to a change, but here we find Cornwall selling " bushels " that are anything but bushels, and for a week or so making the farmers of the rest of the country think that their" brethren in the West had found a 'gold mine!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>AGRICULTURE IN CHESHIRE.</head>
        <p>AGRICULTURE IN CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>The County. That May Be Regarded As The Bright Spot."</p>
        <p>Mr. W. J. Dutton, speaking at a recent meet- ing of the Cheshire Chamber of Agriculture, said that Cheshire farmers had during the last twenty-five years raised a standard which their children would have very great difficulty in following. It was true that Cheshire had lost 2 per cent. of the total acreage in crops. There were 10,936 fewer acres tilled by the farmers of this country than were cultivated 25 years ago. On the other hand arable 'land in the county had increased very considerably. Not- withstanding the fact that they had an in- creased number of horned cattle they were till- ing to-day 11,876 more acres than were under the plough 25 years ago. They were keeping more stock on the grazing land than had ever been known in the county before. In response to the call of the Board of Agriculture they had increased the wheat acreage during the last twelve months by 4,002 acres. They, had also increased their average of oats. The crop this year had in some cases been an absolute failure owing to the drought in May and June. They must expect a reduction in the area under culti- vation for potatoes. for the simple reason that they could nOt get the labour to put in the seed. Their annual acreage was 24,000, but last year it was 21,664, a reduction of 2,739, which was nearly 121 ner cent. of the entire acreage under potatoes. When there was such a big reduction in the acreage, they might expect the public to pay a little more for their potatoes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>Increase in Stock Breeding.</head>
        <p>Increase in Stock Breeding.</p>
        <p>• In Cheshire they had increased their stock of horses from 22,314 to 26,992. Last year, before the drain for .Army, requirements, 'the horses of Cheshire stood at 29,412. They had increased the number of horses in Cheshire by almost 7,000, whereas the whole of the country was showing a deficit of 21 2.3 per cent. That was a remarkable result, which the local Shire Horse Society and those gentlemen who had shown such practical interest in thefl breeding of horses had done so much to bring about. In the last 25 years the increase in the number of horned cattle in England and Wales was 741,000, and this number no less than 32,000 represented the increase in Cheshire. The number in Cheshire in 1890 was 167,000, and in 1915 it was 199,424. With regard to dairy cows, Cheshire was again the bright spot. Cheshire had a few more than it possessed in 1914. There was a big increase in the young stock— two-year-olds and calves—and they had actually a few more in milk than they had a few months ago. Sheep showed a reduction, but sheep in Cheshire were quite a supplementary part of the farming work, and figures respecting theni were not a very safe guide. While the country's stock of pigs had only increased during 25 years by 3,000 head Cheshire could boast of an increase of 194,000 head. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>County of Largo Holdings.</head>
        <p>A County of Largo Holdings.</p>
        <p>A gentleman who was womilis ently connected .with connty matters some time ago made the statement that Cheshire was a county of small holders; and he gave certain figures in support of it. Only half the story was told. 'The state- ment was .that in Cheshire there were .8,720 occupiers of land above one acre and under fifty acres, and this constituted 71 per cent. of the entire holdings of the county. That was only an assumption,. because, although they were 71 per, cent. of the number of occupiers, they only occupied 22 per pent. of the. land. The large holdings comprised 409,575 acres, representing a little under 77 per cent, of the entire holdings of the county. He admitted that the small- holders had done their share along with the large farmers; he had not a word to say against them. The large farmers could work amicably with their neighbours who were smallholders. There was only a lively rivalry between them. But let it not be said that the success of the county was due to the smallholder. It had' very largely depended upon those who occupied the 77 per cent, of the total acreage in the county and had tilled it with such success during the last twenty-five years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>MANURING FRUIT TREES.</p>
        <p>Apples and pears on dwarf stocks which have been planted for some years and probably borne heavy crops of fruit become enfeebled and are in Want of some ,assiStance. Espeeially is this necessary in the, case of trees growing on light and gravelly soils. In such soils if the trees are not given heavy mulehings of 'manure and frequent supplies of liquid manure—if such are obtainable—the fruit becomes dry in texture and poor in quality. Now is the tinie when such assistance may be advantageously given. From now onwards during the next three months every attention should be .paid to the trees in the way, of helping them to perfect their frni buds and lay the foundation of the • coming year's harvest. Far fewer complaints of non- bearing fruit trees would be heard if mo're at- tention were paid to the trees at this season of the year in the way of helping them with rich food: You cannot expect a crop of fruit year , after year if you add nothing to the soil to compensate for the loss of energy the roots have had to bear in the bringing of the fruit to per- fection.—" Wick," in " Farm and Home."</p>
        <p>Heavy words in meeting will not make up for short veight in market.</p>
        <p>And the less salary a man gets the more his wife can pretend he gets.</p>
        <p>Only a woman can enjoy being unhappy be- cause she is misunderstood.</p>
        <p>.:. .:..:. .:..:..:4:..:..:. .:. .:. .:..:. ...:..:..:..:..:..:.,:. ..:..).:..:..:..:..:..:..:. .:. .:. •. •:• •:• •. •. •:•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>FOOTBALL FIELD.</head>
        <p>THE FOOTBALL FIELD.</p>
        <p>SECOND WAR-YEAR SEASON AMONG THE JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>.i. .:. .:. .:. 4. .:. 4. 4. .:. .:. .:. 4. .:. 4. .:. 4. •4. .:. .:. 4, .:. .:. .:. 4. + .:. .:. .:. .:. 4. ..... 4. .:. 4. 4. .:. ic. .c. .:. i. • .:. 4. .:. .:.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Several Matches Postponed Owing To Wretched Weather.</p>
        <p>BIG WIN FOR WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the conditions for playing foot- ball were again very bad, rain falling nearly all the afternoon, and being accompanied by a very strong wind, which greatly interfered with the playing, of fixtures. Several were postponed, and where games were played, there was only a few keen enthusiasts present.</p>
        <p>The Hollins Green team again visited Hood- lane to play their fixture with the Warrington A.F.C., a fixture which, owing to bad weather, was postponed the previous week. It was only after some discussien that it was decided ,to play the match. The home club were able to piace only a scratch team in' the field, as Siddall, Foster, Glover, Leah and G. Whitfield were away. The visitors, although they had not their full team out, had the assistance of Millington, a Queen's Park Ranger and a ' former Bury player. The visitors had the wind in their favour, but the home forwards nearly succeede in scoring in the first fifteen minutes of the game, after which the visitors improved greatly, but failed to get past the defence of Connor, Millward and Brindle. At the interval no goals had been scored. In the second half the home team did nol mince matters at all, Aspinall doing the hat trick" and Illidge and Whit- field supplementing by one each. The game was concluded in semi-darkness with an easy victory for the home club.</p>
        <p>The game between the Emmanuel Institute and the Warrington Central Clubs had to be cancelled owing to the latter being unable to raise a team.</p>
        <p>It is not often that home clubs are unable to raise a teanit but this happened last week when Cairo-street Unitarians made the journey to thn King and Queen ground to play their fixture with the Elmwood Congregationalists. The ratter were unable to fulfil their engagement.</p>
        <p>It is understood that the Bewsey Albion Club has withdrawn from the Warrington an Dis- trict Junior League. It appears that a few weeks ago a protest was lodged against one of the Albion players as being too old to play in the competition. and the club asked by the League Committee to obtain the necessary proof according to their rules. They have failed to comply with the request.</p>
        <p>The game between Fairfield United Reserves and Bewsey A.F.C. was postponed on account of the late arrival of the Bewsey team. '</p>
        <p>The Scouts are not maintaining their form, and last week they sustained another defeat on their own ground. Jks</p>
        <p>The Orford Juniors are to be complimented for their smart victory over the Scouts.</p>
        <p>Last week all the Army Service Units of the League were on the defeated side :—The Scouts, the Cadets, and the C.L.B.</p>
        <p>Howley United are top scorers of the season; 5-0 against C.L.B.</p>
        <p>There was great delight in the Afeisey Juniors' camp on account of_ their victory over the Cadets.</p>
        <p>In to-day's fixtures there will be a very hard struggle between those two keen rivals, Pad- dington United and Rollins Green, on the ground of the former club. Both clubs have selected their strongest teams, and it is ex- pected that Millington, of the Queen's Para Rangers will assist the Rollins Green Club.</p>
        <p>The Paddington United officials would like to arrange fixtures with the Warrington A.F.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCH.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ASSOCIATION v. HOLLINS GREEN.</p>
        <p>In very boisterouk weather, and before a small attendance, the folrowing players turned out 'for their respective clubs on the Hood-lane ground. Neither team, were at full strength :—. Warrington: Brindle; Connor and Millward ; Payne, Heywood and Ditchfield; Illidge, Whit; field, Aspinall, J. J. Millington and Banner. Hotlins Green: Jones; Millington and Mellor; Buckley, Etchells and Cleworth ; Wright, Harri- son, Clarke, Green and Atkinson. Referee, H. Smith.</p>
        <p>The visitors won the toss and defended the lane goal, with the wind in their favour. From the kick-off the home forwards were quickly on the aggressive. A wide pass from Aspinall enabled the Visitors' defence to relieve ' their</p>
        <p>lines, and play was taken into the home half, where a good return by Millward placed the visitors again on the defensive. A free-kick ,to the home team at close quarters cansed Etchells to concede a corner-kick. This was easily cleared, and the visitors raced away towards the home goal; but Millward returned the leather and another corner was gained at the visitors' end. This also was futile. The visitors were now more settled in their play, and placed the home goal in danger. Play continued in the</p>
        <p>home half and Atkinson tested Brindle with a splendid long shot, which was safely got away. Even play followed until the interval, no score having been made. On resuming the home teank were more</p>
        <p>aggressive and the forwards were ciuickly at the visitors' end. A corner-kick was forced, and from a good save by Jones, the visitors' goal had a narrow escape. The visitors made strenuous efforts to break through the home defence, but Connor and Millward proved equal to the occasion and Brindle was not seriously</p>
        <p>troubled. After fifteen minutes of the second portion had gone Aspinall opened the score from thirty yards' range. After another dangerous attack 'Tones rushed out and cleared at the critical moment. Following a short visit to the home end, the home forwards showed good com- bination, and Aspinall scored the second goal. This was added to by a good effort- from Whit- field. Illidge scored a fourth goal after a good individual run. Aspinall added another before the call of time. Result:—Warrington, 5 goals; Hollins Green, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Ckwas • P. W. L. St. Eapliires Stockton Heath Scouts 9 6 3 0 18 12 12 Howley United v. 9 5 3 1 41 15 11</p>
        <p>Fairfield United Res. 7 5 2 0 29 10 10 Orford Juniors Mersey Juniors Bewsey A.F.C. , 6 1 4 1 8 21 3 Latchford C.C.</p>
        <p>Padgate C.L.8.7 0 7 0 6 65 0 BeWsey Albion have withdrawn from the League, and the results of their matches have also been deducted.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD CADETS v. MERSEY JUNIORS</p>
        <p>The above teams met on the Arpley ground. The Cadets' captain won the toss and decided to play with the wind in his favour. From the centre-kick the Cadets gained possession, and at once raided the Juniors' goal, whose defence was fortunate in clearing on several occasions. Bibby opened the score for the Cadets after ten minutes' play. The Juniors made several at- tempts to equalise, but their attacks were easily checked, and they were soon force I on the de- fensive again. , The Cadets continued to keep up the pressure, but failed to add to their score. Half-time :—Cadets, 1 goal; Juniors, nil. The Juniors now had the wind behind them and for a while their forwards showed too much eagerness in their attacks, and some wild shoot- ing was witnessed. After fifteen minutes had elapsed Aikenhead equalised the stores by</p>
        <p>scoring from a well-placed corner-kick. Follow- ing this reverse the Cadets made a dash and played keenly, but failed to break down tho Junior' defence, and in a few minutes they were again defending their own citadel. From a long centre Williams, in attempting to clear, had the misfortune to head the ball through _ .</p>
        <p>his own goal, thus giving the Juniors the lead. The Juniors attacked strongly, and their efforts were soon rewarded by their inside right scoring the third goal. Result :—Mersey Juniors, 3 goals; Latchford Cadets, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>Two surprises occurred in the Northern Union games on Saturday, namely, the defeat of Rochdale Hornets on their own ground by Huddersfield, and the victory of Broughton</p>
        <p>Rangers at Halifax. The weather conditions were again bad, but it was possible to play ten games, the results of which were as follows : HUddersfield, 3 points; Rochdale H., 0. Salford, 3; Oldham, 3. St. Helens, 6; Runcorn, 2. Wigan, 3; St. Helens Recs., 0. Batley, 11; Bradford; 0. Dewsbury, 21; Hunsiet; 0.</p>
        <p>_ Hull, 13; Bramley, 0. Leeds, 17; Hull Kingston R., 8. York, 8; Brighouse, 0. Broughton, 7; Halifax, 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Three Matches Abandoned Owing To Bali Weather.</p>
        <p>Bad weather seems to fill the programme for Saturday afternoons nowadays. Three weeks ago we had a severe frost that made Rugby foot- ball impossible; a fortnight ago the rain was so heavy that it was "not fit for a dog to be out" and again play was impossible; last Saturday we had a combination of wind and rain. Play on the exposed Victoria Park and also .on the Rylands's grolind had again to be abandoned. but it was possible to play two games of forty minutes each at Wildersnool. Hero, Heathside met and defeated Sacred Heart, and the Bea- mont team did the same with St. James'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>HEATHSIDE GAIN TWO POINTS.</head>
        <p>HEATHSIDE GAIN TWO POINTS.</p>
        <p>The match between ITeathside and Sacred ' Heart was played at Wilderspool in a continual downpour of rain, before only about a dozen spectators.. In addition to the disadvantage of playing in the rain, a strong wind which blew down the field made accurate placing of the ball impossible. Sacred Heart, who turned out with only twelve men. had the advantage of the wind in the first, half, but in spite of this, their strongest efforts to nierce the defence of Heath- side were of no avail. After twenty minutes of boisterous play, the teams reversed positions without an interval. Neathside at once began to take up a vigorous offensive, and maintained it until they were awarded a free-kick when practically in front of the " Hearts' " goal. Two points resulted, and shortly afterwards Heath- cide crossed the " Hearts' " line, making their lead into five noints. The attempt to convert this last try failed. Heathside soon had another valnahle chance .of scoring, but offside play robbed them of another three points. Towards the close of the game the "Hearts" rallied and looked very much like scoring, when the whistle 'blew and left the score five points to nil in favour of Heathside. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>BEAMONT TOO GOOD FOR ST. JAMES'.</head>
        <p>BEAMONT TOO GOOD FOR ST. JAMES'.</p>
        <p>The Beamont—St. James' match had no striking feature save that the former showed their superiority over a depleted St. James' team. The conditions were better than they were for the previous game, the rain had ceased. but the wind was still strong. Beamont played with the wind in the first half, and it was confidently expected that they would soon score, for at the outset of the game St. James' had only about 'eight men on the field. But this anticipation was not realised. Though greatly pressed. the St James' men kept their goal in- tact. The St. James' team was pretty confident when the teams changed over, for they had the wind behind their backs and a strengthengd side. But Beamont were too strong for them. and kept up the attack. During a forward rush Bealnont scored close in, Whitfield adding the major paints. The game resulted :—Beamont. 5 points; St. James', nil. For the winners Hill Played a steady game. whilst Brannon, by his tackling, seemed to be the best St. James' player.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>LEAGUE TABLE UP TO DATE.</head>
        <p>LEAGUE TABLE UP TO DATE.</p>
        <p>Club P. W. L. D. (4. T. P. G. T. P. P. Heathside 10 9 1 0 13 18 80 4 6 26 18 Parochial 9 7 1 1 14 33 127 3 1 9 15 Rylands's 9 52 2 10 13 59 4 11' 41 12 Bolton 8 4 2 2 7 10 44 4 7 29 10 St. James' 10 4 5 1 9 . 5 33 8 12 52 9 St. Thomas' 7 3 2 2 ,8 7 37 2 7 25 Silver-st. 6 4 2 0 4 14 50 3 3 15 Beamont 10 4 6 0 7 6 32 13 15 71 8 St. Marv' s 10 3 7 0 2 6 22 8 17 87 6 Sacred RI 10 2 8 0 3 9 33 8 12 52 4 Whitecross 9 69 0 7 6 20 21 36 150 0</p>
      </div>
