        <p>At Gorsey-lane. Rylands's v. Sacred Heart. Referee, Mr. G Crozier. Kick-off 2.30. /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>SACRED HEART v. RYLANDS'S RECS. At Gorsey-lane:— Sacred Heart.—J. Clarke; Baker, Grady, Leah (captain), and Plant; Farrington and Dutton; Tichard, Dutton, Harrison, Dorrian, Sankey and Mercer. Reserves: L. MeGeer and John Clarke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>BEAMONT v. WHITECROSS.</head>
        <p>BEAMONT v. WHITECROSS.</p>
        <p>At Victoria Park. ' Beamont: Barnett; T. Percival, R. Whitfield (captain), A. Makin and P. Hill; W. Ellis and W. Titchard; H. Pemberton, S. Pemberton, J. Higham, F. Jones, T. Littlewood and S. Lunt. Reserves.—A. Guest and J. Nolan.</p>
        <p>BEAMONT JUNIORS v. ORFORD W.S.</p>
        <p>On Victoria Park.</p>
        <p>Beamont.—J. Andertun; J. Darbyshire, W. Brookes, A. Mason and A. Acourt; R. Bibbey and J. Coleman; W. Smith (captain), G. Armi- tage, J. Higgins, H. Monks, C. B. Healy and A. Hackett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>LUCK-IN WAR-TIME.</head>
        <p>LUCK-IN WAR-TIME.</p>
        <p>"I say!" said a man to a "Special Constable." "Yes. What's up?" said n the " Special "to an excited individual in Horsemarket-street.</p>
        <p>"Anybody looking ?" said the excited one. "No," said the "Special," "what the</p>
        <p>matter" "Come inside here?'</p>
        <p>The- whispered words were obeyed, and both entered a house. . "Come into the bedroom—it's safeet there." "What on earth is it?"</p>
        <p>"Hush, lock the door. Someone will hear us !"</p>
        <p>The " Special " did as requested, and began to wonder; when from below the bed the man produced a mysterious parcel. Opening this, he displayed to the " Special " a new Suit 0 Clothes, with a receipted bill pinned on to i for Twenty,Shillings. "Good gracious!</p>
        <p>Where did you get it?"</p>
        <p>said the "-Special." "Not a word to anybody !" said the excited one. "I read the advert. of Suits Ltd. in 'The Examiner '—walked in to 17, Horsenlarket- street, saw the material—gave the order at 205., and here it is." "Lucky fellow," said the 'Special "; "why it's worth three guineas. Can I have the Advertisement? Why, they make Costumes for • 605., and Ladies' Raincoats, as well as Gent's, from 215., also worth three guineas. I'll tell my wife—where is it?"</p>
        <p>"Suits Ltd., 17, Horsemarket-street, War- rington."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH SCOUTS v. ORFORD JUNIORS.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH SCOUTS v. ORFORD JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>These teams met on the ground of the former club, and were at full strength. The home cap- tain won the toss and decided to play with the wind in his favour. The Scouts were quickly in front of the visitors' citadel, but met with a stout defence by the Juniors. The visitors could not make much progress against the gale, and had to defend their own half. The Scouts</p>
        <p>missed many good openings by their wild shoot- ing, and the visitors succeeded in holding their citadel secure. At the interval no scores were registered. On resuming the visitors had the wind in their favour, and had more of the game, but they were at fault in the same manner as the Scouts had been, and sent many shots high over the bar. Johnson, Foster and Hampson de- fended finely. Eventually the Juniors managed to keep better control of the ball, arid became</p>
        <p>Lore dangerous. Foster, in making a good clear- ance, skied the ball, and the wind carried it towards the home goal and Hirst, following up, opened tho score with an easy shot. The Scouts redoubled their efforts to get on equal terms, and made a strong_ raid on the visitors goal, but the defenders were on the alert, and the</p>
        <p>couts soon had to retire again on the defensive. The ball was centred from the Juniors' left and, Clark beating Foster for possession, gave Ramp- son no chance with a hard drive, and registered the second goal. The result being:—Orford Juniors, 2 goals; Stockton Heath Scouts, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>PADGATE C.L.B. v. HOWLEY UNITED.</head>
        <p>PADGATE C.L.B. v. HOWLEY UNITED.</p>
        <p>The above teams met on the ground of the former, and the game was played in a gale of wind. The • United won the toss and had the wind in their favour. From the coinmencement the play was of a very onesided character, th 2 visitors practically doing as they liked. The score at the interval was in the visitors' favour by ten goals to nil. The second half was nearly a repetition of the first, and the visitors added five more goals to their total. Davis scored on five occasions and Miller scored four times, while Bradford (full-back) scored twice. Davis and Miller each scored from penalty-kicks. The game resulted ! Howley United, 15 goals; Padgate C.L.8., 0.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Paddington United v. Hollins Green.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Latchford C.C. v. Bewsey A.P.C. Padgate C.L.B. v. Fairfield United Reserves. Mersey Juniors v St. Elphin's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL INSTITUTE v. NEWTON FARM SCHOOL. Inqitute: T. Knight; E. Crozier and S. Hughes; F. Parr, E. Hardy and W. Ayres; W. Eaton, A. Oakes, T. Hughes, H. Stubbs and Wm. Eaton. Team meet at Central Station by 1.25. 'Bus leaves 1.30.</p>
        <p>Youthful Ideas. The following collection of " answers " taken from examination papers gives some idea of why teachers often grow discouraged with life:— An antesthetic is a person who noes not be- lieve in God. Name some of the early Christian Fathers? Jerome, Oxigen and Ambrasia. Celibacy is when a man's single. The opposite of this is pleurisy. John Milton was famous for keeping bad angels out of heaven. What are the parts of speech? Mouth, teeth, tongue, voice, palate and bronchial tubes. Rivers make the ocean, which would overflow only the sponges at the bottom absorb some of the water. .Why were there nine Muses? Because a cat has nine lives, was the startling reply.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>TYRE SAFETY.</head>
        <p>TYRE SAFETY.</p>
        <p>A photograph of an overturned car appearing in the " Motor " for December 7th remind us that the realities of winter have already arrived. The car shown was travelling on an ice-covered road when it skidded and overturned, in spite of the fact that on three of the wheels steel studded tyres were fitted. The tyre which has exeelled as a reliable non-skid, is the "Clincher Cross" pattern—it has an all-rubber tread, and when driven on the most dangerous surfaces Droves its immense superiority in security. It has been tested on every kind of road, including ice, asphalt and stone, and under every test has maintained its gripping power. This is due to the peculiar quality of the rubber and the unique pattern, which offer resistance at any point on which it may be stopped, so that the wheel does not need to revolve until it reaches a point of resistan.ce: In addition to the value of the "Clincher Cross" as a non-skid it possesses remarkable wearing quality; it has suc- cessfully achieved a 5,000 miles R.A.C. road test, and when it is considered that this test was carried out on a 60 h.p. car weighing 2 tons unloaded, it must be admitted that the wear resisting power is unique.</p>
        <p>• Startling. With an absurd question a man interrupted one of George Ade's stump speeches in Elkhart, Ind. - The humorist paused and smiled. Then he</p>
        <p>said : "That remark was unexpected, startling. As unexpected and startling as a salesman on Broadway. "A lady entered 11 Broadway curio shop and said: _</p>
        <p>" 'Let me see something handsome and cheap' " Just gaze on me, madam,' said the fat salesman."</p>
      </div>
