      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 18, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 18, 1915.</p>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Money.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>ARTS AND CRAFTS EIMBITION.</head>
        <p>ARTS AND CRAFTS EIMBITION.</p>
        <p>Wounded Soldiers Display Their Accomplishments.</p>
        <p>SALE AT RADDON COURT.</p>
        <p>An exhibition of arts and crafts and sale of the work of the patients at the Raddon Court Red Cross H6spital took place at the hospital on Wednesday. There was a large attendance of visitors, amongst whom were the following: Colonel Moggridge, Mrs. Evelyn Willis, Mrs. Arthur Willis, Mrs'. Mitchell, Mrs. Robson Chorley, Mrs. James Fairclough, Miss Tinsley. Mrs. Herbert Woods, Mrs. George Joseph, Mrs. Tilston, Mrs. Broadbent, Mrs. Segar Owen, Mrs. Dakin, Rev. H. V. and Mrs. Pigot, Misses Pigot, Miss Coxon, . Rev. ,G. C. Briggs, Dr. Falkiner, R.A.M.C., Misses Murray, Mrs. Brooke, Mr. Lofthouse, Mr. Mawson (master of the School of Art), Mrs. F. W. Monks, Mrs. Walker. VALUE OF THE RED CROSS SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Opening the proceedings, Colonel Sir Edward Cotton-Jodrell (County Director of the Cheshire Branch of the British Red Cross Society) psked the. soldiers' to think of what they owed to the British Red Cross Society. At the. beginning of the war the Army medical authorities would have been very much handicapped by having very large numbers of casualties to deal with, and an insufficient staff, had it. not ben for the Society. Since then the, work had greatly increased, plenty of . ex- perience had been gained, and the Society hal saved the authorities some hundreds of thousands of pounds. He appealed to the men to think of all this, and to respond as far as possible to the kindness which had been shown them. He congratulated the promoters of the sale upon its great success, and said that theit example might usefully be followed elsewhere. (Applause.) He declared the sale open. Colonel J. A. Moggridge (officer commanding the depot at Orford Barracks) in proposing vote of thanks to Colonel. Cotton-Jodrell, spoke of the work of the different hospitals in this neighbourhood, .and of the interest which the nurses took in the patients. Miss Sylvia Broadbent seconding, said that they were only too pleased to do anything for the patients, and they did not mind the ha-d work so long as the result was successful. She congratulated the workers on their industry, and on the splendid result of their work. • • A VARIETY OF ARTS.</p>
        <p>The numerous article's, including hand. painted Christmas cards (in which the chief in- terest was centred), calendars, knitted articles of various kinds, fretwork model (Made of whit 3 holly wood), breast-pins cut out of coins (the work of Corporal Pearce), were offered for sale. A magnificent water-colour picture, the work of Sergeant Bryan, of the West Yorks Regiment, was raffled. Before enlisting, Sergeant Bryan, who is a native of London, had attended for a considerable time an art school at Lambeth. He is a bookbinder by trade. Whilst in hospital he has painted many pictures. Other pictures had been painted by patients who have just picked up the art while in hospital. Some fancy prices were realised at the sale, calendars fetching sums of ss. 6d., 4s. d. and 2s. 9d., while Private Dagger's fretwork model realised ss. :-td. It may be noted that Private MeLf.iod (by whom several knitted articles had been . done) has won a prize at the Albert Hall for a knitted belt, and that Private Titheridge's belt, which was sold at the Warrington auction sale for £.B 10s., is still going around the country, tild so making money for the British Red Cross So-:frAy. After the articles had been purchased buyers could, if they wished, give them back to the hospital authorities, in order that the latter could send them to the British troops at the Front.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>PRIZE WINNERS.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE WINNERS.</p>
        <p>The prize list was as - follows :--</p>
        <p>Chriamas Cards.—Special first, Sergeant Bryan; .1, Private nowarth; 2, Private Bram- well; 3, Corporal Molyneux; 4, Rifleman Oakes. Calendars.-1, Private Jewsey; 2, Private Williams; 3, Corporal Tipper; 4, Private Wilde. Badges.-1, Private Green; 2, Sergeant-Major Northeote.</p>
        <p>Knitting (groups of articles) .-1, Private McLeod; 2, Corporal Brown; 3, Private Titheridge ; 4, Private Whorlow ; 5, Corporal Molyneux ; 6, Private Deebank.</p>
        <p>Scarves (by beginners).-1, Private Tudhope; 2, Private Donaldson; 3, Private Bramwell; 4. Private Davall; 5, Private Nelson; 6, Private King.</p>
        <p>Belts (by beginners).-1, Private King; 2, Private Whorlow ; 3, Private Tudor.</p>
        <p>Gloves and Mittens and Socks.-1, Private Cox; 2, Private Titheridge; 3, Private King. Wood Carving.-1, Corporal ifaggers.- Metal Carving.-1, Corporal Pearce; 2, Private Bradbury. , .</p>
        <p>Decorated Boxes.-1, Private Bradby. Mats.-1, Corporal Mapston; 2, Corporal Tipper. . '</p>
        <p>The judges were Mrs. Charlton Parr, Mrs. Waring, Mrs. Evelyn _Willis and Mrs. Hutchin- son.</p>
        <p>The money realised as a result of the sale will be devoted towards providng Christmas pre- sents and treats for the soldiers at the hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>IRONIV'ORKERS' DINNER.</head>
        <p>IRONIV'ORKERS' DINNER.</p>
        <p>Need For Co-operation Between Trench And Workshop.</p>
        <p>Feasting, speech-making, and music! What a medley mixture! But take the feasting first, and intermingle the speech-making with the music, and you will find yourself enjoying as pleasant an evening as the members and friends of the Bewsey Lodge of the Associated Iron and Steel Workers qf Great Britain spent at their first annual dinner in the Lion Hotel, Bridge- street, Warrington, on Saturday. Upwards of one hundred and fifty sat down to do justice to the spread placed before them; needless to say, justice was done. However, feasting, though first on the programme, was, only a forerunner of greater events.</p>
        <p>The musical programme, consisting of solos (both vocal and pianoforte), duets and quartets, was ably supported by the Brooklyn Quartet Party, accompanied tby Mr. A. Prow, who dubbed himself "pianist and stop-gap." Toasting played an important part in the pro- ceedings. As usual, "The King" was, given with patriotic enthusiasm, followed by "His Majesty's Forces," proposed by Mr. J. Elwell (who was presiding), to which Lieut. Chilton replied.</p>
        <p>For "The Visitors," proposed by Mr. T. Fellows, Mr. W. Hipkiss responded.</p>
        <p>Need for Saving.</p>
        <p>In proposing "The Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd.," Mr. Poole • spoke of the happy relationship, which the proper working of the society helped to maintain, between masters and men.</p>
        <p>In replying, Mr. C. E. Bunch said that he was a whole-hearted supporter of trade unionism, and hoped the day would come when there would be no room in any ironworks for a non-union man. "As ironworkers," be continued, "we all know that the present high and in- flated prices for iron, and consequent high wages, are due to the war, and will last prob- ably only so long as the war continues." He advised the men to put by the last two advances as a nest egg for the future. He also conveyed a message from Mr. Bleckly (managing direc- tor) saying how glad he was to hear that the men had been able to detach themselves from turmoil and strife, to enjoy so convivial a gathering; also that as the company was con- trolled, 7i per cent. excess profits were now taken by Government. The next toast, "Lodge and District," was proposed by Mr. D. Cowser, Messrs. J. Walker and J. Elwell replying. "The Associated Iron and Steel Workers of Great Britain" was proposed by Messrs. W. Spruce and H. Perks..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>Providing for Widows and Disablement.</head>
        <p>Providing for Widows and Disablement.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Hall (president of the association), in replying; told of the decision of the society to pay to. the Men who were totally disabled in their country's service the full compensation, if they were in benefit before enlistment; and if they fell in. the service of their fellow men to pay to the widow the sum she would have re- ceived had he died as a civilian. Mr. Hall emphasised the need for cb-operation in the workshop and trench, and said that victory must be organised if the liberty of democracy was to be maintained:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <p>L-4 9 9s. 11.1).—Lad's 6-guinea superb 5-stone °Gip./ _L tf real Diamond. 18-ct., very heavy, 4-hoop, claw sot Engagement Ring; great bargain; approval.—Davies's, 30, Low Hill, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>£1 ]_6s. 6D.—Gent's-bc-agrralltdreollgeedngiori Hunter (worth 4 truineas): perfect timekeeper; war- ranted; approval.—Dance's, 30 Low Hill. _</p>
        <p>£6 12s. 6D.—G(Tords eleurin;laupltigglislsaotelild by Russell t!4 Son. Church-street, Liverpool; full iewelled; new this year; accurate; approval.-- Davies's 30, Low Hill, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>DRINK CONTROL.</head>
        <p>DRINK CONTROL.</p>
        <p>What The Mayor &amp; Mr. Dukes</p>
        <p>Told The Conference.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Trades and Labour Council, at their meeting on Tuesday, heard a report from the Secretary (Mr. Charles Dukes) on the recent conference held in Manchester, which had for its object the restriction of the sale of liquor as a means of incrensing the output of munitions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>Mr. Dukes On " Busybodies."</head>
        <p>Mr. Dukes On " Busybodies."</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes explained that it was on the in- structions of the Executive Committee of the Trades Council that he attended the confer- ence to oppose any further restrictions sug- gested. Mi. Dukes reviewed at length the various points raised by the speakers. The first dozen speakers, he said, might be claimed to be representative of the employers. Every argu- ment that had been trotted out,. by people from the Minister of Munitions down to the small employer ;of labour, castigating the workers for their negligence and lack of thrift, was repeated. The general idea was that there was quite an extraordinary amount of drun- kenness, particularly' amongst the women-folk, who, it was alleged, were squandering their allowances in drink. The women's representa- tives.who were there gav4 a flat denial to that statement. He (Mr. Dukes), in a speech at the Conference, said he did not believe- that there, had been any increase in drinking; it had not been proved. • All the arguments brought for- ward had been based on money. No one had stated that the price of liquor had been con- siderably increased doxing the war. If the calculations had been based on normal prices, he questioned whether there had been an in- creased consumption at all. That there was drunkenness he was not surprised, because there had always been drunkenness; but now the worker had got two or three 6)iillings war bonus, and some women were receiving 225. per week without having to keep their. hus- bands, a few busybodies were poking their noses into their private affairs and wanted to know how they were spending the money. The drink evil had always been with them, and people who were now decrying it had re- fused to remedy the evil in normal times. Whilst he held strong views on the drink question, .he regretted the fact that at the Conference they heard nothing but temperance lecturers attempting •to exploit abnormal war conditions, in order to interfere with the con- sumption of drink. He did not !think they were going to make a nation sober by Act of Par- liament. At the inquiry he was questioned by Mr. John Hodge whether he objected to fur- ther restrictions between the hours of nine and ten o'clock. He (Mr. Dukes) replied that he had an objection to any interference with the drink laws. He was not an advocate of drinking. He had his own views as to what was wrong, but the present was no time to seek to impose their pet theories. If there was going to be, any further interference he be- lieved it was going to be resented. The Mayor's Opinion.</p>
        <p>At. the Conference, representing the War- rington Town Council, were the Mayor and Alderman Bennett The Mayor, in a Fpeech, stated that what experience they had at. the Munitions Tribunal of men being summoned for taking too much liquor was confined to Monday drinking. If the restrictions applied to Monday only, in the opinion of the Mayor it would be a way of getting over, the diffi- culty. He (Mr. Dukes) was informed that men even lost ,Monday now because they could not get a drink before they went to work-. (Laughter.) There was no discussion on the report.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>Christmas Greetings From Our</head>
        <p>Christmas Greetings From Our</p>
        <p>Prisoners Of War.</p>
        <p>The dispatch of Christmas parcels to all prisoners of war of the South Lancashire Regi- ment in Germany has now been completed, and the Men's thanks and appreciation of the efforts made to keep them from feeling that they are forgotten ,can be anticipated. Already Some of the men have written to the fund's subscribers their -Christmas greetings and wishes, in addition, to their hearty thanks, for the gifts cf welcome foodstuffs. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Play Of Morals At Rylands-street.</p>
        <p>There are many plays of the type of "The Unmarried Mother," this week occupying the boards of the Royal Court, some of which border very closely .to vulgarity, but the pre- sent production, we are pleased to say, may not be included in that category. C. Vernon Proctor has wound a fine plot on this burning question of the day, and Mr. Oswald Cray is fortunate enough in being able to have such a capable array of talent at his disposal as in the present production. This drama, judging from the attendance during the week, has met with enormous success. Miss Daisy Cook, as Eileen, is an actress of considerable talent. The title-part is an exacting one and difficult of portrayal, yet she got every ounce out of it. Mr. Victor Millard, as Private Walter Newton, the lover, also. rendered his part in capital style, as did Mr. H.. G. Robinson as Gerald Blake, a man of the world. The company is excellently cast, for one minute the audience is on the verge of tears, whilst the next in transports of delight, chiefly through the in- strumentality of Mr. Allan Carruthers, who plays the part of Jimmy O'Goblin, the work- shirker, who eventually throws off his lethargy, and joins the Forces. A play of the type of "The Unmarried Mother" touches the audience, our pockets, and our handkerchiefs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>Visit of " Betty."</head>
        <p>Visit of " Betty."</p>
        <p>During next week Sir Joseph Beecham's popu- lar theatre is to be closed so far as any per- fgrmances are concerned, but is to be reopened oil Boxing Day with one of the late George Edwardes' productions entitled "Betty." Lovers of musical comedy, we may add, are assured of witnessing a brilliant entertainment, and it is highly probable that those who do not book their seats in advance may be deprived of seeing this success of the London season. The box office is now open.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>A "Bill" Of All-Round Exce Mr. Will A. Jackson, the popular manager of the Hippodrome, has again been providing excellent fare for his patrons. Every item on the programme is of first-rate quality. In consequence full houses at both performances have been the order for the week.</p>
        <p>Premier place is awarded to "William 'Enery's Wedding," a typical Lancashire sketch, played naturally and capitally by Terence Byron and his 'company. Opening with an element of rich humour, which received appreciative applause, the latter part of the sketch is full of pathos, which goes to make one of the best "turns" we have as yet wit- nessed in this particular branch of entertain- ment. Another item of considerable note is Franco Piper, the inventor and originator of swinging, juggling and spinning banjos. 'Both novel and pleasing, this "turn" is one of the successes of the evening, merits whole-hearted applause, and is quite out Of 'the ordinary. The humorous parts of the programme are in the hands-of Jack Stocks, the trouble shifter, and Ez. Tait, the topical Character comedian, respectively, the •latter composing the songs he sings. Both of exceptional ability, they should go far in the profession. The original crazy 'dancing juggler, Halina, is very clever, and his clean and neat manipulation with sundry. articles leaves the audience in a state of be- wilderment.</p>
        <p>The Morandinis provide a sensational bend- ing bamboo pole and ladder act, which liter- ally gives one 'heart leaps every few moments, and another "turn" of the same kind is pre- sented by the Five Whiteleys, knockabouts and wire-walkers. " Gaumont Graphic" and several selections .of good music by the orchestra complete a programme worthy of note, and to intending patrons to-night we would advise an early arrival.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>Next Week's Programme.</head>
        <p>Next Week's Programme.</p>
        <p>• Premier place nest week on the bill is awarded to Duncan's Royal Scotch .Collies, the educated canines, and to all lovers of animals this turn should prove especially interesting; Another Jack McPherson, the Caledonian jester, in Scotch character studies, is also due to appear, and is sure of a worthy reception. The Musical McGregors, in their wonderful manipulation of the zylophone ; Jack Murray, comedian; Harry Brown in his latest success, "Pack up all you've got "; Eugene Garadini, described as England's premier actor-musician; Ada Wright. comedienne, in funny patter songs. and the " Gaumont Graphic" of topical pictures pro- vides truly fine Christmas fare at ,this variety theatre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>" STAR " FILMS AT THE STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>" STAR " FILMS AT THE STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>"Tip-top" programmes were again the order at the Star Kinema House during the first half of this week. "The Verdict of the Heart," adapted from Charles Garvice's novel of the same title, occupied the first place, and a thorough good three-part picture it proved to be. The fifth episode of the "Exploits of Elaine," depicting the "Poisoned Rom," was very thrilling; while "Black and White Snow- ball," a Vitagraph comedy, was much enjoyed. " Wifie's Ma Comes Back," featuring Billy Reeves, comedian, was exceedingly funny. " Pathe's Gazette" illustrated several very interesting war scenes. "Jim the Penman" is the title of a fine film which occupies premier position during the week-encl. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>"Two Fine Films."</head>
        <p>"Two Fine Films."</p>
        <p>Splendid films, quite in accordance with this popular house, are to be shown during the next week, for on Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday the star picture is one entitled "The Second in Command." The latter half of the week pre- mier place is awarded to that fine film "Anthony and Cleopatra." On Christmas Day there are to be two performances, at six and eight p.m. A splendid programine being aspired, you are requested to visit this populair cinema house early.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>OLYMPIA GETS READY.</head>
        <p>OLYMPIA GETS READY.</p>
        <p>Coldstream Guards Band Among The</p>
        <p>Attractions.</p>
        <p>OlyMpia closes to-day in preparation for the annual transformation, which converts its auditorium into a stage and the stage into an auditorium. This is the last step necessary to meet the rush which may be expected when the Grand Circus Season , opens on Boxing Day, December 27th, with the Coldstream Guards as one of the attractions in ha enormous pro- gramme. .</p>
        <p>The first circus season at Olympia in 1913-14 was a marked success, but the Christmas fixture has increased in popularity with leaps and bounds. . Last year, after almost insuperable difficulties in replacing by native talent the almost invariably alien turns' of which circuses were made up, Olympia was rewarded with still higher records in business done. This year Mr. Ernest Wighton, well-known to Liverpool theatregoers, who makes the circus his particular care, reports that he has 'achieved his yearly -ambition of going one better in the way of acts. engaged The performers for the opening week comprise some big favourites of former years and many novelties.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>GERMAN MEASLES.</head>
        <p>GERMAN MEASLES.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams Wishes He Could Alter The Name.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council on Tuesday at Dutton Workhouse, Mr.T. Antwis presiding, one of the chief items for discussion was the recent epidemic of measles and German measles in the district. Attending was likr . Adams who read his report on tho matter.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Woodwatit stated that in his opinion the prevailing disdge di infinitely more harm than scarlet fever, as it was more catching and liable to spread. Whilst assured that every- thing was being done to combat the existing menace, he -moved that the medical officer of health should also have the assistance of the T.solai-ion Committee. which was seconded. He hoped the disease would be immediately stamped out, and that they would no more hear of the one entitled "German measles?!</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams humorously replied that he wished he could alter the name.</p>
      </div>
