      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <p>N.</p>
        <p>FEARNHEAD.</p>
        <p>Local Relief Fund.--:A service of song entitled 'The Torn Bible" was rendered by the married people Connected with the Independent Metho- dist Church, Fearnhead, oil Saturday. The ser- vice was illustrated by lantern slides, the reader being the Rev. A. J. Morton, of Padgate, and Mr. L. Barton, of Croft,, occupied the chair. Solos were sung by Mrs. Holmes and Mrs. W. J. Bate; duets by Mr. W.ilngthes and -Mrs. IL Hughes, and Mrs. J. Barton and Mrs. frainp. son; quartets by Mrs. W. Hughes, Mrs. V. Hughes, Mr. R.. Hughes and Mr. L. Miller. Mr. W. J. Bate officiated at the organ. The pro- ceeds,. which were in aid of the soldiers" local benefit fund, were about ,f.q.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>DUTTON.</head>
        <p>DUTTON.</p>
        <p>An Unburied Carcase.—At the Daresbury Petty Sessions, held at Stockton Heath on Tuesday, John Richardson, of Dutton Park, was summoned for having without reasonable ex- cuse, permitted the carcase of a calf to remain(</p>
        <p>unburied in a field. The defendant pleaded that there was no offensive smell, and that the carcase was a long way from a road.' 2dr. F. A. Frost, who was in the chair, told him that the animal should have been buried. Defend- ant was fined Ms.,</p>
      </div>
