      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-18_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>TOYS FON POOR CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>TOYS FON POOR CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I appeal to the residents in Pretwat Union (St. 'Helens, Widnes, Prescot and neigh- bourhood) for toys—new or broken—for the use of our little charges in the Whiston Institu- tion?</p>
        <p>Although the healthy children over threo years old are now transferred to Cottage Homes. there still remain upwards of a hundred children in our nursery, infirmary wards and mental hospitals, and it is for these I Plead. Christmas is essentially the Children's Fes- tival, and those of us whose work lies amongst them, and, I am sure, scores besides, wish to give them as many as possible of the little pleasures which mean so much to children at Christmastide.</p>
        <p>A post-card sent to me will insure the collec- tion of toys, picture books, or any other gifts donors think will piew.e the bairns. Ur. ICING, Master. IVhiston Institution, Prescot. December 14th, 1915.</p>
