        <p>TERRITORIAL ROLL OF *HONOUR. WOUNDED. PRIVATE W. T. PERCIVAL. • --- Wounded In The Arm, But Progressing Favourably. , Mr. and Mrs. Francis Percival, of 94, Long- ford-street, have received news to the effect that their son, Private William Thomas Percival, of the Warrington Territorials, has been wounded in the left arm. He has since been brought to England, and placed in hospital .at Norwich, from which place he has written home to say •</p>
        <p>that the wound is only a slight one, and that he is progressing favourably. Private Percival, who is 18 years of age, for- merly attended the St. Alban' School, and also worshipped at St. Alban's Church, Bewsey- street. He was employed by Whyman's Foundry, Ltd., Dallam-lane, as a moulder. He was called up at the outbreak of war, having previously served in the Territorials for two years.</p>
        <p>KILLED. LIEUTENANT BUNTING. Secondary School "Old Boy" Falls ° Gallantly In Gallipoli. News has been received in Warrington that an "old boy" of the Secondary School, Lieut. Russel R. Bunting, of the Essex Regiment, was killed in action in Gallipoli, leading his men to attack the Turkish trenches on the evening of August 6th. When he attended school his family lived at 47, Walton-road, Stockton</p>
