        <p>LIEUTENANT R. R. BUNTING. Killed in Action.</p>
        <p>Heath, but they have since reproved to 'Ching- ford. Lieutenant Bunting was one of the most suc- cessful pupils the school has had. In 1912 he obtained his Intermediate B.Sc., London University, and in 1913 he graduated B.Sc. with Honours in Physics. He is the third "old boy" of the school to lay down his life for his country—the other two being Victor H. Fletcher, of the 4th &amp;pith Lancs., and Harold Fryer, of the King's Own Scottish Borderers. PRIVATE J. DICKINSON. Dies At Sea From Shock And Frost- Bite. Private John -Dickinson, of the Lancashire [ Fusiliers, whose wife and child reside at 14, Gilbert-street, has died from shock and frost- bite, on board the hospital ship Dunluce Castle Writing to, his wife on IsTovember 4th; whilst serving with the Mediterranean Expeditionary Force, Private Dickinson said that he was in the best of health, and also added that he was forwarding a postal order with which a Christ- mas present was to be bought for his son. Another—and as it proved to be the last letter —bearing the date November 15th, was received by Mrs. Dickinson on Wednesday. It ran as follows: " Just a few lines, hoping to find you and son in the best of health, as it leaves me. I have received two parcels but no letter. Will you let me know if you received the postal order</p>
