        <p>PRIVATE JOHN DICKINSON. Died at Sea.</p>
        <p>which I sent for Jack's 'Christmas present? 1 hope that before long I shall be with you all at lotne once ,more. Oh! what a day that wil.: be. Wishing you all a merry Christmas, and a prosperous and happy New Year." Employed by Rylands Bros. Private Dickinson was 31 years of age, and a son of Mr. James Dickinson, of Brook-street. He was educated at the National School, Church-street, and until the outbreak of 'war was employed by Messrs. 'Rylands Bros., Ltd., illthe netting department: Being a Reservist, he was called up immediately upon the Out- break-daf war, and very shortly afterwards was drafted to the Front. Tie took part in all the memorable engagements from Mons to the river Aisne, being wounded in the right thigh whilst Serving at the latter place. A Narrow Escape. He had a very remarkable cape from being killed later on, for a bullet passed through the upper part of his cap, but, fortunately did not strike him. He was invalided home in October of last year, during which time his. eldest child, aged four years, died. . Private Dickinson's brother, William, of the Lancashire Fusiliers, has just returned to France, after* a short leave at home, while a brother-in-law, William McElroy. of Greenall- street, is serving in the trenches with the South Lancashire Regiment.</p>
